__ATLAS_SET("en",{"ui":{"brand":"Latent Atlas","lead":"An illustrated atlas of machine learning. Each concept, from linear regression to Mamba, is drawn as a three-dimensional figure you can turn in your hand, with a plain explanation, real-world uses and working code.","statConcepts":"concepts","statChapters":"chapters","statLanguages":"languages","start":"Start with chapter 1","featured":"Featured figure","chapters":"Chapters","chapter":"Chapter {n}","conceptOf":"{i} of {n}","index":"Index","search":"Search {n} concepts","searchShort":"Search","noResults":"No concept matches “{q}”. Try an English term or a shorter word.","dragHint":"Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom","resetView":"Reset view","keyIdea":"Colour marks the key idea","formula":"Key formula","useCases":"Typical use cases","industry":"In industry","code":"Code example","copy":"Copy","copied":"Copied","prev":"Previous","next":"Next","home":"Home","close":"Close","language":"Language","loading":"Loading the text","inChapter":"More in this chapter","keys":"Use ← and → to move between concepts","footer":"Figures are schematic. They are drawn to show how an idea works, not to reproduce exact numbers.","searchHero":"Search algorithms, models and ideas","resultsCount":"{n} concepts found"},"cats":{"found":"Foundations","prep":"Preprocessing & Features","reg":"Regularization","lin":"Linear & Classic Models","tree":"Trees & Ensembles","eval":"Evaluation & Metrics","xai":"Interpretability","unsup":"Unsupervised Learning","ts":"Time Series","opt":"Optimization","nn":"Neural Network Basics","arch":"Deep Architectures","gen":"Generative Models","tf":"Transformers","llm":"LLM Training & Alignment","inf":"Inference & Efficiency","app":"LLM Systems & Reasoning","front":"Frontier Trends"},"items":{"eda":{"n":"Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)","d":"Exploratory data analysis is the systematic first inspection of a dataset to understand its structure, quality and relationships before any model is built. A practitioner checks column types, row counts, missing-value patterns, duplicates, value ranges and the distribution of each feature, then studies relationships between features and with the target using summary statistics, histograms, box plots, scatter plots and correlation matrices.\n\nThe goal is to form hypotheses and catch problems early: skewed features that need a transform, categorical columns with thousands of levels, label errors, impossible values such as negative ages, or a column that is suspiciously predictive because it encodes the answer. EDA also guides the choice of validation scheme, for example by revealing time ordering or grouped records. A common mistake is running EDA on the full dataset, including the test set, and then making preprocessing decisions from it; this quietly leaks test information into the modeling choices.","u":["Auditing data quality before model training","Discovering skew, outliers and missing patterns","Finding leaky or target-encoding columns early"],"x":"A retail analytics team receiving a new point-of-sale export first profiles every column, plots weekly sales per store and checks missing rates by region. The analysis reveals that one region reports refunds as negative sales and that a store ID changed format mid-year, both of which are fixed before a demand forecasting model is trained."},"supervised":{"n":"Supervised Learning","d":"Supervised learning trains a model to map inputs to known outputs using examples that are labeled with the correct answer. Given pairs (x, y), the algorithm adjusts its parameters to minimize a loss that measures how far its predictions are from the labels, and then applies the learned function to new, unlabeled inputs.\n\nProblems split into classification, where y is a category such as spam or not spam, and regression, where y is a continuous value such as a price. Typical algorithms range from linear and logistic regression to gradient-boosted trees and deep neural networks. Supervised learning is the default choice whenever reliable labels exist and the prediction target is clearly defined.\n\nIts main limitation is the cost and quality of labels: models learn label noise and annotator bias as faithfully as true signal. Performance must be measured on held-out data drawn from the same distribution as production traffic, because low training error alone says nothing about generalization.","u":["Email spam classification from labeled messages","House price prediction from property attributes","Medical image diagnosis from annotated scans"],"x":"An insurance company trains a gradient-boosted classifier on years of historical claims labeled as fraudulent or legitimate by investigators. The model scores each new claim within milliseconds, and the team retrains it quarterly because fraud patterns change and newly confirmed labels arrive with a delay."},"unsupervised":{"n":"Unsupervised Learning","d":"Unsupervised learning finds structure in data that has no labels. Instead of predicting a known target, the algorithm models the data itself: it groups similar points, compresses them into fewer dimensions, estimates their density or flags points that do not fit.\n\nThe main families are clustering (k-means, DBSCAN, Gaussian mixtures, hierarchical clustering), dimensionality reduction (PCA, UMAP, autoencoders) and density estimation or anomaly detection (isolation forest, kernel density). These methods are useful when labels are expensive or undefined, for exploring a new dataset, for creating features for a later supervised model, and for detecting unusual behavior.\n\nThe central difficulty is evaluation. Without ground truth there is no single correct answer, so results depend heavily on feature scaling, the distance metric and hyperparameters such as the number of clusters. Internal scores like the silhouette coefficient measure geometric separation, not business usefulness, so clusters must be validated by domain experts or downstream impact.","u":["Customer segmentation from purchase behavior","Anomaly detection in server or sensor metrics","Compressing embeddings for visualization and search"],"x":"A subscription streaming service clusters users by viewing-time patterns, genres and device usage to discover audience segments that marketing had not defined. Analysts review each cluster's profile before naming it, and the segment labels are then used as features in churn models and for targeting campaigns."},"selfsup":{"n":"Self-Supervised Learning","d":"Self-supervised learning creates its own training signal from unlabeled data by hiding or transforming part of an input and training a model to recover it. Because the labels come from the data itself, no human annotation is needed.\n\nCommon pretext tasks include predicting the next token (GPT-style language models), predicting masked tokens (BERT), reconstructing masked image patches (MAE) and contrastive learning, where two augmented views of the same example are pulled together in embedding space while views of different examples are pushed apart (SimCLR, CLIP). The pretrained network is then fine-tuned or used as a frozen feature extractor for downstream tasks with few labels.\n\nThis paradigm underlies modern foundation models. A frequent misconception is that it is the same as unsupervised clustering; in fact it uses an ordinary supervised loss on automatically generated targets. The choice of pretext task and augmentations strongly determines which features are learned, and a poor choice can teach shortcuts that do not transfer.","u":["Pretraining language models on raw web text","Learning image features without manual labels","Building embeddings for retrieval and transfer learning"],"x":"A medical imaging group has hundreds of thousands of unlabeled chest X-rays but only a few thousand with radiologist labels. They pretrain a vision encoder with a masked-autoencoder objective on all images, then fine-tune it on the labeled subset, which gives better accuracy than training from scratch on the small labeled set."},"rl":{"n":"Reinforcement Learning","d":"Reinforcement learning trains an agent to choose actions that maximize cumulative reward through trial-and-error interaction with an environment. At each step the agent observes a state, takes an action, receives a scalar reward and moves to a new state; it must learn a policy without being told which action was correct.\n\nValue-based methods such as Q-learning and DQN estimate the expected return of each action and act greedily on those estimates. Policy-gradient methods such as REINFORCE and PPO adjust the policy directly in the direction that increases expected return. A discount factor γ weights near-term against long-term reward, and the agent must balance exploring unfamiliar actions with exploiting known good ones.\n\nReinforcement learning fits sequential decision problems: games, robotics and aligning language models with human preferences (RLHF). It is sample-inefficient, sensitive to reward design and often unstable. A poorly specified reward is easily exploited: agents learn behavior that scores well but does not achieve the intended goal.","u":["Game-playing agents and simulators","Robot control and locomotion policies","Fine-tuning LLMs with human preference rewards"],"x":"A data center operator trains a reinforcement learning controller in a simulator to adjust cooling setpoints based on temperature and load readings, with reward tied to energy use and safety limits. Before deployment, the learned policy runs in recommendation mode, and hard constraints override any action outside approved operating ranges."},"split":{"n":"Train / Validation / Test Split","d":"A train, validation and test split divides data into three disjoint sets with separate jobs: the model learns parameters on the training set, hyperparameters and design choices are tuned on the validation set, and the test set is used once at the end to estimate performance on unseen data.\n\nEvery decision made by looking at a score adapts the model to that data. After many rounds of tuning on one set, its score becomes optimistically biased, so a final untouched set is needed for an honest estimate. Typical ratios are 70/15/15 or 80/10/10, with smaller holdouts when data is plentiful.\n\nThe split must mirror how the model will be used. For time series, split by time so validation and test come after training. When several rows share an entity such as a patient or user, split by group so the same entity never appears on both sides. A random split in these cases produces misleadingly high scores.","u":["Tuning hyperparameters without touching test data","Reporting an unbiased final performance estimate","Comparing candidate models on a fixed holdout"],"x":"A credit risk team trains on loans issued in earlier years, tunes on the following quarter and reports final metrics on the most recent quarter, mimicking how the model will score future applicants. The test quarter is locked in a separate storage location so that analysts cannot accidentally use it during feature selection."},"cv":{"n":"Cross-Validation","d":"Cross-validation estimates how well a model generalizes by training and evaluating it several times on different partitions of the data. In k-fold cross-validation the data is split into k folds; each fold serves once as the validation set while the model is trained on the other k − 1 folds, and the k scores are averaged.\n\nUnlike a single holdout split, every example is used for both training and validation, giving a less noisy estimate plus a standard deviation that shows model stability. Variants handle special structure: stratified k-fold preserves class proportions, group k-fold keeps all rows of one entity in the same fold, and time-series split always validates on data that comes after the training window.\n\nCross-validation costs k times the training time. When it is used to select hyperparameters, the averaged score of the winning configuration is optimistically biased; nested cross-validation or a separate test set is required for an unbiased final estimate.","u":["Comparing models on small or medium datasets","Hyperparameter search with reliable score estimates","Estimating performance variance across data subsets"],"x":"A pharmaceutical analytics team with only a few hundred patient records uses grouped 5-fold cross-validation so that all visits from one patient stay in the same fold. They report the mean and standard deviation of the AUC across folds, which reveals that one candidate model is far less stable than its average score suggests."},"stratified":{"n":"Stratified Sampling","d":"Stratified sampling splits or samples data so that each subset keeps the same proportion of a chosen variable, usually the class label, as the full dataset. The data is divided into strata by that variable, and the same fraction is drawn from each stratum.\n\nIt is mostly used for train/test splits and cross-validation folds. With imbalanced labels, a plain random split can by chance place very few positive examples in a validation fold, which makes metrics noisy or even undefined. Stratification removes this source of variance, so scores across folds are more comparable. It can also be applied to other key variables, such as region or device type, and in regression by stratifying on binned target values.\n\nStratification only controls the distribution of the chosen variable; it does not prevent leakage between related rows. It also does not fix class imbalance itself. A stratified split of a 1% positive dataset still gives each subset about 1% positives.","u":["Train/test splits for imbalanced classification","Stable cross-validation folds for rare classes","Survey sampling across demographic groups"],"x":"A fraud detection team with a 0.3% positive rate uses stratified 5-fold cross-validation so that every fold contains a comparable number of fraud cases. Without it, some folds had so few positives that the PR-AUC varied wildly between runs, making model comparisons unreliable."},"leak":{"n":"Data Leakage","d":"Data leakage occurs when information that would not be available at prediction time enters model training, producing evaluation scores that are far better than real-world performance.\n\nLeakage takes several forms. Target leakage happens when a feature is derived from the label or recorded after the outcome, such as a refund flag used to predict fraud. Train-test contamination happens when preprocessing such as scaling, imputation, target encoding or feature selection is fitted on the full dataset before splitting. Temporal leakage happens when a random split lets the model train on the future and predict the past, and group leakage happens when the same customer or patient appears in both training and test data.\n\nTypical symptoms are a suspiciously high score or one feature with overwhelming importance. Prevention means fitting all preprocessing inside a pipeline on the training fold only, splitting by time or group when appropriate, and checking for every feature exactly when its value becomes known.","u":["Auditing features for post-outcome information","Building leak-free preprocessing pipelines","Diagnosing models that fail after deployment"],"x":"A hospital readmission model reached near-perfect validation AUC, and an audit showed that one feature was a discharge code assigned only after a patient was readmitted. After removing it and rebuilding features from data available at discharge time, the score dropped to a realistic level and matched later production results."},"overfit":{"n":"Overfitting","d":"Overfitting happens when a model learns patterns specific to its training data, including noise, so that it performs well on training examples but poorly on new data. The tell-tale sign is a large and growing gap between training and validation performance.\n\nIt occurs when model capacity is high relative to the amount and quality of data: deep trees, high-degree polynomials, many features with few rows, or neural networks trained for too long. The model memorizes examples instead of the underlying relationship. Remedies include more data, simpler models, regularization (L1, L2, dropout, weight decay), early stopping, data augmentation and bagging.\n\nOverfitting can also happen at the level of the experiment. Repeatedly tuning hyperparameters or selecting features against the same validation set adapts the whole pipeline to that set. Low training loss is not a problem in itself; very large networks can fit training data perfectly and still generalize well, so the validation curve is the signal to watch.","u":["Diagnosing train/validation gaps during training","Choosing tree depth or polynomial degree","Deciding when to stop neural network training"],"x":"A demand forecasting team noticed that a deep gradient-boosted model scored almost perfectly on training weeks but missed badly on recent weeks. Limiting tree depth, raising the minimum samples per leaf and using early stopping on a time-based validation set closed most of the gap."},"underfit":{"n":"Underfitting","d":"Underfitting is when a model is too simple or too constrained to capture the real structure of the data, so it performs poorly on both training and validation sets. Unlike overfitting, there is little gap between the two errors; both are simply high.\n\nCommon causes are a model family that cannot express the relationship (a straight line for a curved pattern), too few or uninformative features, excessive regularization, stopping training too early, or a learning rate so low that the optimizer barely moves. In bias-variance terms, an underfit model has high bias.\n\nFixes go in the opposite direction from overfitting: use a more flexible model, add or engineer features such as interactions and nonlinear transforms, reduce regularization strength, or train longer. A useful check is whether the model can overfit a tiny subset of the data; if even that fails, the problem is likely a bug, a bad learning rate or a broken data pipeline rather than genuine underfitting.","u":["Diagnosing models that plateau at high training error","Deciding to add features or model capacity","Tuning regularization strength that is too strong"],"x":"A pricing team's linear model for delivery times had similar but disappointing error on training and validation data. Adding distance-by-traffic interaction features and switching to a gradient-boosted model reduced both errors, confirming that the original model lacked capacity rather than data."},"biasvar":{"n":"Bias–Variance Tradeoff","d":"The bias-variance tradeoff describes how a model's expected prediction error splits into systematic error from overly simple assumptions (bias) and sensitivity to the particular training sample (variance). For squared error, expected error equals bias squared plus variance plus irreducible noise.\n\nBias measures how far the average prediction, taken over many possible training sets, is from the true function. Variance measures how much predictions change from one training set to another. Simple models such as linear regression tend to have high bias and low variance; flexible models such as deep decision trees have low bias and high variance. Regularization, ensembling and more data shift this balance: bagging mainly lowers variance, while boosting mainly lowers bias.\n\nThe classical picture says test error is U-shaped in model complexity. Modern overparameterized networks can show double descent, where error falls again beyond the point of interpolating the training data, so the tradeoff is a guide rather than a strict law.","u":["Choosing model complexity and regularization","Explaining why bagging and boosting help","Diagnosing underfitting versus overfitting"],"x":"A team forecasting energy load compared a linear model with a fully grown decision tree and found the first missed peaks while the second changed sharply when retrained on different months. Switching to a random forest kept low bias while averaging away much of the variance, giving more stable forecasts."},"curse":{"n":"Curse of Dimensionality","d":"The curse of dimensionality refers to the set of problems that appear when data has many features: the volume of the space grows exponentially, so data becomes sparse and many intuitions from two or three dimensions stop working.\n\nTo cover a unit hypercube at a fixed density, the number of samples needed grows exponentially with the number of dimensions. Distances also concentrate: in high dimensions the nearest and farthest neighbors of a point end up at nearly the same distance, which weakens distance-based methods such as k-nearest neighbors, k-means and kernel methods with isotropic kernels. Most of the volume of a high-dimensional ball lies near its surface, and random vectors are almost orthogonal.\n\nIt motivates feature selection, dimensionality reduction and models with strong inductive biases. The curse is less severe than worst-case theory suggests when real data lies near a low-dimensional manifold, which is why deep networks and embeddings work well on images and text despite their huge nominal dimension.","u":["Explaining why k-NN degrades with many features","Motivating feature selection and PCA","Choosing distance metrics for embedding search"],"x":"A team building a nearest-neighbor recommender on raw one-hot product features found that similarity scores barely differed between relevant and irrelevant items. Projecting items into a learned 128-dimensional embedding space restored meaningful distances and made approximate nearest-neighbor search practical."},"learncurve":{"n":"Learning Curve","d":"A learning curve plots a model's training and validation performance against the amount of training data (or, in deep learning, against training steps). It shows whether more data, more capacity or more regularization is the most promising next step.\n\nTo build one, the model is trained on increasing subsets of the training set, often with cross-validation at each size, and both scores are recorded. If training and validation errors converge at a high value, the model has high bias, and more data will not help much; a richer model or better features are needed. If a large gap remains between low training error and higher validation error that keeps shrinking as data grows, the model has high variance and collecting more data is likely to pay off.\n\nSmall-sample points are noisy, so averaging across folds matters. The curve must be computed on validation data that matches production; otherwise it only describes performance on a different distribution.","u":["Deciding whether collecting more data is worthwhile","Diagnosing high bias versus high variance","Estimating labeling budget for a new task"],"x":"A document classification team considered paying for 50,000 more labeled examples. Their learning curve showed validation accuracy had already flattened and matched training accuracy, so they invested in a stronger pretrained encoder instead of additional labeling."},"drift":{"n":"Data Drift & Distribution Shift","d":"Distribution shift means the data a model sees in production differs from the data it was trained on, which can silently degrade accuracy. Data drift usually refers to changes in the input distribution P(X); concept drift refers to changes in the relationship P(y | X), where the same inputs now map to different outcomes.\n\nShift has many sources: new user segments, seasonality, product changes, upstream pipeline bugs, sensor recalibration or feedback from the model itself. Monitoring compares production feature distributions with a reference window using statistics such as the population stability index (PSI), Kolmogorov-Smirnov tests or Jensen-Shannon divergence, and tracks prediction distributions and, when labels arrive, actual performance.\n\nResponses include retraining on recent data, time-weighted training and fallback rules. A key pitfall is assuming that detected input drift always hurts accuracy, or that no input drift means the model is fine. Concept drift can occur without any visible change in P(X), so label-based monitoring remains essential.","u":["Monitoring production feature distributions","Triggering scheduled or alert-based retraining","Detecting upstream data pipeline breakages"],"x":"A payments company computes daily PSI for each model feature against the training window and alerts when a value exceeds an agreed threshold. One alert traced a sudden shift in a transaction-amount feature to a currency conversion change upstream, which was fixed before fraud recall dropped noticeably."},"minmax":{"n":"Min-Max Normalization","d":"Min-max normalization rescales each feature linearly to a fixed range, usually [0, 1], by subtracting the feature's minimum and dividing by its range. The shape of the distribution is unchanged; only its location and scale move.\n\nIt is useful when a model or downstream step expects bounded inputs, for example pixel intensities fed to a neural network, features for distance-based methods such as k-nearest neighbors, or inputs to algorithms that assume values in a known interval. The minimum and maximum must be learned from the training data only and then applied unchanged to validation, test and production data.\n\nIts main weakness is sensitivity to outliers: a single extreme value sets the range and squeezes all other values into a narrow band. New data outside the training range maps outside [0, 1], which some systems do not expect. Tree-based models are invariant to monotonic rescaling, so they gain nothing from it.","u":["Scaling image pixel values for neural networks","Equalizing feature ranges for k-NN and k-means","Bounding inputs for sigmoid-based models"],"x":"A computer vision team divides 8-bit pixel values by 255, a fixed min-max scaling, before training a defect detection network on factory images. Because the range is fixed by the sensor format rather than learned from data, the same preprocessing works unchanged on every production camera."},"standard":{"n":"Standardization (Z-score)","d":"Standardization transforms each feature to have zero mean and unit standard deviation by subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard deviation. The result, called a z-score, expresses each value as a number of standard deviations from the average.\n\nMany algorithms are sensitive to feature scale. Gradient descent converges faster when features have similar scales, regularized linear models penalize coefficients fairly only when features are comparable, and PCA, SVMs, k-means and k-nearest neighbors all depend on distances or variances that a large-scale feature would dominate. Standardization is the usual default for these methods.\n\nThe mean and standard deviation must be computed on training data only and reused for all later data. Standardization does not make a distribution normal; a skewed feature stays skewed. It is also influenced by outliers, since both the mean and the standard deviation are, so robust scaling may be better for heavy-tailed data. Tree-based models do not need it.","u":["Preprocessing for linear models and SVMs","Preparing features before PCA","Speeding up gradient descent convergence"],"x":"A marketing analytics team fits a regularized logistic regression on features ranging from age to annual spend in thousands of dollars. Standardizing inside a scikit-learn pipeline ensures that the L2 penalty treats every feature fairly and that the same scaling parameters are stored and applied by the production scoring service."},"robust":{"n":"Robust Scaling","d":"Robust scaling centers each feature on its median and divides by its interquartile range (IQR), the distance between the 25th and 75th percentiles. Because the median and IQR ignore the extreme tails, a few outliers do not distort the scaling of the remaining values.\n\nWith standardization or min-max scaling, one huge value can inflate the standard deviation or the range so much that typical values collapse into a tiny interval. Robust scaling keeps the bulk of the data on a sensible scale while outliers stay large after transformation, which is useful when they carry information. It suits spiky features such as transaction amounts or response times in linear models, SVMs or neural networks.\n\nRobust scaling does not remove or cap outliers; it only stops them from setting the scale. If a model is itself sensitive to extreme inputs, clipping or a log transform may still be needed. As with any scaler, the statistics must be fitted on training data only.","u":["Scaling transaction amounts with rare huge values","Preprocessing sensor data with spikes","Linear models on heavy-tailed features"],"x":"An observability team trains an anomaly detector on service latency metrics, where rare spikes are thousands of times the typical value. Robust scaling keeps normal latencies well spread out so the model can learn their structure, while the spikes remain clearly extreme after scaling."},"logtf":{"n":"Log Transform","d":"A log transform replaces a positive value x with log(x), or log(1 + x) when zeros are present. It compresses large values much more than small ones, turning right-skewed, multiplicative data into a more symmetric, additive form.\n\nIncomes, prices, counts and file sizes often span several orders of magnitude. On the log scale, a change from 10 to 100 counts the same as a change from 100 to 1,000, so ratios become differences. This often linearizes relationships, stabilizes variance and tames very large values. Applied to a regression target, it turns a model trained with squared error into one that focuses on relative rather than absolute error.\n\nLog is undefined for zero and negative values, so log1p or a shift is needed, and the shift is an arbitrary choice. When a model is trained on a log target, exponentiating its predictions estimates the median rather than the mean, which biases totals downward unless corrected.","u":["Modeling prices or incomes with heavy right tails","Stabilizing variance of count features","Predicting log-sales to focus on relative error"],"x":"A real estate platform predicts sale prices with a gradient-boosted model trained on log(price), so that a 10% error on a cheap apartment and on a luxury house are penalized similarly. Predictions are converted back with exp, and the team checks aggregate bias on a validation set because the back-transform tends to underestimate mean prices."},"boxcox":{"n":"Box-Cox Transform","d":"The Box-Cox transform is a family of power transformations, indexed by a parameter λ, that makes strictly positive data closer to normally distributed. The value of λ is chosen by maximum likelihood so that the transformed data best fits a normal distribution.\n\nWhen λ = 1 the data is only shifted, λ = 0.5 is close to a square-root transform, and λ = 0 is defined as the natural logarithm. By searching over λ, Box-Cox picks the strength of compression that best reduces skewness and stabilizes variance, instead of assuming a log transform. It is applied to features and targets in linear models and time-series forecasting.\n\nBox-Cox requires strictly positive inputs; for data with zeros or negative values the Yeo-Johnson transform is the usual alternative. The fitted λ must come from training data only. As with the log transform, inverting predictions made on the transformed scale does not give an unbiased estimate of the original mean.","u":["Normalizing skewed features for linear models","Stabilizing variance in time-series forecasting","Meeting normality assumptions in statistical tests"],"x":"A utility company forecasting daily electricity consumption applies Box-Cox to the series before fitting an ETS model, because the seasonal swing grows with the overall level. The fitted λ is stored with the model, and forecasts are inverse-transformed before being sent to planners."},"impute":{"n":"Missing Value Imputation","d":"Missing value imputation fills in absent entries in a dataset for models that cannot handle them. The replacement can be a simple statistic, a value predicted from other features or a model-specific default.\n\nSimple strategies use the mean or median for numeric columns and the most frequent value or a dedicated \"missing\" category for categorical ones. Advanced methods use similar rows (k-nearest neighbors) or iteratively regress each feature on the others (MICE). Adding a binary indicator that marks which values were missing is often valuable, because the fact that a value is missing can itself be predictive.\n\nThe right method depends on why data is missing: completely at random, at random given other features, or not at random, where missingness depends on the unseen value itself. Imputation statistics must be fitted on training data only. Mean imputation shrinks variance and weakens correlations, and gradient-boosting libraries such as LightGBM and XGBoost handle missing values natively, so imputation is not always needed.","u":["Filling missing lab results in clinical data","Handling optional form fields in applications","Preparing incomplete sensor data for linear models"],"x":"A lending team scoring loan applications finds that many applicants leave the employment-length field empty. They impute the median within a training-only pipeline and add a missing-indicator feature, which turns out to be predictive because applicants who skip the field default at a different rate."},"outlier":{"n":"Outlier Handling","d":"Outlier handling is the process of detecting data points that are far from the bulk of the distribution and deciding whether to keep, correct, cap, transform or remove them. The decision depends on whether it is an error or a genuine rare event.\n\nDetection rules include z-scores (for example |z| > 3), the IQR rule that flags values beyond 1.5 × IQR outside the quartiles, and multivariate methods such as isolation forest, local outlier factor or Mahalanobis distance, which catch points that look normal on each feature separately but unusual in combination. Data-entry errors can then be corrected or removed, while legitimate extremes can be capped, log-transformed or handled with robust losses such as Huber.\n\nOutliers matter most for models based on squared error, means and distances, and less for trees. A frequent mistake is to delete outliers automatically: in fraud detection, fault monitoring or rare-disease diagnosis, the outliers may be exactly the cases the model needs to learn.","u":["Cleaning data-entry errors in tabular datasets","Protecting linear regression from extreme points","Flagging unusual sensor readings for review"],"x":"An e-commerce analytics team found order values of exactly 9,999,999 caused by a test account and a default placeholder in one system. They removed those rows as errors, but kept genuine large business orders and capped their influence with a Huber loss in the revenue forecasting model."},"clipwin":{"n":"Clipping & Winsorizing","d":"Clipping and winsorizing limit extreme values by replacing anything beyond a chosen threshold with the threshold itself. Clipping uses fixed bounds, such as 0 and 1,000; winsorizing sets the bounds at percentiles of the data, such as the 1st and 99th, so the cut adapts to each feature.\n\nUnlike deleting outliers, both methods keep every row and its ordering among non-extreme values while limiting how much a single point can pull a mean, a regression coefficient or a gradient. They are common for heavy-tailed features in linear models and neural networks, for robust summary statistics, and for financial ratios in which a few firms have enormous values.\n\nPercentile thresholds must be computed on training data and reused unchanged. Capping erases information about how extreme a value was, which is harmful if the extremes are the signal of interest, as in fraud. Tree models are largely unaffected by monotonic tails, so the step is often unnecessary for them.","u":["Limiting heavy-tailed features for linear regression","Robust averages of financial ratios","Stabilizing neural network inputs with rare spikes"],"x":"A quantitative research team winsorizes company valuation ratios at the 1st and 99th percentiles within each month before fitting cross-sectional regressions. This prevents a handful of firms with near-zero earnings and enormous ratios from dominating the coefficient estimates."},"onehot":{"n":"One-Hot Encoding","d":"One-hot encoding converts a categorical variable with k categories into k binary columns, exactly one of which is 1 for each row. It lets models that require numeric input use categories without implying any order or distance between them.\n\nAssigning integers such as red = 1, green = 2, blue = 3 would tell a linear model or neural network that blue is three times red and that green lies between them. One-hot vectors avoid this, giving each category its own coefficient. For linear models with an intercept, one column is often dropped to avoid perfect multicollinearity, the so-called dummy variable trap. Encoders should be fitted on training data and handle unseen categories.\n\nThe main limitation is dimensionality: a column with tens of thousands of values, such as a user ID or ZIP code, produces a huge sparse matrix and very few examples per column. Alternatives for high cardinality include target encoding, frequency encoding, hashing or learned embeddings.","u":["Encoding low-cardinality categories for linear models","Representing product type or day of week","Preparing nominal features for neural networks"],"x":"An airline's pricing team encodes cabin class, booking channel and departure weekday as one-hot features in a logistic regression that predicts no-shows. The encoder runs inside the deployed pipeline with unknown categories ignored, so a newly launched booking channel does not break scoring."},"targetenc":{"n":"Target Encoding","d":"Target encoding replaces each category with a statistic of the target computed over rows of that category, typically the mean of the label, so a high-cardinality feature becomes a single informative numeric column.\n\nFor a binary target, a city becomes the fraction of positive outcomes in that city. Rare categories give noisy means, so the estimate is smoothed toward the global mean with a weight that grows with the category count. This keeps dimensionality low even for features such as merchant ID or ZIP code, and suits both linear and tree models.\n\nThe major risk is target leakage: if a row's own label contributes to its encoding, the model sees a distorted version of the answer and overfits. The safe approach is out-of-fold encoding, where each training row is encoded using statistics from other folds only, then fitting the encoder on all training data to transform test data. scikit-learn's TargetEncoder applies this cross-fitting automatically in fit_transform.","u":["Encoding merchant or ZIP code for fraud models","Compressing high-cardinality IDs for linear models","Click-through prediction with many advertiser categories"],"x":"An online marketplace predicts whether a listing will sell within a week using seller ID, which has hundreds of thousands of values. Out-of-fold smoothed target encoding turns the ID into one column reflecting each seller's historical sell-through, while heavy smoothing keeps new sellers close to the global average."},"freqenc":{"n":"Frequency Encoding","d":"Frequency encoding replaces each category with how often it appears in the training data, either as a raw count or as a proportion. It turns a categorical column of any cardinality into one numeric feature without using the target.\n\nThe intuition is that popularity is often informative on its own: frequent merchants, common device models or popular products may behave differently from rare ones. Because it ignores labels, it carries no target-leakage risk, is cheap and handles very high cardinality. It pairs well with tree models, which can split on count thresholds, and is often combined with other encodings.\n\nIts main limitation is that categories with the same frequency become indistinguishable, even if they behave very differently. The counts must come from training data and be stored; unseen categories in production typically receive a count of zero or a small default. If the data volume changes over time, raw counts drift, so normalized frequencies are usually safer.","u":["Encoding device model or browser in click logs","Capturing popularity of merchants or products","Cheap leakage-free encoding for gradient boosting"],"x":"An ad-tech team adds frequency-encoded publisher domain and app ID to a LightGBM click-through model. The counts are computed from a fixed training window and stored as a lookup table in the feature service, with unseen domains mapped to zero so that new publishers are treated as rare."},"binning":{"n":"Binning (Discretization)","d":"Binning converts a continuous feature into a categorical one by dividing its range into intervals and assigning each value to the interval it falls in. Common schemes are equal-width bins, equal-frequency (quantile) bins and bins chosen by domain knowledge or by a decision tree.\n\nBinning lets a linear model capture nonlinear effects, because each bin gets its own coefficient, and it limits the influence of outliers, which simply fall into the end bins. Binned features are easy to explain, which is why credit scorecards assign points per bin. Quantile binning is robust to skew, while equal-width binning can leave some bins almost empty.\n\nThe cost is lost information: values near a boundary are treated as different, while values at opposite ends of a bin are treated as identical. The number and placement of bins are additional hyperparameters that can overfit. Flexible models such as gradient-boosted trees learn their own split points, so binning rarely helps them.","u":["Building points-based credit scorecards","Letting linear models capture nonlinear effects","Grouping ages or incomes into report categories"],"x":"A bank's credit risk team bins applicant age, income and utilization into quantile-based groups and fits a logistic regression scorecard. Regulators and loan officers can read the points assigned to each bin directly, which a black-box model would not allow."},"featureeng":{"n":"Feature Engineering","d":"Feature engineering is the creation of new input variables from raw data so that a model can learn the relevant pattern more easily. It encodes domain knowledge that the model would otherwise need much more data, or a much more flexible form, to discover.\n\nTypical techniques include ratios (debt divided by income), interactions, date parts such as hour of day, aggregates over a customer's history (orders in the last 30 days), distances between locations and log transforms. For tabular data, well-designed features often matter more than the choice between strong algorithms, and they also make models easier to explain.\n\nEvery feature must be computable at prediction time using only information available then; aggregates that peek into the future are a classic source of leakage. The same code should produce features for training and serving, often through a feature store, to avoid training-serving skew. Deep learning reduces manual feature engineering for images and text but not for most tabular problems.","u":["Customer-history aggregates for churn prediction","Ratio and interaction features for credit scoring","Time and location features for delivery estimates"],"x":"A food delivery company improved its delivery-time model by adding features such as restaurant preparation time over the last hour, courier distance to the restaurant and current order backlog. These features are computed in a shared feature store, so the training pipeline and the real-time prediction service use exactly the same definitions."},"lagfeat":{"n":"Lag Features","d":"Lag features are past values of a time series used as inputs for predicting its current or future value. A lag-k feature at time t is simply the observed value at time t − k, so the model can learn autocorrelation and seasonality from history.\n\nThey turn forecasting into ordinary supervised learning. Useful lags reflect the data's rhythm: lag 1 for momentum, lag 7 for weekly cycles in daily data, lag 24 or 168 for hourly data, and lag 52 for weekly data with yearly seasonality. Lags of related series, such as price, are also common.\n\nThe forecast horizon constrains which lags are legal. If predictions are made seven days ahead, any lag shorter than seven days is not available at prediction time, and using it leaks future information and inflates validation scores. Lagging also creates missing values at the start of each series, and splits must respect time order.","u":["Daily sales forecasting with gradient-boosted trees","Hourly electricity load prediction","Predicting next-day demand per store and item"],"x":"A grocery chain forecasts item demand per store 14 days ahead with LightGBM, using lags of 14, 21, 28 and 364 days computed per store-item pair. Shorter lags were deliberately excluded because they would not exist at the time the replenishment order is placed."},"rollfeat":{"n":"Rolling Window Features","d":"Rolling window features summarize a time series over a sliding window of recent observations, for example the mean, standard deviation, minimum, maximum or sum of the last seven days. They capture the local level, trend and volatility of a series more smoothly than single lag values.\n\nA rolling mean reduces noise and shows the recent level; a rolling standard deviation measures recent volatility; rolling counts or sums capture activity, such as transactions in the past hour. Exponentially weighted means give more weight to recent points without a hard cut-off. Several window lengths, such as 7, 28 and 90 days, are usually combined per entity.\n\nThe window must end at or before the last information available at prediction time. A rolling mean that includes the current target value, or any value inside the forecast horizon, leaks the answer. The standard safe pattern is to shift the series by the horizon first and then compute the rolling statistic.","u":["Transaction velocity features for fraud detection","Recent average demand for sales forecasting","Volatility features for financial risk models"],"x":"A card issuer computes, for each card, the number and total amount of transactions in the last 1 hour, 24 hours and 7 days, and compares the current amount with the 30-day rolling average. These velocity features are maintained in a streaming system so that they are fresh within seconds for real-time fraud scoring."},"imbalance":{"n":"Class Imbalance","d":"Class imbalance occurs when one class is much rarer than the others, such as fraud, defects or disease, so a model can reach high accuracy by always predicting the majority class while being useless for the cases that matter.\n\nDuring training, the loss is dominated by majority examples, and the model may rarely predict the minority class. During evaluation, accuracy becomes misleading; precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC and cost-based metrics are more informative. PR-AUC is especially useful because its random baseline equals the positive rate, which makes small gains visible. Common remedies are class weights, resampling (oversampling, SMOTE, undersampling), cost-based threshold choice and collecting more minority examples.\n\nImbalance alone is not always the problem. A well-specified probabilistic model trained on imbalanced data can still produce good rankings and calibrated probabilities; often only the threshold needs to change. Resampling changes the predicted probabilities, so they must be recalibrated if the outputs are used as real probabilities.","u":["Fraud detection with rare positive transactions","Manufacturing defect detection from sensor data","Rare disease screening from patient records"],"x":"A manufacturing quality team predicts defective parts, which make up a fraction of a percent of output. They train with class weights, evaluate with PR-AUC and recall at a fixed inspection budget, and choose a threshold so that the number of flagged parts matches what inspectors can review each shift."},"smote":{"n":"SMOTE Oversampling","d":"SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) balances a dataset by creating new synthetic minority-class examples instead of duplicating existing ones. Each synthetic point is placed at a random position on the line segment between a minority example and one of its k nearest minority neighbors.\n\nCompared with random oversampling, which copies rows and encourages a model to memorize them, SMOTE fills in the region around minority examples and gives classifiers a broader decision region for the rare class. Variants include Borderline-SMOTE, ADASYN and SMOTE-NC for mixed numeric and categorical features.\n\nSMOTE must be applied only to the training fold, inside the cross-validation loop; resampling before splitting leaks synthetic copies of validation points into training. Interpolation assumes that the space between minority points is also minority territory, which can fail with overlapping classes, noisy labels or categorical features. In practice, class weights or threshold tuning with gradient boosting often perform as well, and SMOTE distorts predicted probabilities.","u":["Balancing rare fraud cases for classical classifiers","Augmenting minority classes in small tabular datasets","Rare failure prediction from equipment sensors"],"x":"A telecom team predicting rare network equipment failures applies SMOTE inside an imbalanced-learn pipeline so that each cross-validation fold is resampled independently. They compare it against class weighting and keep whichever yields better PR-AUC on an untouched, time-based test period."},"undersample":{"n":"Undersampling","d":"Undersampling balances an imbalanced dataset by removing examples from the majority class rather than adding minority examples. The simplest form keeps all minority rows and a random subset of majority rows.\n\nIt cuts training time and memory, which matters for click logs with hundreds of millions of rows, and makes the minority class weigh more in the loss. Informed variants such as Tomek links and edited nearest neighbors remove majority points near the boundary or likely mislabeled. Ensembles such as balanced random forests or EasyEnsemble train many models on different undersampled subsets so that little information is lost overall.\n\nThe obvious cost is discarded data, which can increase variance and hurt the model's view of the majority class. Undersampling also shifts predicted probabilities upward; if the model's scores are used as probabilities, they must be corrected for the sampling rate. It should be applied only to training data, never to validation or test sets.","u":["Reducing massive click logs for CTR models","Speeding up training on highly imbalanced data","Balanced bagging ensembles for rare events"],"x":"An advertising platform keeps every click but only a small random fraction of non-click impressions when training its click-through model, which cuts training cost substantially. Because served bids depend on calibrated probabilities, predictions are corrected for the negative sampling rate before use."},"classweight":{"n":"Class Weights","d":"Class weights scale each example's contribution to the training loss according to its class, so that errors on rare classes cost more than errors on common ones.\n\nA common choice, called balanced weighting, sets each class weight inversely proportional to its frequency, so every class contributes equally to the total loss. Weights can also reflect business costs, for example making a missed fraud case ten times as costly as a false alarm. Most libraries support this directly: class_weight in scikit-learn, scale_pos_weight in XGBoost and LightGBM, and weight arguments in PyTorch losses.\n\nCompared with resampling, class weights use all the data and add no synthetic points, which makes them a clean first option. They shift the decision boundary and inflate predicted probabilities for the minority class, so outputs are no longer calibrated and may need recalibration. Extreme weights can make training unstable, especially with stochastic gradients and very few minority examples per batch.","u":["Imbalanced classification with logistic regression or SVMs","Cost-sensitive fraud and churn models","Weighted cross-entropy for rare classes in deep learning"],"x":"A customer-support team classifies tickets into many categories, a few of which, such as security incidents, are rare but urgent. They train a text classifier with weighted cross-entropy that raises the cost of missing those categories, and monitor per-class recall rather than overall accuracy."},"featsel":{"n":"Feature Selection","d":"Feature selection chooses a subset of the available input features, aiming for a simpler, faster and often more robust model with similar or better accuracy.\n\nMethods fall into three groups. Filter methods score each feature independently of the model, using correlation, mutual information or statistical tests, and are fast but blind to interactions. Wrapper methods such as recursive feature elimination or forward selection repeatedly train a model on candidate subsets, which captures interactions but is expensive. Embedded methods select during training, as L1 regularization drives some coefficients to exactly zero and tree models provide importance scores.\n\nSelection reduces overfitting from noisy features, lowers data-collection costs and improves interpretability. Selection must happen inside the cross-validation loop; choosing features on the full dataset and then cross-validating gives optimistic scores. Importance-based selection is unstable when features are correlated, because the model can use either of two redundant features, and impurity-based importances in trees are biased toward high-cardinality features.","u":["Removing noisy features from wide tabular data","Reducing feature-collection cost in production","Simplifying models for interpretability or regulation"],"x":"A credit team started with several hundred candidate features from bureau and application data. Using L1-regularized logistic regression and permutation importance inside cross-validation, they kept a few dozen features, which reduced dependency on costly data vendors without lowering validation AUC."},"vif":{"n":"Multicollinearity & VIF","d":"Multicollinearity occurs when two or more features are strongly linearly related, which makes the individual coefficients of a linear model unstable and hard to interpret. The variance inflation factor (VIF) measures how much the variance of a coefficient is inflated by this redundancy.\n\nTo compute the VIF for feature j, regress it on all the other features and take R²ⱼ from that regression; VIF = 1 / (1 − R²ⱼ). A VIF of 1 means no linear relation; values above roughly 5 to 10 are commonly treated as problematic. With high VIF, small data changes can flip coefficient signs, and standard errors and p-values become unreliable.\n\nRemedies include dropping or merging redundant features, using domain-motivated ratios, applying PCA, or using ridge regression, which stabilizes correlated coefficients. Multicollinearity mainly harms interpretation and inference; it usually does not hurt predictive accuracy as long as the correlation structure stays the same in production. Compute VIF with an intercept included.","u":["Checking regressors before interpreting coefficients","Cleaning features for econometric or pricing models","Diagnosing unstable coefficient signs"],"x":"A pricing analytics team building an explanatory regression for insurance premiums found that vehicle weight, engine size and horsepower had VIFs well above 10, and their coefficients changed sign between quarters. They kept horsepower and a weight-to-power ratio, which made the model's effects stable enough to present to actuaries."},"dimred":{"n":"Dimensionality Reduction","d":"Dimensionality reduction maps data with many features to a smaller number of dimensions while preserving the structure that matters, such as variance, neighborhoods or class separation.\n\nLinear methods include principal component analysis (PCA), which finds orthogonal directions of maximum variance, and truncated SVD, which works on sparse matrices such as text. Nonlinear methods such as t-SNE and UMAP preserve local neighborhoods and are mainly used for visualization, while autoencoders learn compressed representations with neural networks. Supervised variants like linear discriminant analysis use labels to keep directions that separate classes.\n\nIt helps fight the curse of dimensionality, removes redundancy among correlated features, speeds up training and storage, and makes data visible in two or three dimensions. The reduced features are harder to interpret, and unsupervised reduction may discard low-variance directions that are highly predictive. t-SNE and UMAP plots distort global distances and cluster sizes, so they should not be read as faithful maps of the data.","u":["Visualizing embeddings or cell types in 2D","Compressing correlated sensor features","Speeding up nearest-neighbor search"],"x":"A single-cell genomics group reduces expression profiles of thousands of genes to 50 principal components, then runs UMAP on those components for visualization and clustering. The PCA step removes noise and makes the neighbor graph computation fast enough for datasets with millions of cells."},"l1":{"n":"L1 Regularization (Lasso)","d":"L1 regularization adds the sum of the absolute values of a model's weights to the loss, which pushes many weights to exactly zero and therefore performs automatic feature selection. In linear regression this is called the lasso.\n\nThe absolute-value penalty has a corner at zero: its subgradient there is the whole interval [−λ, λ], so a weight stays exactly at zero unless its feature reduces the loss by more than λ. Geometrically, the diamond-shaped constraint region touches the loss contours at its corners, where some coordinates are zero. Lasso is solved with coordinate descent or proximal methods, since the penalty is not differentiable at zero.\n\nL1 is useful when many features are expected to be irrelevant, when a compact model is needed. With highly correlated features, lasso tends to pick one somewhat arbitrarily and drop the others, and the selection can change between data samples. Features must be standardized first, since the penalty depends on coefficient scale.","u":["Sparse linear models with many candidate features","Selecting genes or biomarkers from wide data","Compact, interpretable scoring models"],"x":"A marketing analytics team fits a lasso model on hundreds of campaign, channel and seasonality features to explain weekly sign-ups. The cross-validated penalty leaves a small set of nonzero coefficients that analysts can review, and the team checks selection stability across bootstrap samples before trusting individual features."},"l2":{"n":"L2 Regularization (Ridge)","d":"L2 regularization adds the sum of squared weights to the loss, which shrinks all weights smoothly toward zero and reduces variance. In linear regression it is called ridge regression.\n\nThe squared penalty's gradient is proportional to the weight itself, so large weights are pulled in strongly and small weights only slightly; weights rarely become exactly zero. Ridge has a closed-form solution, w = (XᵀX + λI)⁻¹Xᵀy, and adding λI makes the matrix invertible even when features are collinear or outnumber samples. With correlated features, L2 spreads weight across them, shrinking correlated coefficients toward equal values instead of choosing one. From a Bayesian view, it corresponds to a Gaussian prior on the weights.\n\nL2 is the default regularizer for linear models and neural networks when most features are expected to contribute a little. It does not produce sparse models, so it is not a feature selector. Features should be standardized first, and the intercept is normally not penalized.","u":["Stabilizing regression with correlated predictors","Regularizing logistic regression on many features","Preventing large weights in neural networks"],"x":"An energy trading desk predicts next-day prices from dozens of strongly correlated weather and load forecasts. Ridge regression keeps all inputs but stabilizes their coefficients, so day-to-day retraining does not produce large swings in the model's behavior."},"elasticnet":{"n":"Elastic Net","d":"Elastic net regularization combines the L1 (lasso) and L2 (ridge) penalties, giving sparse models like lasso while handling groups of correlated features more gracefully.\n\nTwo hyperparameters control it: an overall strength α and a mixing ratio (l1_ratio in scikit-learn) between L1 and L2. With l1_ratio = 1 it is the lasso, and with l1_ratio = 0 it is ridge. The L1 part sets irrelevant weights to exactly zero; the L2 part makes the problem strictly convex and encourages correlated features to enter or leave the model together, instead of lasso's tendency to pick one arbitrarily. It also behaves well when features outnumber samples, where lasso selects at most n features.\n\nIt is a strong default for wide, correlated data such as genomics, text n-grams or marketing features. Both hyperparameters should be tuned by cross-validation, and features must be standardized. The combined penalty adds bias to the nonzero coefficients, so refitting on the selected features is sometimes used for interpretation.","u":["Gene expression models with correlated genes","Text regression on many correlated n-grams","Wide marketing-mix or survey data"],"x":"A biotech team predicts drug response from thousands of gene expression measurements on a few hundred patients. Elastic net keeps groups of co-expressed genes together rather than picking one per group, which makes the selected signatures more consistent across resamples and easier to discuss with biologists."},"weightdecay":{"n":"Weight Decay","d":"Weight decay shrinks a neural network's weights by a small fraction at every optimization step, which keeps weights small and acts as a regularizer. The update multiplies each weight by (1 − ηλ) before or alongside the gradient step.\n\nFor plain stochastic gradient descent, weight decay is mathematically equivalent to adding an L2 penalty (λ/2)‖w‖² to the loss. For adaptive optimizers such as Adam, the two are not equivalent: an L2 term added to the loss is divided by Adam's per-parameter scaling, so weights with large gradient history are barely regularized. AdamW fixes this by applying decay directly to the weights, separately from the adaptive gradient step, and is the standard optimizer for training transformers.\n\nTypical values are around 0.01 to 0.1 with AdamW. Biases and normalization parameters (LayerNorm and BatchNorm scales and shifts) are usually excluded from decay. Because decay is multiplied by the learning rate in common implementations, changing the schedule also changes the effective regularization.","u":["Regularizing transformer pretraining with AdamW","Improving generalization of image classifiers","Fine-tuning pretrained models without drifting far"],"x":"A team fine-tuning a language model for customer-support classification uses AdamW with weight decay 0.01 and excludes biases and LayerNorm weights from decay, following common transformer practice. They tune decay together with the learning rate, since both control how far the weights move from the pretrained values."},"dropout":{"n":"Dropout","d":"Dropout is a regularization technique that randomly sets a fraction of a layer's activations to zero during each training step, which prevents neurons from relying too heavily on specific other neurons.\n\nWith drop probability p, each unit is kept with probability 1 − p. Modern frameworks use inverted dropout: kept activations are scaled by 1/(1 − p) during training, so the expected activation is unchanged and no rescaling is needed at inference, when dropout is turned off. Dropout can be viewed as training an ensemble of weight-sharing subnetworks whose average the full network approximates at test time.\n\nTypical rates are 0.1 in transformers and 0.2 to 0.5 in fully connected layers. Forgetting to switch the model to evaluation mode, so dropout stays active at inference, is a common bug that makes predictions noisy. Dropout also slows convergence. Many large language models trained on huge datasets use little or no dropout because overfitting is not their main problem.","u":["Regularizing fully connected layers in small-data tasks","Attention and residual dropout in transformers","Monte Carlo dropout for uncertainty estimates"],"x":"A team training a tabular neural network for insurance risk on a modest dataset adds dropout of 0.3 after each hidden layer, which narrows the gap between training and validation loss. For uncertainty estimates, they also run Monte Carlo dropout at inference by keeping dropout active and averaging multiple forward passes."},"earlystop":{"n":"Early Stopping","d":"Early stopping halts training when performance on a validation set stops improving and keeps the model from the best validation step. It prevents an iterative learner from continuing to fit noise after it has learned the generalizable signal.\n\nValidation loss is checked periodically. A patience parameter sets how many evaluations without improvement are allowed before stopping, and the weights from the best evaluation are restored. Early stopping is used in neural network training and is built into gradient-boosting libraries, where it chooses the number of trees. It acts as implicit regularization, similar to an L2 penalty for linear models trained with gradient descent.\n\nThe validation set used for stopping becomes part of model selection, so its score is optimistic, and a separate test set is still needed. Noisy validation curves can trigger a stop too early, so patience and a minimum improvement threshold should be set sensibly. When retraining on all data, the best iteration count is often reused.","u":["Choosing the number of trees in gradient boosting","Stopping neural network training at the best epoch","Saving compute in hyperparameter searches"],"x":"A ranking team trains LightGBM models with up to several thousand trees and early stopping on a time-based validation week. The best iteration is logged with each run, and the production model is retrained on all data with that iteration count scaled slightly for the larger training set."},"augment":{"n":"Data Augmentation","d":"Data augmentation creates new training examples by applying label-preserving transformations to existing ones, which increases the effective size and diversity of the dataset and teaches the model useful invariances.\n\nFor images, standard transforms are random crops, horizontal flips, rotations, color jitter and scaling, along with mixing methods such as Mixup and CutMix that blend two images and their labels. For audio, augmentations include time shifts, added noise and SpecAugment masking. For text, options include back-translation and paraphrasing. Augmentations are applied randomly on the fly, so each epoch sees new variants.\n\nAugmentation is one of the most effective regularizers in computer vision and is central to contrastive self-supervised learning. Transformations must truly preserve the label: flipping a digit 6 vertically may turn it into a 9, and horizontal flips are wrong for text in images or left-right medical findings. Augmentation is applied only to training data, not to validation or test sets, except for deliberate test-time augmentation.","u":["Training image classifiers on limited labeled data","Making speech models robust to background noise","Contrastive self-supervised pretraining of image encoders"],"x":"A retail company builds a shelf-product recognition model from photos taken in stores with varied lighting and angles. Random crops, rotations, brightness changes and blur during training make the model robust to new stores, while horizontal flips are disabled because they mirror the text on product packaging."},"labelsmooth":{"n":"Label Smoothing","d":"Label smoothing replaces hard one-hot training targets with soft targets that put most of the probability on the correct class and spread a small amount, ε, across all classes.\n\nWith hard targets, cross-entropy keeps rewarding the model for pushing the correct logit ever higher relative to the others, which can lead to overconfident predictions and large logits. With smoothing, the target for the correct class becomes 1 − ε + ε/K and every other class gets ε/K, so the optimal logits stay finite. Typical values are ε = 0.1. It is widely used in image classification and machine translation and often improves accuracy and calibration.\n\nLabel smoothing also has costs. It erases information about which wrong classes are similar to the right one, which makes a smoothed teacher a weaker source for knowledge distillation. It can hurt calibration when combined with other calibration methods, and for language models it raises perplexity even when translation quality improves.","u":["Regularizing large image classifiers","Training neural machine translation models","Reducing overconfidence in noisy-label datasets"],"x":"A team training an image classifier on product photos with some mislabeled examples uses cross-entropy with label smoothing of 0.1. The model becomes less overconfident on ambiguous images, which reduces the number of high-confidence errors that bypass human review."},"batchnorm":{"n":"Batch Normalization","d":"Batch normalization normalizes each feature or channel using the mean and variance computed over the current mini-batch, then applies a learned scale γ and shift β.\n\nDuring training, every activation is standardized with batch statistics, and running averages of the mean and variance are updated. At inference, those stored running statistics are used instead, so the output for an example does not depend on other examples. In convolutional networks, statistics are shared over the batch and spatial positions of each channel. Batch normalization allows higher learning rates, reduces sensitivity to initialization and adds a mild regularizing noise, mostly because it smooths the optimization landscape.\n\nIt depends on batch size: with very small batches, the statistics are noisy and accuracy suffers, and distributed training may need synchronized batch norm. Forgetting to switch to evaluation mode, or a mismatch between training and serving distributions, gives wrong outputs. These limitations are why transformers and recurrent models use layer normalization instead.","u":["Training deep convolutional image networks","Stabilizing high learning rates in CNNs","Faster convergence in residual networks"],"x":"A medical imaging team trains a ResNet on 3D scans where GPU memory allows only two volumes per device. Plain batch normalization performed poorly with such tiny batches, so they switched to synchronized batch norm across GPUs, later comparing it with group normalization, which does not depend on batch size."},"layernorm":{"n":"Layer Normalization","d":"Layer normalization standardizes the activations of each individual example across its feature dimension, then applies a learned scale and shift. Unlike batch normalization, its statistics do not depend on other examples in the batch.\n\nFor a token vector in a transformer, the mean and variance are computed over its hidden dimension, the vector is normalized, and per-feature parameters γ and β restore representational flexibility. Because the computation is identical at training and inference and works with any batch size or sequence length, layer normalization is the standard choice in transformers and recurrent networks. Placement matters: Post-LN (after the residual addition) was used in the original transformer, while Pre-LN (before each sublayer) trains more stably in deep models without long learning-rate warmup, and is now more common.\n\nIn convolutional networks it often performs worse than batch normalization. Many recent large language models replace it with RMSNorm, which drops the mean-centering step and the bias for slightly lower cost.","u":["Normalization inside transformer blocks","Stabilizing recurrent neural network training","Models with variable or tiny batch sizes"],"x":"A team training a domain-specific transformer for legal text moved from Post-LN to Pre-LN blocks after seeing loss spikes early in training. The change let them train deeper models with a shorter warmup, while LayerNorm parameters were excluded from weight decay."},"rmsnorm":{"n":"RMSNorm","d":"RMSNorm (root mean square normalization) rescales each activation vector by its root mean square and a learned per-feature gain, without subtracting the mean and usually without a bias. It is a simpler and cheaper variant of layer normalization.\n\nThe vector x is divided by √(mean(x²) + ε) and multiplied by a learned weight g. Skipping mean-centering saves one reduction and the bias parameter while matching LayerNorm's stability in transformers. The original paper argued that re-scaling invariance, not re-centering, is what makes layer normalization effective. RMSNorm is used in many modern large language models, including LLaMA, Mistral and Gemma, typically in a Pre-norm arrangement before attention and feed-forward sublayers.\n\nBecause it does not center activations, RMSNorm is not a drop-in replacement for LayerNorm in a pretrained model; weights trained with one will not work with the other. Precision also matters: implementations often compute the norm in float32 even in bfloat16 models.","u":["Normalization layers in LLaMA-style language models","Reducing normalization cost in large transformers","Pre-norm blocks for stable deep training"],"x":"An LLM team training a LLaMA-style model uses RMSNorm before every attention and MLP sublayer and a final RMSNorm before the output head. Their fused kernel computes the norm in float32 within a bfloat16 model, which avoids rare numerical spikes in long training runs."},"gradclip":{"n":"Gradient Clipping","d":"Gradient clipping limits the size of the gradient before each optimizer step so that a single bad batch cannot throw the parameters far away. The most common form, clipping by global norm, computes the L2 norm of all gradients concatenated together; if it exceeds a threshold c, every gradient is multiplied by c / ‖g‖. This keeps the update direction unchanged and only shortens its length. A cruder variant, clipping by value, caps each element independently and therefore can change the direction.\n\nClipping is standard for recurrent networks, where exploding gradients are common, and for transformer pretraining, where a max norm of 1.0 is a typical default. It also makes training with large learning rates or mixed precision more stable. Clipping does not fix vanishing gradients, and a threshold that is too low silently turns the optimizer into fixed-length steps. Logging the pre-clip gradient norm is a cheap way to detect loss spikes and data problems.","u":["Stabilizing LSTM and GRU training on long sequences","Preventing loss spikes in large transformer pretraining","Bounding per-example gradients in differentially private training"],"x":"A team pretraining a multilingual language model clips the global gradient norm to 1.0 at every step. They log the pre-clip norm to their dashboard, because sudden jumps in that value often point to corrupted data shards or a learning rate that is too high. When a spike appears, they roll back to the last checkpoint and skip the offending batches."},"linreg":{"n":"Linear Regression","d":"Linear regression models a numeric target as a weighted sum of input features plus an intercept. Ordinary least squares chooses the weights that minimize the sum of squared vertical differences between predictions and observed values, which has the closed-form solution w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy when XᵀX is invertible. In practice libraries solve it with QR or SVD decompositions for numerical stability, or with gradient descent for very large data.\n\nIt is the default baseline for any regression task and remains valuable when interpretability matters: each coefficient is the expected change in the target per unit change in a feature, holding the other features fixed. Under the Gauss–Markov assumptions, OLS is the best linear unbiased estimator. Its weaknesses are sensitivity to outliers, because squared errors amplify large residuals, and unstable coefficients when features are highly correlated. A common misconception is that \"linear\" refers to the features; it refers to the parameters, so log or polynomial features still give a linear model.","u":["Baseline model for any numeric prediction task","Estimating price elasticity from sales data","Interpretable effect sizes in econometric analysis"],"x":"A real estate platform fits a linear regression of listing price on floor area, location dummies and building age as a transparent baseline. Agents can read the coefficients directly, for example the price added per square meter. The team monitors residuals by neighborhood to catch areas where the linear assumption breaks down."},"tls":{"n":"Total Least Squares","d":"Total least squares fits a line or hyperplane by minimizing the perpendicular distances from the points to it, rather than only the vertical distances used by ordinary least squares. This treats errors in inputs and target symmetrically, which suits data where every measured variable is noisy.\n\nThe solution comes from the singular value decomposition. After centering the data, the fitted hyperplane is orthogonal to the right singular vector with the smallest singular value; for a line in two dimensions, this is the same as taking the first principal component as the direction of the line. Because of this, TLS is closely related to PCA and orthogonal regression. It is useful for sensor calibration, comparing two measurement instruments, and geometric fitting in computer vision. A key limitation is that it assumes equal noise variance in all variables, so features must be scaled to comparable error levels first, and it is even more sensitive to outliers than OLS.","u":["Comparing readings from two noisy measurement instruments","Fitting lines and planes to 3D point clouds","Calibrating sensors when both axes contain error"],"x":"A medical device company compares a new blood glucose meter against a laboratory reference, and both readings contain measurement error. They use an orthogonal (total least squares) fit instead of OLS, because OLS would underestimate the slope when the reference itself is noisy. The fitted slope and intercept feed directly into the calibration report."},"polyreg":{"n":"Polynomial Regression","d":"Polynomial regression captures curved relationships by adding powers and products of the original features, then fitting an ordinary linear model on the expanded set. A degree-2 expansion of features x₁ and x₂ adds x₁², x₂² and x₁x₂; the model stays linear in its parameters, so least squares and regularization work unchanged.\n\nIt is a simple way to model smooth nonlinearity, such as diminishing returns or a U-shaped dose response, while keeping a closed-form fit. The degree controls the bias-variance trade-off: low degrees underfit, and high degrees produce wild oscillations, especially near the edges of the data, which is a classic illustration of overfitting. The number of terms also grows combinatorially with the number of features and the degree. Good practice is to scale the features before expansion, choose the degree with cross-validation, and add ridge regularization. Extrapolation outside the training range is particularly unreliable, because high-order terms explode quickly.","u":["Modeling diminishing returns of advertising spend","Fitting smooth dose-response curves in experiments","Adding interaction terms to a linear baseline"],"x":"A marketing analytics team models weekly conversions as a degree-2 polynomial of ad spend to capture saturation at high budgets. They pick the degree with cross-validation and add ridge regularization to keep coefficients stable. The fitted curve is only used within the observed spend range, because predictions beyond it are unreliable."},"logreg":{"n":"Logistic Regression","d":"Logistic regression is a linear classifier that predicts the probability of a positive class by passing a weighted sum of features through the sigmoid function. Equivalently, it models the log-odds of the outcome as a linear function of the inputs, so each coefficient is the change in log-odds for a one-unit increase in that feature.\n\nThe weights are fit by maximizing the likelihood, which is the same as minimizing binary cross-entropy. The loss is convex, so optimizers such as L-BFGS reliably find the global minimum. The softmax extension handles multiple classes. Logistic regression is fast, interpretable, and usually produces reasonably calibrated probabilities, which makes it a strong baseline for credit scoring, medical risk and click prediction. Its decision boundary is linear, so nonlinear effects require engineered features. On perfectly separable data the unregularized weights grow without bound, which is one reason scikit-learn applies L2 regularization by default. Despite its name, it is a classification method, not a regression method.","u":["Credit default scoring on tabular data","Click-through rate prediction baseline","Clinical risk scores with interpretable odds ratios"],"x":"A consumer lending company uses L2-regularized logistic regression to score loan applications, because regulators require explanations of each decision. Coefficients are converted into scorecard points, and the predicted default probability sets the interest rate tier. The team checks calibration every month, since economic shifts can move the actual default rate away from predictions."},"knn":{"n":"k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)","d":"k-nearest neighbors predicts a label for a new point by looking at the k most similar training points and taking a majority vote for classification or an average for regression. Training only stores the data, so it is called a lazy or instance-based learner.\n\nAll the modeling choices live in the distance metric and in k. A small k follows the data closely and has high variance; a large k smooths the decision boundary and increases bias. Weighting neighbors by inverse distance often helps. Because distances drive everything, features must be scaled, otherwise a feature measured in thousands dominates one measured in fractions. KNN works well for low-dimensional problems with a meaningful similarity measure, and the same idea powers modern embedding search. Its main limitations are slow prediction on large datasets, which requires index structures such as KD-trees or approximate nearest neighbor libraries, and the curse of dimensionality, which makes distances less informative as the number of features grows.","u":["Item-to-item recommendations from embedding similarity","Imputing missing values from similar records","Simple baseline for low-dimensional classification"],"x":"A music streaming service finds similar tracks by running nearest neighbor search over audio embeddings. Exact KNN is too slow for tens of millions of tracks, so they use an approximate index and accept a small loss in recall for millisecond latency. The neighbor lists are refreshed nightly when new tracks are added."},"naivebayes":{"n":"Naive Bayes","d":"Naive Bayes is a probabilistic classifier that applies Bayes' theorem under the simplifying assumption that features are conditionally independent given the class. Under that assumption, the class likelihood factorizes into a product of one-dimensional distributions, which can be estimated by simple counting or by fitting a mean and variance per feature.\n\nVariants differ in the per-feature distribution: multinomial for word counts, Bernoulli for binary indicators, and Gaussian for continuous values. Training is a single pass over the data, prediction is very fast, and the method works surprisingly well on high-dimensional sparse data such as text, even with few examples. Laplace (additive) smoothing prevents a word never seen with a class from forcing that class's probability to zero. The independence assumption is almost always violated, so although the ranking of classes is often correct, the predicted probabilities tend to be pushed toward 0 and 1 and are poorly calibrated. Correlated or duplicated features are effectively counted multiple times.","u":["Spam filtering from email word counts","Fast baseline for document topic classification","Real-time text routing with very low latency"],"x":"A customer support platform routes incoming tickets to departments with a multinomial Naive Bayes model over TF-IDF features. It retrains in seconds whenever agents relabel tickets, and predictions take microseconds. Because its probabilities are overconfident, the team only uses the top predicted class and sends low-margin cases to a human queue."},"svm":{"n":"Support Vector Machine (SVM)","d":"A support vector machine finds the decision boundary that separates two classes with the widest possible margin, the distance to the closest training points. Only those closest points, the support vectors, determine the boundary, so the solution is sparse.\n\nReal data is rarely separable, so the soft-margin formulation allows violations through slack variables, with the hyperparameter C trading margin width against training errors: a large C fits the training data tightly, while a small C gives a wider, more regularized margin. This is equivalent to minimizing hinge loss plus an L2 penalty. With the kernel trick, an SVM can learn nonlinear boundaries, for example with an RBF kernel whose gamma controls how local each support vector's influence is. SVMs suit small to medium datasets with many features, such as text. Kernel SVM training scales roughly quadratically or worse with sample count, features must be scaled, and the raw output is a margin score, not a calibrated probability.","u":["Text classification with high-dimensional sparse features","Small-sample bioinformatics classification tasks","Image classification on handcrafted feature descriptors"],"x":"A pharmaceutical research group classifies compounds as active or inactive from a few thousand molecular fingerprints using an RBF-kernel SVM. They tune C and gamma with cross-validation, since the dataset is small enough for kernel methods. Because they need probabilities for ranking candidates, they add a separate calibration step on held-out data."},"kerneltrick":{"n":"Kernel Trick","d":"The kernel trick lets a linear algorithm learn nonlinear patterns by replacing every inner product between data points with a kernel function that equals an inner product in a higher-dimensional feature space. The feature mapping itself is never computed, only pairwise kernel values.\n\nThis works for any method that can be written entirely in terms of dot products, including support vector machines, kernel ridge regression, kernel PCA and Gaussian processes. Common kernels are the polynomial kernel and the RBF kernel, whose feature space is infinite-dimensional. A valid kernel must produce a positive semidefinite Gram matrix for any set of points (Mercer's condition). The main cost is that the n × n kernel matrix makes training scale at least quadratically in memory and time, so kernel methods become impractical beyond tens of thousands of samples. Approximations such as random Fourier features or the Nyström method restore linear scaling at some loss of accuracy.","u":["Nonlinear SVM classification without explicit features","Gaussian process regression for surrogate modeling","Kernel PCA for nonlinear dimensionality reduction"],"x":"An engineering team builds a Gaussian process surrogate model to tune manufacturing process settings, using an RBF kernel to encode that similar settings give similar yields. With only a few hundred experiments, the n × n kernel matrix is cheap to compute. For a larger sensor dataset they switch to random Fourier features to keep training time linear."},"lda":{"n":"Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)","d":"Linear discriminant analysis is both a classifier and a supervised dimensionality reduction method that finds directions best separating known classes. As a classifier, it assumes each class is a Gaussian with its own mean but a shared covariance matrix; under that assumption the Bayes-optimal decision boundaries are linear.\n\nAs a projection, LDA maximizes the ratio of between-class scatter to within-class scatter, which yields at most C − 1 discriminant directions for C classes. This differs from PCA, which ignores labels and keeps the directions of largest total variance, even if they do not separate the classes. LDA is fast, has a closed-form solution, and works well with small samples when its assumptions roughly hold; shrinkage of the covariance estimate helps when features outnumber samples. It struggles when classes have very different covariances, where quadratic discriminant analysis fits better, and when the class structure is nonlinear. In NLP, the same acronym also refers to Latent Dirichlet Allocation, an unrelated topic model.","u":["Supervised projection before visualization or clustering","Classifying EEG signals in brain-computer interfaces","Small-sample classification with shrinkage covariance"],"x":"A brain-computer interface team classifies imagined hand movements from EEG features with shrinkage LDA. The method trains in milliseconds on a short calibration session per user and gives stable results with few trials. Its linear boundary also keeps inference fast enough for real-time control."},"perceptron":{"n":"Perceptron","d":"The perceptron is the simplest linear classifier: it outputs one class if a weighted sum of the inputs is positive and the other class otherwise, and updates its weights only after a mistake. On each misclassified example, it adds the input vector, scaled by the true label and a learning rate, to the weight vector.\n\nThe perceptron convergence theorem guarantees that this rule finds a separating hyperplane in a finite number of updates if the data is linearly separable. If the data is not separable, the weights never settle, which is why variants such as the averaged or pocket perceptron exist. The step activation has no useful gradient, so modern neural networks replaced it with differentiable activations and gradient descent; a multilayer perceptron is therefore not a stack of classic perceptrons. Its inability to learn XOR showed the need for hidden layers. It still serves as a fast online learner for large sparse problems and as a teaching example.","u":["Online learning on streaming sparse features","Teaching linear separability and learning rules","Fast baseline for linearly separable problems"],"x":"A search team prototypes a query intent classifier using an averaged perceptron over millions of sparse n-gram features. The model updates online as new labeled queries arrive and needs no tuning beyond the number of passes. Once the prototype proves the features are useful, they replace it with logistic regression to obtain probability scores."},"dtree":{"n":"Decision Tree","d":"A decision tree predicts by asking a sequence of simple questions about the features, and following the answers down to a leaf that stores a class or a value. Each node splits on one feature and threshold, and the leaves partition the feature space into axis-aligned rectangles.\n\nTrees are grown greedily from the top: at each node the algorithm picks the split that most reduces impurity, measured by Gini impurity or entropy for classification and by variance (squared error) for regression. They need no feature scaling, capture interactions automatically, and are easy to explain. Their main weakness is high variance: a fully grown tree memorizes noise, and a small change in the data can produce a very different tree. Limiting depth, requiring a minimum number of samples per leaf, or pruning reduces overfitting. In practice, single trees are mostly used for interpretability, while ensembles such as random forests and gradient boosting use trees as building blocks for accuracy.","u":["Explainable rules for eligibility or triage decisions","Building block for random forests and boosting","Quick exploratory analysis of feature interactions"],"x":"A hospital triage team trains a shallow decision tree, limited to depth four, to flag emergency patients who need immediate review. Clinicians accepted it because every prediction can be traced as a short list of conditions. The team deliberately accepts slightly lower accuracy than a boosted model in exchange for that transparency."},"gini":{"n":"Gini Impurity","d":"Gini impurity measures how mixed the class labels in a node are: it equals the probability that two labels drawn at random, with replacement, from the node are different. A pure node, where every sample has the same class, has a Gini impurity of 0; for two equally frequent classes it reaches its maximum of 0.5.\n\nDecision tree algorithms such as CART evaluate each candidate split by the weighted average Gini impurity of the child nodes and choose the split with the largest decrease from the parent. It needs no logarithms and selects almost the same splits as entropy. Summing the impurity decreases a feature produces across all trees gives the \"mean decrease in impurity\" feature importance used by random forests. That importance is computed on training data and is biased toward high-cardinality and continuous features, which offer many split points. Gini impurity should not be confused with the Gini coefficient used in credit scoring, which equals 2·AUC − 1.","u":["Choosing splits in CART classification trees","Impurity-based feature importance in random forests","Measuring label purity of clusters or segments"],"x":"A fraud analytics team inspects impurity-based importances from their random forest and notices that a high-cardinality merchant ID ranks first. Because Gini-based importance favors features with many split points, they confirm the ranking with permutation importance on a validation set. The merchant ID drops sharply in the permutation ranking, so they drop it from the model."},"entropy":{"n":"Entropy & Information Gain","d":"Entropy measures the uncertainty of a label distribution in bits, and information gain is the reduction in entropy achieved by splitting the data on a feature. A node with a single class has entropy 0, while a node with two equally likely classes has entropy 1 bit, the maximum for binary labels.\n\nDecision tree algorithms such as ID3 and C4.5, and CART with criterion=\"entropy\", choose the split that maximizes information gain: the parent's entropy minus the size-weighted entropy of the children. Information gain equals the mutual information between the split and the label. In practice it produces trees very similar to those from Gini impurity, with slightly higher compute cost because of the logarithm. A known pitfall is that raw information gain favors features with many distinct values, such as IDs, since many tiny pure groups yield high gain without generalizing. C4.5 corrects for this with the gain ratio, which divides the gain by the entropy of the split itself.","u":["Selecting splits in ID3, C4.5 and CART trees","Ranking features by mutual information with the label","Choosing informative questions in active learning"],"x":"A telecom churn team ranks hundreds of candidate features by their mutual information with the churn label before building models. This information-gain screening quickly removes features with almost no relationship to churn. They then check survivors for leakage, because suspiciously high gain often comes from fields updated after the customer left."},"cart":{"n":"CART & Axis-Aligned Splits","d":"CART (Classification and Regression Trees) builds binary decision trees by repeatedly splitting the data on one feature at one threshold. Each split asks whether x_j ≤ t, so every decision boundary is perpendicular to one feature axis and the tree partitions the space into rectangular boxes.\n\nAt each node CART searches every feature and every candidate threshold, typically midpoints between sorted unique values, and picks the pair that most reduces Gini impurity for classification or squared error for regression. The search is greedy, so the tree is not globally optimal. CART also defines cost-complexity pruning, which trades tree size against training error. Axis-aligned splits make trees invariant to monotonic feature transformations, so scaling is unnecessary, and they keep rules readable. The downside is that a diagonal boundary, such as x₁ > x₂, must be approximated by a staircase of many splits; adding a difference feature or using oblique trees can help. scikit-learn's trees implement optimized CART.","u":["Building interpretable binary classification trees","Regression trees for piecewise-constant predictions","Base learners inside random forests and boosting"],"x":"An insurance pricing team uses CART regression trees to segment policyholders into groups with similar claim costs. Because each split is a single threshold on one feature, the resulting segments can be written as plain rules for actuaries to review. They add engineered ratio features when they notice the tree building long staircases to approximate diagonal boundaries."},"pruning":{"n":"Tree Pruning","d":"Tree pruning reduces overfitting in decision trees by removing branches that add complexity without improving generalization. Pre-pruning, or early stopping, prevents the tree from growing past limits such as a maximum depth or a minimum number of samples per leaf. Post-pruning first grows a large tree and then collapses subtrees whose removal costs little accuracy.\n\nThe standard post-pruning method in CART is minimal cost-complexity pruning. It minimizes the training error plus α times the number of leaves; increasing α produces a nested sequence of smaller trees, and cross-validation picks the best α. Post-pruning is often better than early stopping because a split that looks useless on its own can enable valuable splits below it, a situation greedy stopping rules miss. Pruning matters most for single interpretable trees. It is usually unnecessary in random forests, where averaging many deep trees controls variance, and in gradient boosting, where trees are kept shallow by design.","u":["Simplifying decision trees for human-readable rules","Reducing overfitting of single trees on noisy data","Selecting tree complexity via cross-validated alpha"],"x":"A credit policy team needs a decision tree small enough to print on one page for compliance review. They grow a full tree, compute the cost-complexity pruning path, and choose the largest alpha whose cross-validated accuracy stays within one standard error of the best. The final tree has a dozen leaves instead of several hundred."},"bootstrap":{"n":"Bootstrap Sampling","d":"Bootstrap sampling creates a new dataset of the same size as the original by drawing observations at random with replacement. Some points appear several times and others not at all; on average about 63.2% of the unique original points appear in each sample, because the chance that a given point is never drawn is (1 − 1/n)ⁿ ≈ 1/e ≈ 36.8%.\n\nRepeated resampling approximates drawing new datasets from the population. Statisticians use it to estimate the standard error or a confidence interval of almost any statistic, such as a median, an AUC or a model coefficient, without a closed-form formula. In machine learning, bootstrap samples are the basis of bagging and random forests, and the left-out points provide out-of-bag validation. The bootstrap assumes the observations are independent and identically distributed, so naive resampling underestimates uncertainty for time series or grouped data; block or cluster bootstrap variants are needed there. It also fails for extreme-value statistics such as the maximum.","u":["Confidence intervals for model evaluation metrics","Generating diverse training sets for bagging ensembles","Estimating uncertainty in A/B test effects"],"x":"An ML platform team reports every offline AUC with a 95% bootstrap confidence interval computed from 1,000 resamples of the test set. This prevents teams from shipping a model whose apparent improvement is within noise. For data with repeated users, they resample whole users rather than individual rows to keep the intervals honest."},"bagging":{"n":"Bagging","d":"Bagging, short for bootstrap aggregating, trains many copies of the same model on different bootstrap samples of the training data and averages their predictions, or takes a majority vote. Because each model sees a slightly different dataset, their errors are partly independent, and averaging cancels much of that noise.\n\nBagging mainly reduces variance while leaving bias roughly unchanged, so it helps most with unstable, low-bias learners such as fully grown decision trees, and does little for stable models like linear regression. The variance of an average of B models with pairwise correlation ρ is ρσ² + (1 − ρ)σ²/B, so adding models has diminishing returns once the correlation term dominates. Random forests go further by decorrelating trees through feature subsampling. Bagged models can be trained in parallel, and the points left out of each bootstrap sample give a free out-of-bag estimate of generalization error. A common misconception is that bagging fixes underfitting; averaging many shallow, biased models remains biased.","u":["Stabilizing high-variance decision trees","Parallel ensemble training on multi-core machines","Reducing variance of neural network predictions"],"x":"A demand forecasting team bags twenty deep regression trees trained on bootstrap samples of store-level history. The bagged ensemble is noticeably more stable week to week than a single tree, which store managers had complained about. Training runs in parallel across cores, so the ensemble adds little wall-clock time."},"oob":{"n":"Out-of-Bag (OOB) Score","d":"The out-of-bag score is a validation estimate for bagged ensembles that uses, for each training point, only the models whose bootstrap sample did not include that point. Since each bootstrap sample leaves out about 36.8% of the data, every point is out-of-bag for roughly a third of the trees, and their aggregated prediction acts like a held-out prediction.\n\nComparing these OOB predictions with the true labels gives an accuracy, R² or other metric that approximates cross-validation without extra models or a validation split. It is convenient for tuning random forests on small datasets and checking whether more trees still help. OOB predictions are made by only about a third of the ensemble, so with few trees the estimate is noisy and slightly pessimistic. Like any row-level resampling, it is overly optimistic when rows are not independent, for example with time series or repeated measurements of the same customer; a grouped or time-based split is needed in those cases.","u":["Tuning random forests without a validation split","Quick generalization estimate on small datasets","Choosing the number of trees in bagging"],"x":"A small genomics lab with only a few hundred patient samples uses the random forest OOB score instead of a separate validation set, so no data is wasted. They tune max_features by comparing OOB accuracy across a short grid. Before publishing, they confirm the result with grouped cross-validation, because some patients contributed multiple samples."},"rf":{"n":"Random Forest","d":"A random forest is an ensemble of decision trees, each trained on a bootstrap sample of the data, whose predictions are averaged or combined by vote. Its key addition to bagging is feature subsampling: at every split, each tree considers only a random subset of features and picks the best split among them.\n\nSampling features anew at each split, not once per tree, keeps strong predictors from dominating every tree and decorrelates the ensemble, which lets averaging reduce variance much more. Trees are usually grown deep, since averaging handles their variance. Random forests work well out of the box on tabular data, do not overfit from adding more trees, give a free out-of-bag error estimate, and parallelize easily. However, they are usually less accurate than tuned gradient boosting, produce large models with slower inference, cannot extrapolate beyond the range of the training targets, and their default impurity-based importances are biased toward high-cardinality features.","u":["Strong default model for tabular classification","Feature screening with permutation importance","Robust baselines on small, noisy datasets"],"x":"A manufacturing quality team predicts defective parts from sensor readings with a random forest of 500 trees. It needed almost no tuning, handles missing sensor channels after simple imputation, and its OOB score tracks well with production accuracy. When latency became an issue on edge devices, they reduced tree depth and count after measuring the accuracy cost."},"maxfeat":{"n":"Feature Subsampling (max_features)","d":"Feature subsampling restricts each split in a tree to a random subset of the features, controlled in scikit-learn by the max_features parameter. The tree still picks the best split among the sampled features, and a fresh subset is drawn at every node.\n\nThis is the ingredient that turns bagged trees into a random forest. When one or two features are very strong, plain bagging produces trees that all split on them first, so the trees are correlated and averaging helps little. Hiding those features from some splits forces trees to explore other structure and lowers the correlation between trees, reducing ensemble variance. Common defaults are √p for classification and between p/3 and p for regression; scikit-learn's RandomForestRegressor uses all features by default. Smaller values increase diversity but raise the bias of each tree, and with many irrelevant features a small subset may often contain no useful feature at all. XGBoost offers similar options, colsample_bytree and colsample_bynode.","u":["Decorrelating trees in random forests","Regularizing gradient boosting via column sampling","Speeding up training on very wide datasets"],"x":"A marketing team's random forest kept splitting on a single dominant spending feature, and adding trees stopped improving validation scores. Lowering max_features from all features to the square root made the trees more diverse and improved out-of-bag accuracy. They chose the final value by comparing OOB scores across a small grid."},"extratrees":{"n":"Extra Trees","d":"Extra Trees, short for extremely randomized trees, is a tree ensemble that adds randomness to the split thresholds as well as to the features. At each node it samples a subset of features, draws one random threshold for each within that feature's range in the node, and keeps the best of these random candidate splits.\n\nBecause thresholds are not optimized, each tree has somewhat higher bias, but the trees are less correlated and the ensemble's variance drops further than in a random forest. Skipping the full threshold search also makes training faster. By default, scikit-learn's Extra Trees fit each tree on the whole training set without bootstrap sampling, so out-of-bag scores are unavailable unless bootstrap is enabled. Extra Trees often match random forests in accuracy and can be better on noisy, high-dimensional data, and give smoother regression surfaces. They can underperform when only a few features carry signal through precise thresholds, since random cut points may miss them.","u":["Fast tree ensembles on large tabular data","Variance reduction on noisy, high-dimensional features","Feature importance screening at low compute cost"],"x":"A remote sensing team classifies land cover from hundreds of spectral bands per pixel. They switched from a random forest to Extra Trees because training was faster on millions of pixels and accuracy was the same on their validation maps. The faster training lets them retrain a model for each new satellite scene."},"boosting":{"n":"Boosting","d":"Boosting builds a strong model by adding weak learners one at a time, with each new learner focusing on the mistakes of the ensemble built so far. The prediction is a weighted sum of all learners, usually shallow trees.\n\nIn AdaBoost, misclassified examples receive larger weights so the next learner concentrates on them. In gradient boosting, each new tree is fit to the negative gradient of the loss with respect to the current ensemble's predictions, which for squared error is simply the residual of the whole ensemble so far. Unlike bagging, which trains independent models in parallel to reduce variance, boosting is sequential and mainly reduces bias. Boosted trees, as in XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost, lead most tabular benchmarks. Because each step keeps fitting the training errors, boosting can overfit with too many rounds or deep trees, so a small learning rate, early stopping on validation data and regularization are essential. Label noise is a further weakness, particularly for AdaBoost.","u":["Winning models for tabular prediction tasks","Learning-to-rank for search and recommendations","Risk scoring with many heterogeneous features"],"x":"An online marketplace predicts the probability that a listing sells within a week using gradient-boosted trees over price, seller history and category features. Boosting was chosen because it consistently beat neural networks on their tabular data with far less tuning. They use early stopping on a time-based validation set to choose the number of trees."},"adaboost":{"n":"AdaBoost","d":"AdaBoost (Adaptive Boosting) is the original practical boosting algorithm: it trains a sequence of weak classifiers, typically one-split decision stumps, and after each round increases the weights of the training examples the current classifier got wrong. The next classifier is therefore forced to concentrate on the hard cases.\n\nEach classifier also receives a vote weight α = ½ ln((1 − ε)/ε), where ε is its weighted error, so more accurate learners have more say in the final weighted majority vote. AdaBoost can be shown to perform forward stagewise additive modeling with the exponential loss, which links it to gradient boosting. It is simple, has few hyperparameters, and historically powered the Viola–Jones real-time face detector. Its main weakness is sensitivity to label noise and outliers: mislabeled points receive exponentially growing weights and can dominate later rounds. On modern tabular benchmarks it is usually outperformed by gradient boosting libraries, which support other losses, regularization and much faster training.","u":["Boosting decision stumps for simple classifiers","Cascaded real-time object detection","Teaching the core idea of reweighting errors"],"x":"The Viola–Jones face detector, used in early digital cameras, applied AdaBoost to select a small number of informative Haar-like features from a very large pool. The boosted classifiers were arranged in a cascade so that most background windows were rejected after evaluating only a few features. This made real-time detection possible on modest hardware."},"gbm":{"n":"Gradient Boosting","d":"Gradient boosting builds an additive model of decision trees in which each new tree is trained to predict the negative gradient of the loss function with respect to the current ensemble's predictions. Viewed this way, boosting is gradient descent in function space: every tree is one step that moves the predictions downhill on the loss.\n\nFor squared error the negative gradient is just the residual y − F(x); for log loss it is y − p. Only the gradient changes, so one algorithm handles regression, classification, ranking and custom objectives. Key hyperparameters are the learning rate, number of trees, tree size and minimum samples per leaf. A lower learning rate with more trees usually generalizes better, and early stopping on validation loss chooses the tree count. Classic implementations, including scikit-learn's GradientBoostingClassifier, use all rows by default (subsample = 1.0). Gradient boosting overfits more easily than random forests when left untuned, and its sequential training parallelizes only within each tree.","u":["Tabular regression and classification in production","Ranking with pairwise or listwise objectives","Insurance claim severity and frequency modeling"],"x":"An insurance company predicts claim frequency with gradient-boosted trees using a Poisson loss and exposure as an offset. Boosting captured interactions between driver age, vehicle type and region that the previous generalized linear model missed. The pricing team still reviews partial dependence plots before each release to make sure the effects are plausible."},"xgboost":{"n":"XGBoost","d":"XGBoost is a widely used gradient boosting library that adds explicit regularization and a second-order optimization step to classic gradient-boosted trees. For every candidate tree it uses both the gradient g and the Hessian h of the loss, computes optimal leaf weights in closed form, and scores splits by how much they reduce a regularized objective.\n\nThe objective penalizes the number of leaves through γ and the size of leaf weights through λ (L2) and α (L1), making trees more conservative. XGBoost also learns a default direction for missing values at each split, supports exact, approximate and histogram-based split finding, column and row subsampling, GPU training and custom objectives. It became a standard tool for tabular data. Common mistakes include tuning only n_estimators while leaving max_depth too large, forgetting early stopping, and assuming row subsampling is on by default; subsample defaults to 1. For very large or high-cardinality categorical data, LightGBM or CatBoost may be faster or more convenient.","u":["Click-through and conversion prediction on tabular logs","Fraud detection with missing and sparse features","Strong baseline in data science competitions"],"x":"A payments company scores every card transaction with an XGBoost model on features such as amount, merchant category and recent velocity counts. The histogram tree method keeps nightly retraining on hundreds of millions of rows within a fixed time budget. Inference runs in a few milliseconds per transaction, and SHAP values are logged so analysts can explain declines."},"lightgbm":{"n":"LightGBM","d":"LightGBM is a gradient boosting library from Microsoft designed for speed and memory efficiency on large datasets. It bins continuous features into histograms, grows trees leaf-wise rather than level-wise, and offers techniques such as Gradient-based One-Side Sampling (GOSS) and Exclusive Feature Bundling to reduce work further.\n\nLeaf-wise growth always splits the leaf with the largest loss reduction, so for the same number of leaves it typically reaches lower training loss than depth-wise growth. The trade-off is that trees can become very deep and overfit small datasets; num_leaves is therefore the main complexity control, together with min_data_in_leaf and max_depth. LightGBM natively handles categorical features by searching for an optimal grouping of categories, and treats missing values automatically. It is a common first choice for large tabular problems and learning-to-rank. A frequent pitfall is setting num_leaves close to 2^max_depth on small data, which produces overfit models; another is passing high-cardinality categoricals without enough regularization, which can also overfit.","u":["Large-scale tabular classification and regression","Learning-to-rank with the LambdaRank objective","Demand forecasting with many lag features"],"x":"A grocery delivery company forecasts item-level demand for thousands of stores with LightGBM on lag, calendar and promotion features. They picked LightGBM because one global model trains on hundreds of millions of rows in minutes on a single machine. Store and item IDs are passed as categorical features, with min_data_per_group raised to avoid overfitting rare items."},"catboost":{"n":"CatBoost","d":"CatBoost is a gradient boosting library from Yandex designed for categorical features and for reducing a subtle overfitting called prediction shift. It converts categories into numbers with ordered target statistics: each row's encoding is computed only from the target values of rows that come before it in a random permutation, so a row's own label never leaks into its feature.\n\nThe same idea, ordered boosting, is applied to the gradients used to build trees. CatBoost also grows oblivious (symmetric) trees, in which every node at the same depth uses the same split; this acts as a regularizer and makes inference very fast. As a result, CatBoost often gives strong results with default settings on data with many high-cardinality categoricals, such as user or product IDs. Training can be slower than LightGBM on purely numeric data, and categorical columns must be declared explicitly; passing them as integer codes without declaring them loses the benefit of the ordered encoding.","u":["Tabular data with many high-cardinality categoricals","Strong results with little hyperparameter tuning","Low-latency scoring with symmetric trees"],"x":"A travel booking site predicts whether a user will book a hotel using features like city, hotel ID, device and traffic source, many with thousands of levels. CatBoost was chosen because its ordered target statistics avoided the leakage the team had seen with manual target encoding. The symmetric trees also keep scoring latency low when ranking hundreds of hotels per search."},"goss":{"n":"GOSS (Gradient-based One-Side Sampling)","d":"GOSS is a row sampling technique introduced in LightGBM that speeds up gradient boosting by keeping all training examples with large gradients and only a random fraction of those with small gradients. Small-gradient examples are already fit well, so they add little information to the next split.\n\nConcretely, GOSS sorts rows by the absolute value of their gradient, keeps the top a fraction, randomly samples a b fraction from the rest, and multiplies the sampled small-gradient rows by (1 − a)/b when computing split gains. That reweighting keeps the gain estimate approximately unbiased while greatly reducing the number of rows processed per iteration. It differs from uniform random subsampling in stochastic gradient boosting, which ignores gradients. A caveat is that it can amplify the influence of noisy or mislabeled points, which often have large gradients, and in recent LightGBM versions it is selected with data_sample_strategy=\"goss\" rather than as a boosting type.","u":["Faster boosting on very large tabular datasets","Reducing per-iteration cost in LightGBM training","Focusing compute on poorly fit examples"],"x":"An ad-tech team retrains a LightGBM click model several times a day on billions of impression logs. Enabling GOSS reduced training time substantially with only a small change in validation log loss, which they verified in an offline comparison before switching. They monitor the effect separately for rare ad categories, where aggressive sampling could hurt."},"histsplit":{"n":"Histogram-Based Splitting","d":"Histogram-based splitting speeds up decision tree training by first discretizing each continuous feature into a small number of bins, commonly 255, and then searching for splits only at bin boundaries. Instead of sorting every feature value at every node, the algorithm accumulates the sum of gradients and Hessians (or counts) per bin and scans these short histograms.\n\nThis reduces split finding from roughly O(n) per feature per node, after sorting, to O(bins), and stores features as small integers, saving memory. A further trick is histogram subtraction: a child's histogram can be obtained by subtracting its sibling's histogram from the parent's, so only the smaller child needs to be built. LightGBM, XGBoost's hist method, CatBoost and scikit-learn's HistGradientBoosting all rely on it. The loss of precision from binning rarely hurts accuracy and can act as mild regularization. It may matter when a feature has an important threshold that falls inside a wide bin, or for features with very skewed distributions.","u":["Fast gradient boosting on millions of rows","Memory-efficient training with quantized features","GPU-accelerated tree construction for large datasets"],"x":"A logistics company retrains an arrival-time model daily on tens of millions of past deliveries. Moving from exact split finding to scikit-learn's HistGradientBoostingRegressor cut training time from hours to minutes with no meaningful change in error. The faster cycle allowed them to add new features and retrain more often."},"subsample":{"n":"Row Subsampling (Stochastic GB)","d":"Row subsampling, introduced as stochastic gradient boosting, trains each boosting tree on a random fraction of the training rows drawn without replacement. Typical values of the subsample parameter are between 0.5 and 0.9.\n\nThe randomness makes successive trees less dependent on the same points, which regularizes the model and often improves test error. It also makes each iteration cheaper. In libraries the parameter is opt-in: scikit-learn's GradientBoosting, XGBoost and LightGBM all default to using every row, so subsample = 1.0, and LightGBM additionally requires bagging_freq > 0 for bagging_fraction to take effect. If the fraction is too small, each tree sees too little data and the model needs many more rounds, which can increase total training time and noise. For imbalanced data, plain row sampling may leave few positives per tree; in that case keep a higher fraction or use stratified or gradient-aware sampling such as GOSS.","u":["Regularizing gradient boosting against overfitting","Reducing per-tree training cost on large data","Adding diversity to boosted tree ensembles"],"x":"A churn prediction team found their XGBoost model overfitting to a few heavy users. Setting subsample to 0.7 and colsample_bytree to 0.8, together with a lower learning rate, improved validation AUC and made results more stable across retraining runs. They fix the random seed in production so that retraining differences reflect data changes rather than sampling noise."},"shrinkage":{"n":"Shrinkage (Learning Rate in Boosting)","d":"Shrinkage scales the contribution of each new tree in a boosting ensemble by a learning rate η between 0 and 1, so the model moves only part of the way toward correcting its current errors at each step. Instead of F_m = F_{m−1} + h_m, the update becomes F_m = F_{m−1} + η·h_m.\n\nSmaller steps leave room for later trees to correct mistakes and reduce dependence on any single tree, which improves generalization. The cost is that a smaller learning rate needs proportionally more trees to reach the same training loss, so η and the number of estimators must be tuned together. A common practice is to fix a rate between 0.01 and 0.1 and choose the tree count with early stopping. A frequent mistake is tuning the number of trees at one learning rate and then changing the rate without retuning. Shrinkage in boosting is unrelated to shrinkage estimators such as ridge regression, although both reduce variance.","u":["Improving generalization of gradient-boosted trees","Trading training time for accuracy in boosting","Pairing with early stopping to set tree count"],"x":"A credit risk team trains final LightGBM models with a learning rate of 0.02 and early stopping, after doing faster hyperparameter search at 0.1. The lower rate gives a small but consistent gain in validation AUC at the cost of several times more trees. They check that the extra trees still meet the scoring latency budget before deploying."},"stacking":{"n":"Stacking","d":"Stacking, or stacked generalization, combines several different models by training a second-level model, the meta-learner, to make the final prediction from the base models' outputs. Rather than averaging with fixed weights, the meta-learner learns how much to trust each base model, and possibly where.\n\nThe crucial detail is that the meta-learner must be trained on out-of-fold predictions: each base model is fit with K-fold cross-validation, and its predictions on the held-out folds form the training features for the second level. Using in-sample predictions would leak the training labels and make the meta-learner trust overfit models. Stacking works best with diverse base learners, for example a gradient boosting model, a linear model and a neural network, and a simple meta-learner such as regularized logistic or linear regression. It often gives small but reliable gains and is popular in competitions. In production, it multiplies training and inference cost and complexity, so the gain must justify maintaining several models.","u":["Combining diverse models for competition leaderboards","Blending tree and linear models on tabular data","Merging text and tabular model predictions"],"x":"An insurance fraud team stacks a gradient boosting model on tabular claim data with a text classifier on adjuster notes, using logistic regression as the meta-learner. Out-of-fold predictions from five-fold cross-validation train the meta-learner, preventing leakage. The combined model outperforms either input alone, and the meta-learner's coefficients show how much each source contributes."},"voting":{"n":"Voting Ensemble","d":"A voting ensemble combines the predictions of several independently trained models with a fixed rule instead of a learned one. In hard voting, each classifier votes for a class and the majority wins; in soft voting, the predicted class probabilities are averaged, optionally with weights, and the class with the highest average is chosen. For regression, the equivalent is averaging predictions.\n\nVoting helps when the models are individually reasonable and make different kinds of errors, because uncorrelated mistakes tend to cancel. Soft voting usually beats hard voting since it uses each model's confidence, but it requires reasonably calibrated probabilities; a badly calibrated or overconfident model can dominate the average. Voting is simpler and less prone to overfitting than stacking, because there is no meta-learner to train, but it cannot learn that one model is better in certain situations. Adding a weak or highly correlated model can make the ensemble worse, so each member should earn its place through validation.","u":["Combining diverse classifiers for robust predictions","Averaging models trained with different random seeds","Simple ensembles where stacking would overfit"],"x":"A medical imaging startup averages the probabilities of three convolutional networks trained with different architectures to flag suspicious scans. Soft voting reduced the number of confident mistakes compared with any single network. They calibrate each network with temperature scaling before averaging so that no single model dominates."},"confusion":{"n":"Confusion Matrix","d":"A confusion matrix is a table that counts how a classifier's predictions line up with the true labels, with one row per actual class and one column per predicted class. For a binary problem it contains four cells: true positives, false positives, false negatives and true negatives.\n\nNearly every classification metric is computed from these counts. Precision uses the predicted-positive column, recall uses the actual-positive row, and accuracy uses the diagonal. For multiclass problems, the off-diagonal cells show exactly which classes are confused with each other, which helps debugging more than a single score; for example, it may reveal that a model mixes up two similar product categories while handling the rest well. Normalizing each row by its total shows per-class recall. A binary confusion matrix depends on the chosen decision threshold, so it describes one operating point, not the model as a whole. Conventions also differ: scikit-learn puts actual classes in rows, while some textbooks use the transpose.","u":["Diagnosing which classes a model confuses","Deriving precision, recall and specificity","Communicating error types to business stakeholders"],"x":"A document processing company classifies scanned forms into twenty types and reviews a row-normalized confusion matrix after each retraining. It showed that two similar tax forms were frequently swapped, so they added a feature from the form header. Monitoring that cell in production then confirmed the fix held."},"accuracy":{"n":"Accuracy","d":"Accuracy is the fraction of predictions that are correct: the number of true positives and true negatives divided by the total number of examples. It is the most intuitive classification metric and works well when classes are roughly balanced and every kind of error has a similar cost.\n\nIts major weakness is class imbalance. If only 1% of transactions are fraudulent, a model that predicts \"not fraud\" for everything reaches 99% accuracy while catching nothing. In such cases precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC or balanced accuracy, the average of per-class recall, give a more honest picture. Accuracy also hides which errors occur and depends on the decision threshold, so two models with equal accuracy can behave very differently. A good habit is to always compare accuracy against the majority-class baseline. Top-k accuracy, which counts a prediction as correct if the true class is among the k highest-scoring classes, is common in image classification and recommendation.","u":["Evaluating balanced multiclass image classifiers","Top-k accuracy for large label spaces","Quick sanity check against majority baseline"],"x":"An e-commerce team classifying product photos into balanced categories reports top-1 and top-5 accuracy as its main metrics. For their returns-fraud model, by contrast, they explicitly avoid accuracy, because fraud is rare and a trivial model would look excellent. That model is evaluated with precision at a fixed review capacity instead."},"precision":{"n":"Precision","d":"Precision is the fraction of positive predictions that are actually positive. It answers the question: when the model raises an alarm, how often is it right? It ignores correctly rejected negatives and missed positives.\n\nPrecision matters most when false positives are costly, for example when a flagged payment blocks a legitimate customer or when every alert needs human review. Raising the decision threshold usually increases precision while lowering recall, so the two must be reported together or combined in a single number such as F1. Precision depends on the class prior: the same model will show lower precision on a population where positives are rarer, even if its ability to separate classes is unchanged. Precision at k, the precision among the top k ranked items, is the standard variant for search and review queues. Precision is undefined when the model makes no positive predictions, and libraries then return 0 with a warning.","u":["Alert systems with limited human review capacity","Spam filtering where false positives hurt users","Precision at k for search result quality"],"x":"A bank's anti-money-laundering team can investigate only a fixed number of alerts per day, so they tune the model's threshold to maximize precision among the top-ranked cases. They report precision at that daily capacity rather than overall accuracy. When precision drops, investigators spend time on false alarms, so it is tracked weekly."},"recall":{"n":"Recall","d":"Recall, also called sensitivity or true positive rate, is the fraction of actual positives that the model correctly identifies. It answers the question: of all the cases we should have caught, how many did we catch?\n\nRecall is the key metric when missing a positive is expensive, as in cancer screening, fraud detection or safety-critical defect inspection. Lowering the decision threshold increases recall, but it also produces more false positives and lowers precision, so recall alone can always be made perfect by predicting everything as positive. It should therefore be reported together with precision or with specificity, the recall of the negative class. Unlike precision, recall does not depend on the class prior, because it only looks at actual positives. In retrieval and recommendation, recall at k measures the fraction of all relevant items that appear in the top k results, which is the usual metric for evaluating the candidate generation stage of a recommender or a vector search index.","u":["Disease screening where misses are dangerous","Recall at k for retrieval candidate generation","Defect detection on manufacturing lines"],"x":"A semiconductor manufacturer sets the threshold of its wafer defect detector so that recall on a held-out set of known defects stays above a target agreed with quality engineers. The resulting false alarms are reviewed by a second, slower inspection step. Recall is recalculated on every new product line, because new defect types are often missed at first."},"f1":{"n":"F1 Score","d":"The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, giving a single number that is high only when both are high. Because the harmonic mean is dominated by the smaller value, a model with precision 1.0 and recall 0.1 gets an F1 of about 0.18, not 0.55 as a simple average would give.\n\nF1 is common in imbalanced classification and for tasks such as named entity recognition, where true negatives are numerous and uninformative; F1 does not use TN at all. The more general F-beta score weights recall β times as much as precision, so F2 favors recall and F0.5 favors precision. For multiclass problems, macro-F1 averages per-class F1 equally, which highlights rare classes, while micro-F1 pools all counts and, for single-label multiclass tasks, equals accuracy. F1 depends on the decision threshold, so comparing models at the default 0.5 can be misleading. It also assumes precision and recall matter equally, which is rarely true in practice.","u":["Evaluating named entity recognition and extraction","Imbalanced classification model comparison","Macro-F1 for multiclass tasks with rare classes"],"x":"A legal tech company evaluates its clause extraction model with entity-level F1, because both missed clauses and spurious extractions create review work. They track macro-F1 across clause types so that rare but important clauses are not hidden by common ones. When a client prioritizes completeness, they report F2 instead."},"roc":{"n":"ROC Curve","d":"The ROC (receiver operating characteristic) curve shows how a binary classifier trades off true positives against false positives as its decision threshold moves. Each point plots the true positive rate (recall) against the false positive rate for one threshold, tracing a curve from (0, 0) to (1, 1).\n\nA perfect classifier reaches the top-left corner, while random guessing lies on the diagonal. Because both rates are computed within a single class, the curve does not change if the class ratio changes, which makes it useful for comparing a model's ranking ability across datasets. The same property is a weakness under heavy imbalance: a false positive rate of 1% can still mean thousands of false alarms when negatives vastly outnumber positives, which looks small on the ROC plot. There, the precision–recall curve is more informative. The ROC curve depends only on the order of scores, so it says nothing about whether predicted probabilities are calibrated.","u":["Choosing an operating point for a classifier","Comparing ranking quality across models","Diagnostic test evaluation in medicine"],"x":"A radiology AI vendor presents ROC curves for its fracture detector to hospitals, together with the sensitivity and specificity at the operating point used in practice. Each hospital can then pick a threshold that matches its own tolerance for false alarms. The vendor reports the curve separately for each scanner manufacturer to reveal performance shifts."},"rocauc":{"n":"ROC-AUC","d":"ROC-AUC is the area under the ROC curve, summarizing how well a model ranks positives above negatives across all thresholds. It has a direct probabilistic meaning: the AUC equals the probability that a randomly chosen positive example receives a higher score than a randomly chosen negative example, with ties counted as half.\n\nA value of 0.5 means random ranking and 1.0 perfect separation. Because it is threshold-free and insensitive to the class ratio, ROC-AUC is a standard metric for comparing models in credit scoring, medicine and advertising. It measures ranking only, so a model with a good AUC can still be badly calibrated. Under heavy class imbalance, a high AUC can coexist with poor precision, because much of the area comes from unusably high false positive rates; PR-AUC or partial AUC is often more relevant there. In credit scoring, the Gini coefficient equals 2·AUC − 1.","u":["Comparing credit scoring models","Threshold-free evaluation of diagnostic tests","Tracking ranking quality of click models"],"x":"A consumer credit team tracks ROC-AUC, reported as Gini (2·AUC − 1), on each monthly cohort of loans to monitor model degradation. A drop triggers investigation into data drift before calibration problems show up in loss rates. Because AUC ignores calibration, they also monitor predicted versus observed default rates separately."},"prcurve":{"n":"Precision–Recall Curve","d":"The precision–recall curve plots precision against recall for every possible decision threshold of a binary classifier. Lowering the threshold raises recall, usually at the cost of precision, so the curve shows exactly how many false alarms must be accepted to catch a given share of positives.\n\nUnlike the ROC curve, the PR curve ignores true negatives, which makes it much more informative when positives are rare and negatives are plentiful, as in fraud, rare disease or information retrieval. The baseline for a random classifier is a horizontal line at the positive rate, not a diagonal, so the curve's meaning depends on the class ratio. PR curves are not monotonic: precision can go up and down as the threshold changes, and the usual practice is to report interpolated precision, the maximum precision at any recall at least as large. Teams typically use the curve to choose an operating threshold that meets a minimum precision or recall requirement.","u":["Choosing thresholds for rare-event detectors","Evaluating information retrieval systems","Comparing models on highly imbalanced data"],"x":"A cybersecurity company detecting malicious logins plots precision–recall curves because attacks are a tiny fraction of traffic and the ROC curve looked misleadingly good. They choose the threshold where precision stays above the level their security analysts can handle. Curves are recomputed weekly, since the attack rate changes the baseline."},"prauc":{"n":"PR-AUC","d":"PR-AUC is the area under the precision–recall curve, summarizing how well a classifier finds positives without producing many false positives across all thresholds. It is most often computed as average precision (AP), the mean of precision values at each threshold weighted by the increase in recall, which avoids the optimistic bias of linear interpolation between curve points.\n\nIts key property is that the random-classifier baseline equals the positive rate. On a dataset with 1% positives, a PR-AUC of 0.3 can be an excellent result, while a ROC-AUC of 0.9 on the same data might hide very low precision. This makes PR-AUC the preferred summary metric for rare-event problems such as fraud, defect detection and retrieval, where mean average precision (mAP) over queries or classes is the standard. Because the baseline depends on prevalence, PR-AUC values cannot be compared across datasets with different positive rates. It also emphasizes the high-score region, so it is sensitive to the few top-ranked mistakes.","u":["Fraud and anomaly detection model selection","Mean average precision in object detection","Evaluating retrieval and ranking quality"],"x":"A payments company selects fraud models by average precision on a time-based holdout, since fraud is well under one percent of transactions. Reporting the positive rate next to AP lets stakeholders see how far the model is above random. ROC-AUC is still logged, but it rarely distinguished between candidate models."},"threshold":{"n":"Decision Threshold","d":"The decision threshold is the cutoff that turns a model's continuous score or probability into a class label: predict positive if the score is at least t. The default of 0.5 is only a convention and is rarely optimal.\n\nChanging the threshold moves the model along its ROC and precision–recall curves without retraining, trading false positives for false negatives. The right value depends on error costs and capacity. If probabilities are calibrated and a false negative costs C_FN while a false positive costs C_FP, the cost-minimizing rule is to predict positive when p ≥ C_FP / (C_FP + C_FN). Otherwise, teams choose t on validation data to hit a target recall, precision or daily alert volume. The threshold needs revisiting when class balance or the score distribution drifts. Resampling the training data shifts the scores and therefore the appropriate threshold.","u":["Matching alert volume to review team capacity","Cost-sensitive decisions in fraud and credit","Meeting a minimum recall for safety screening"],"x":"An e-commerce fraud team sets the decline threshold so that the expected cost of fraud losses plus lost legitimate sales is minimized, using calibrated probabilities. A lower second threshold sends borderline orders to manual review instead of declining them. Both thresholds are recomputed whenever the model is retrained, because the score distribution shifts."},"calibration":{"n":"Probability Calibration","d":"A classifier is well calibrated when its predicted probabilities match observed frequencies: among all cases predicted at 0.8, about 80% should actually be positive. Calibration is separate from ranking quality; a model can have an excellent AUC and still output probabilities that are systematically too high or too low.\n\nMany models are miscalibrated by default. Naive Bayes and boosted trees often push probabilities toward the extremes, random forests tend to avoid the extremes, and modern deep networks are frequently overconfident. It is checked with a reliability diagram, which compares mean prediction and observed rate per bin, and summarized by expected calibration error or Brier score. It is fixed after training with a held-out set: Platt scaling fits a logistic function to the scores, isotonic regression fits a flexible monotonic mapping but needs more data, and temperature scaling divides neural network logits by a single learned constant. Calibration matters whenever probabilities feed decisions, such as expected cost or pricing.","u":["Turning risk scores into reliable probabilities","Expected-value decisions in pricing and bidding","Temperature scaling for overconfident neural networks"],"x":"An online advertising platform uses predicted click probabilities directly in its bid calculation, so systematic overprediction would mean overpaying for every impression. After each retraining they fit isotonic calibration on a recent holdout and check that predicted and observed click rates match within each bucket. Calibration drift is monitored daily and triggers recalibration."},"logloss":{"n":"Log Loss (Cross-Entropy)","d":"Log loss, also called cross-entropy loss, measures how well predicted probabilities match the true labels through the average negative log of the probability assigned to the correct class. A confident correct prediction costs almost nothing, while a confident wrong prediction costs a lot: predicting 0.01 for an event that happens costs about 4.6 nats.\n\nIt is the standard training loss for logistic regression and neural network classifiers, because minimizing it is equivalent to maximizing the likelihood of the data, and it is a strictly proper scoring rule, meaning the expected loss is minimized only by reporting the true probabilities. As a metric it rewards both ranking and calibration. For language models, the average per-token cross-entropy is the quantity behind perplexity, which equals exp of the cross-entropy. The unbounded penalty is a practical pitfall: a single prediction of exactly 0 for an observed event gives infinite loss, so implementations clip probabilities, and a few mislabeled examples can dominate.","u":["Training loss for classifiers and language models","Evaluating probabilistic click-through predictions","Comparing models where calibration matters"],"x":"An ad-ranking team uses normalized log loss as its primary offline metric, because bids depend on accurate click probabilities and not just ranking. A model change that improves AUC but worsens log loss is investigated before launch. They also break the metric down by traffic segment to catch calibration problems hidden in the average."},"mse":{"n":"MSE / RMSE","d":"Mean squared error is the average of the squared differences between predictions and true values, and root mean squared error is its square root, which brings the metric back to the units of the target. Squaring penalizes large errors much more than small ones.\n\nMSE is the default loss for regression because it is smooth and convex for linear models, and the prediction that minimizes it is the conditional mean of the target. It suits cases where large errors are especially harmful or noise is roughly Gaussian. The same squaring makes it sensitive to outliers: a few extreme points can dominate both training and evaluation, in which case MAE or the Huber loss is more robust. RMSE is scale-dependent, so values cannot be compared across targets; R² or normalized RMSE helps there. Note that RMSE is always at least as large as MAE, and a large gap between them signals that a few big errors are present.","u":["Default regression training loss","Evaluating forecasts where large misses are costly","Comparing regression models on the same target"],"x":"An energy utility evaluates its hourly load forecasts with RMSE, because large forecast errors force expensive last-minute power purchases. They report RMSE next to MAE so operators can see whether errors come from many small misses or a few large ones. Peak hours are evaluated separately, since errors there cost the most."},"mae":{"n":"MAE","d":"Mean absolute error is the average of the absolute differences between predictions and true values. It is in the target's units and easy to explain: an MAE of 3 means predictions are off by 3 units on average.\n\nBecause errors grow linearly rather than quadratically, MAE is much less influenced by outliers than MSE, and the prediction that minimizes it is the conditional median rather than the mean. This suits skewed targets, such as delivery times, and settings where each unit of error costs the same. As a training loss it is harder to optimize, because its gradient has constant magnitude and is undefined at zero; the Huber loss, which is quadratic near zero and linear beyond a threshold, is a common compromise. MAE is scale-dependent, so it cannot be compared across targets; MAPE and scaled variants such as MASE address that, though MAPE fails when actual values are near zero.","u":["Forecast accuracy reporting for business stakeholders","Robust evaluation with outliers in the target","Median-oriented prediction of skewed quantities"],"x":"A food delivery platform reports the MAE of its estimated delivery times in minutes, because customers and operations teams understand it directly. They train with a Huber loss so that rare extreme delays, such as those caused by severe weather, do not distort typical predictions. MAE is tracked by city and time of day to spot local problems."},"r2":{"n":"R² (Coefficient of Determination)","d":"R² measures the fraction of the target's variance that a regression model explains, compared with always predicting the mean. It equals one minus the ratio of the residual sum of squares to the total sum of squares. A value of 1 means perfect predictions, 0 means the model does no better than the mean, and on held-out data the score can be negative when the model is worse than that constant baseline.\n\nR² is useful for comparing models on the same dataset because it is unitless. It is not comparable across datasets with different target variance, and a high value does not prove the model is correct or unbiased. On training data, R² never decreases when you add features, which is why adjusted R² exists for linear models. A common misconception is that R² is the square of a correlation for every model; that identity only holds for least-squares linear regression with an intercept, evaluated on training data.","u":["Reporting fit quality of regression models","Comparing candidate regressors on one validation set","Sanity-checking a model against the mean baseline"],"x":"A real-estate analytics team reports R² on a time-held-out set of home sales alongside MAE in dollars. R² tells stakeholders how much price variation the model captures, while MAE tells them the typical error size. They track both in their monitoring dashboard because R² can drop simply when the price range in a new market narrows."},"mdi":{"n":"Mean Decrease in Impurity (MDI)","d":"Mean Decrease in Impurity scores a feature by how much it reduces node impurity, summed over every split that uses it, weighted by the fraction of samples reaching that node, and averaged across the trees of an ensemble. It is the default feature_importances_ attribute in scikit-learn's tree models and comes for free after training.\n\nBecause it is computed from the training data during fitting, MDI is fast and needs no extra pass. That convenience has costs. It is biased toward features with many possible split points, such as continuous variables or high-cardinality categories, and it can assign importance to pure noise features the trees used to overfit. When two features are correlated, the credit is split between them in an arbitrary way. MDI also says nothing about the direction of an effect. Use it for a quick first look, and confirm important conclusions with permutation importance on held-out data or with SHAP values.","u":["Quick feature ranking after training a forest","Pruning obviously useless columns before retraining","Debugging which inputs a tree model relies on"],"x":"A fraud team training a random forest on transaction features inspects MDI after each retrain to spot sudden shifts in which inputs dominate. When a high-cardinality merchant ID jumped to the top, they checked permutation importance on a validation set and found it was mostly overfitting, so they replaced it with target-encoded merchant risk."},"permimp":{"n":"Permutation Importance","d":"Permutation importance measures how much a model's score drops when the values of one feature are randomly shuffled, breaking that feature's relationship with the target while keeping its distribution. A large drop means the model depends on the feature for its predictions.\n\nThe method is model-agnostic: it only needs a fitted model, a dataset and a metric. Computing it on a held-out set reveals what actually helps generalization, avoiding the training-set bias of impurity-based scores. Repeating the shuffle several times gives a mean and a spread. The main pitfall is correlated features. When two columns carry the same information, shuffling one leaves the other intact, so both can look unimportant even though the shared signal matters. Shuffling also creates unrealistic rows, such as a 2-meter-tall child, which forces the model to extrapolate. Grouping correlated features and permuting them together, or clustering features first, reduces these problems.","u":["Model-agnostic feature ranking on validation data","Detecting leakage features the model over-relies on","Comparing feature reliance across model families"],"x":"A credit-risk team computes permutation importance on an out-of-time validation month for every candidate model before approval. Running it on held-out data exposed an application-timestamp field that only helped on training data, and grouping the correlated income fields into one permutation block gave regulators a clearer picture of what drove decisions."},"shap":{"n":"SHAP (Shapley Values)","d":"SHAP explains an individual prediction by assigning each feature a share of the difference between that prediction and the model's average output, using Shapley values from cooperative game theory. The shares add up exactly to the prediction minus the baseline.\n\nA feature's Shapley value is its average marginal contribution over all possible orders in which features could be added to the input. Features absent from a coalition are replaced by values drawn from a background dataset. SHAP is the only additive attribution satisfying efficiency, symmetry, dummy and additivity, which makes it a principled choice for both local explanations and global summaries such as mean absolute SHAP per feature. Exact computation is exponential in the number of features, so practical tools use model-specific algorithms or sampling approximations. Results depend on the background data, and with correlated features the default interventional approach can evaluate the model on unrealistic inputs. SHAP explains the model, not causal effects in the real world.","u":["Explaining individual loan or claim decisions","Global feature importance with direction of effect","Debugging unexpected predictions in production models"],"x":"An insurance company attaches the top three SHAP contributions to each claim flagged for manual review, so adjusters see why the model scored it as risky. They precompute explanations in the batch scoring job to avoid adding latency, and they monitor the distribution of SHAP values per feature to detect drift in model behavior."},"treeshap":{"n":"TreeSHAP","d":"TreeSHAP is an algorithm that computes exact Shapley values for tree ensembles in polynomial time by exploiting the tree structure instead of enumerating every feature subset. Generic SHAP estimation is exponential in the number of features; TreeSHAP runs in O(T·L·D²) for T trees with L leaves and depth D.\n\nThe algorithm pushes all feature subsets down each tree at once, tracking what proportion of them follow each path. Two variants exist. The path-dependent version uses the training cover counts stored in the nodes and needs no background data, while the interventional version averages over a background dataset and better matches the causal-style interventional definition. This speed makes it practical to explain millions of predictions from XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost or scikit-learn forests, and to compute SHAP interaction values. A known caveat is that the path-dependent variant can give nonzero credit to features the model does not actually use when features are correlated.","u":["Explaining every prediction from gradient-boosted trees","Computing SHAP interaction values between feature pairs","Fast global importance for large tabular models"],"x":"A marketing team scoring tens of millions of customers nightly with LightGBM uses TreeSHAP to store per-customer reason codes in the feature store. Exact tree-based computation finishes in the same batch window as scoring, which a sampling-based explainer could not do at that scale."},"pdp":{"n":"Partial Dependence Plot (PDP)","d":"A partial dependence plot shows how a model's average prediction changes as one or two features are varied across a grid while all other features keep their observed values. Each grid point is the mean prediction over the whole dataset with the chosen feature forced to that value.\n\nPDPs give a global, model-agnostic view of the shape of an effect: monotonic, threshold-like, saturating or U-shaped. Two-feature PDPs reveal interactions as non-parallel contours. They are useful for checking that a model has learned plausible relationships, such as risk rising with debt. The method assumes the varied feature is roughly independent of the others. When features are correlated, the grid creates combinations that never occur in the data, and the curve reflects extrapolation. Averaging can also hide heterogeneity: if half the samples go up and half go down, the PDP looks flat. ICE curves or accumulated local effects (ALE) plots address these two weaknesses.","u":["Validating the direction of feature effects","Visualizing two-way feature interactions","Communicating model behavior to non-technical reviewers"],"x":"A utility company builds partial dependence plots of predicted electricity demand against temperature for each regional model. Reviewers check for the expected U-shape driven by heating and cooling, and a region whose curve flattened at high temperatures revealed missing air-conditioning load data in training."},"ice":{"n":"ICE Plot (Individual Conditional Expectation)","d":"An Individual Conditional Expectation plot draws one curve per data point, showing how that point's prediction changes as a single feature is varied while its other features stay fixed. The partial dependence plot is exactly the average of these curves.\n\nICE plots expose heterogeneity that averaging hides. If curves are roughly parallel, the feature acts additively; if they cross or fan out, the feature interacts with others. Centered ICE (c-ICE) subtracts each curve's value at a reference point so differences in shape stand out from differences in level. Coloring curves by another feature helps identify which variable drives the interaction. With many samples the plot becomes cluttered, so a random subset of a few hundred curves is typical. ICE shares the main weakness of PDPs: when the varied feature is correlated with others, many grid points describe unrealistic inputs, and the curves partly show extrapolation rather than behavior on real data.","u":["Detecting interactions hidden by partial dependence","Inspecting how specific customers respond to a feature","Checking monotonicity at the individual level"],"x":"A pricing team at an online retailer plots centered ICE curves of predicted conversion against discount level. The curves showed that loyal customers barely respond to discounts while new visitors respond strongly, an interaction the flat-looking PDP concealed, which led to segment-specific promotion rules."},"lime":{"n":"LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations)","d":"LIME explains a single prediction by fitting a simple, interpretable model, usually a sparse linear model, to the black-box model's behavior in a small neighborhood around that input. The surrogate's coefficients serve as the explanation.\n\nThe procedure perturbs the instance many times (for tabular data by sampling feature values, for text by removing words, for images by hiding superpixels), queries the black-box model on each perturbation, weights samples by their proximity to the original, and fits a weighted linear regression. It works with any model that returns predictions and produces explanations in human terms such as words or image regions. Its main weakness is instability: different random seeds, kernel widths or perturbation schemes can produce noticeably different explanations for the same input. Perturbed samples may also fall off the data manifold. Unlike SHAP, LIME offers no guarantee that attributions sum to the prediction, so check explanation stability before relying on it.","u":["Explaining text classifier decisions word by word","Highlighting image regions behind a prediction","Ad hoc debugging of any black-box model"],"x":"A customer-support team uses LIME on its ticket-routing text classifier to show agents which words triggered a routing to the billing queue. Because explanations varied between runs, they fixed the random seed, raised the number of samples, and only showed words that appeared consistently across five repeated runs."},"counterfactual":{"n":"Counterfactual Explanations","d":"A counterfactual explanation describes the smallest realistic change to an input that would flip the model's decision, for example \"the loan would be approved if annual income were 4,000 higher and the credit card balance 1,500 lower.\" It answers what would need to be different, rather than why the current output occurred.\n\nGenerating one is an optimization problem: find a point close to the original that receives the desired prediction. Good counterfactuals are sparse (few features change), plausible (they lie near the data distribution), actionable (they avoid immutable features like age or birthplace) and diverse when several options are offered. Methods such as DiCE produce multiple counterfactuals with constraints on which features may vary. They are popular for adverse-action notices and user-facing recourse. A key pitfall is that a counterfactual describes the model, not the world: following the advice may not change the real outcome, and if the model is retrained the same change may no longer flip the decision.","u":["Recourse suggestions for rejected loan applicants","Explaining churn predictions with actionable levers","Auditing models for reliance on immutable attributes"],"x":"A consumer lender generates counterfactuals for declined applications, restricting changes to features the applicant can control, such as debt balance and requested amount. The team offers two or three diverse options and re-checks them against the latest model version before sending, since a retrained model may no longer honor earlier advice."},"kmeans":{"n":"k-Means Clustering","d":"k-Means partitions data into k clusters by repeatedly assigning each point to its nearest centroid and then moving each centroid to the mean of its assigned points. It minimizes the within-cluster sum of squared distances, also called inertia.\n\nThe algorithm (Lloyd's algorithm) always converges, but only to a local minimum, so the starting centroids matter. k-means++ initialization spreads initial centroids apart and multiple restarts keep the best result. k-Means is fast, scales to millions of points with mini-batch variants, and works well for compact, roughly spherical clusters of similar size. It is common for customer segmentation, vector quantization and building codebooks. Its assumptions are also its limits: it uses Euclidean distance, so features must be scaled; it cannot find elongated or nested shapes; and it forces every point, including outliers, into a cluster. Choosing k needs external judgment, aided by the elbow method or silhouette score, which give hints rather than definitive answers.","u":["Customer segmentation from behavioral features","Color quantization and image compression","Building codebooks for vector search indexes"],"x":"A subscription streaming service clusters users by standardized viewing-time features into a handful of segments with k-means, then gives each segment a different home-page layout. They retrain monthly, match new centroids to old ones to keep segment labels stable, and use MiniBatchKMeans because the user base has tens of millions of rows."},"hierclust":{"n":"Hierarchical Clustering","d":"Hierarchical clustering builds a tree of nested clusters, called a dendrogram, instead of a single flat partition. The common agglomerative version starts with every point as its own cluster and repeatedly merges the two closest clusters until one remains.\n\nThe linkage criterion defines the distance between clusters. Single linkage uses the closest pair of points and can follow long chains; complete linkage uses the farthest pair and favors compact groups; average linkage uses the mean pairwise distance; Ward linkage merges the pair that least increases total within-cluster variance and behaves like a hierarchical k-means. Cutting the dendrogram at a chosen height or number of clusters yields a flat clustering, and the tree itself shows structure at several scales. It suits small to medium datasets, taxonomy building and exploratory analysis. Standard implementations need O(n²) memory for the distance matrix, which limits them to tens of thousands of points, and early merges can never be undone.","u":["Building product or document taxonomies","Clustering gene expression profiles in biology","Exploring structure at multiple granularities"],"x":"An e-commerce catalog team clusters product embeddings within each category using Ward linkage to propose subcategory trees. Merchandisers inspect the dendrogram and choose the cut height per category, which a flat method like k-means could not offer, and the O(n²) cost is acceptable because each category holds only a few thousand items."},"dbscan":{"n":"DBSCAN","d":"DBSCAN (Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise) groups points that lie in dense regions and labels points in sparse regions as noise. It needs no preset number of clusters and can discover clusters of arbitrary shape.\n\nTwo parameters define density: eps, the neighborhood radius, and min_samples, the number of points required within eps for a point to be a core point. Clusters grow by connecting core points that are within eps of each other; border points join a cluster without extending it, and everything else is noise, labeled −1. This makes DBSCAN useful for spatial data, anomaly-aware clustering and shapes such as rings that k-means cannot separate. Its main limitation is a single global density threshold: when clusters have very different densities, no eps works for all of them. Results are sensitive to eps and degrade in high dimensions where distances concentrate. A k-distance plot helps choose eps, and HDBSCAN removes the need for a fixed eps.","u":["Clustering GPS points into places or stops","Separating noise from dense groups in sensor data","Finding irregularly shaped clusters in embeddings"],"x":"A delivery company runs DBSCAN with haversine distance on drivers' GPS drop-off points to find actual building entrances, which often differ from geocoded addresses. Isolated points become noise and are ignored, and the team tunes eps in meters per city because urban and suburban densities differ."},"gmm":{"n":"Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM)","d":"A Gaussian Mixture Model represents the data distribution as a weighted sum of several Gaussian components, each with its own mean and covariance. Each point gets a probability of belonging to every component, which is called soft clustering.\n\nParameters are fitted by the Expectation-Maximization algorithm, alternating between computing each point's component responsibilities and re-estimating weights, means and covariances from those responsibilities. Full covariance matrices let clusters be elongated and tilted ellipses, which k-means cannot represent; k-means is roughly a GMM with equal, spherical, shared variance and hard assignments. Because it is a density model, a GMM also gives log-likelihoods for anomaly detection and allows sampling new points. The number of components can be chosen with BIC or AIC. Pitfalls include sensitivity to initialization, convergence to local optima, and singular covariances when a component collapses onto a few points, which regularization (reg_covar) prevents. Full covariances also need many parameters in high dimensions.","u":["Soft customer segmentation with membership probabilities","Density-based anomaly scoring of sensor readings","Speaker modeling in classic speech systems"],"x":"A manufacturing team fits a GMM to multivariate vibration and temperature readings from healthy machines and flags readings whose log-likelihood falls below a low percentile of training scores. Full covariance captures the correlation between vibration and temperature, and BIC selected the number of operating modes automatically."},"em":{"n":"EM Algorithm (Expectation-Maximization)","d":"The Expectation-Maximization algorithm finds maximum-likelihood parameters for models with hidden variables by alternating two steps until the likelihood stops improving. It is the standard fitting method for Gaussian mixtures, hidden Markov models and many missing-data problems.\n\nIn the E-step, the algorithm uses the current parameters to compute the posterior distribution over the hidden variables, for example each point's probability of belonging to each cluster. In the M-step, it maximizes the expected complete-data log-likelihood under that posterior, which often has a closed-form solution such as a weighted mean. Each iteration is guaranteed not to decrease the observed-data likelihood, because EM maximizes a lower bound (the ELBO) that touches the true likelihood at the current parameters. The guarantee is only for monotone improvement, not for the global optimum: EM converges to a local maximum or saddle point, so multiple random restarts are standard. Convergence can be slow when hidden variables carry much information.","u":["Fitting Gaussian mixture models","Training hidden Markov models (Baum-Welch)","Estimating parameters with missing data"],"x":"A genomics lab uses EM to estimate allele frequencies when individual genotypes are only partly observed in low-coverage sequencing. The E-step computes genotype probabilities per sample and the M-step updates population frequencies, with several random restarts because the likelihood surface has multiple peaks."},"pca":{"n":"PCA (Principal Component Analysis)","d":"Principal Component Analysis finds a new set of orthogonal axes, the principal components, ordered by how much variance of the data they capture. Projecting onto the first few components gives a lower-dimensional representation that keeps as much variance as possible.\n\nAfter centering the data, the components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, or equivalently the right singular vectors from an SVD of the data matrix; the eigenvalues give the variance explained by each. PC1 minimizes the sum of squared orthogonal distances from points to the line, which is why it differs from a regression line that minimizes vertical errors. PCA is used for compression, denoising, visualization, decorrelating inputs and speeding up downstream models. Standardize features first when they have different units, or variables with large scales will dominate. PCA is linear and unsupervised: it captures only linear structure, and the directions of highest variance are not guaranteed to be the most useful for predicting a target.","u":["Compressing high-dimensional features before modeling","Visualizing data in two or three dimensions","Denoising signals by dropping low-variance components"],"x":"A semiconductor fab applies PCA to hundreds of correlated sensor readings per wafer and monitors the leading component scores plus the reconstruction error. A rise in reconstruction error signals a new kind of process deviation, and the small number of components makes control charts manageable for engineers."},"kpca":{"n":"Kernel PCA","d":"Kernel PCA performs principal component analysis in an implicit high-dimensional feature space defined by a kernel function, which lets it capture nonlinear structure that linear PCA misses. It never computes the feature mapping directly, only inner products through the kernel.\n\nThe method builds the n×n kernel matrix, centers it in feature space, and takes its top eigenvectors. Projections of new points are kernel-weighted combinations of these eigenvectors. With an RBF kernel, concentric circles or curved manifolds can become linearly separable in the first components. It is useful as a nonlinear preprocessing step before a linear classifier and for denoising when an approximate inverse map is learned. Because the kernel matrix is n×n, memory and time grow quadratically and cubically with the number of samples, so it is limited to tens of thousands of points unless approximations such as Nyström are used. Results depend strongly on the kernel and its bandwidth, and the components have no direct interpretation in input features.","u":["Nonlinear feature extraction before linear classifiers","Unfolding curved manifolds for visualization","Image denoising with pre-image reconstruction"],"x":"A spectroscopy team classifying material samples applies kernel PCA with an RBF kernel to a few thousand spectra, then trains a logistic regression on the top components. The nonlinear projection separated two material grades that overlapped under linear PCA, and the small dataset kept the quadratic kernel cost acceptable."},"svd":{"n":"Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)","d":"Singular Value Decomposition factors any real matrix A into UΣVᵀ, where U and V have orthonormal columns and Σ is a diagonal matrix of non-negative singular values sorted from largest to smallest. Every matrix has an SVD, including rectangular and rank-deficient ones.\n\nGeometrically, A rotates the input (Vᵀ), scales each axis (Σ) and rotates again (U). Keeping only the top k singular values gives the best rank-k approximation in both Frobenius and spectral norm, a result known as the Eckart–Young theorem. This makes SVD the basis of PCA, latent semantic analysis, low-rank compression, matrix completion for recommendations, pseudo-inverses and least-squares solvers. Randomized and truncated SVD scale it to large sparse matrices such as TF-IDF. A practical caution is that singular vectors are only defined up to sign, and up to rotation when singular values are equal, so comparing components across runs requires alignment. Full SVD of a large dense matrix is costly, so truncated methods are preferred.","u":["Latent semantic analysis on TF-IDF matrices","Low-rank compression of weights or images","Collaborative filtering via matrix factorization"],"x":"A document search team applies truncated SVD to a large sparse TF-IDF matrix to obtain a few hundred dense topic dimensions for each document. The randomized solver handles the sparse input without densifying it, and the resulting vectors feed a nearest-neighbor index for related-article suggestions."},"tsne":{"n":"t-SNE","d":"t-SNE (t-distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding) is a nonlinear method that maps high-dimensional data to two or three dimensions for visualization, aiming to keep similar points close together. It focuses on preserving local neighborhoods rather than global distances.\n\nThe algorithm converts pairwise distances into neighbor probabilities with a Gaussian kernel whose width is set per point by the perplexity parameter, roughly the effective number of neighbors. In the low-dimensional map it uses a heavy-tailed Student-t distribution, which lets dissimilar points spread apart and reduces crowding. It then minimizes the KL divergence between the two distributions with gradient descent. t-SNE produces clear cluster pictures of embeddings, single-cell data and image features. Its output is easy to misread: cluster sizes and the distances between clusters are not meaningful, results change with perplexity and random seed, and it has no transform for new points. Use it for exploration, not as features for downstream models.","u":["Visualizing learned embeddings from neural networks","Exploring cell types in single-cell RNA data","Inspecting class separation in feature spaces"],"x":"A computer-vision team plots t-SNE maps of image embeddings from their classifier's penultimate layer to spot mislabeled examples and confused classes. They run several perplexity values and seeds before drawing conclusions, and they explicitly avoid interpreting distances between clusters in their review notes."},"umap":{"n":"UMAP","d":"UMAP (Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection) is a nonlinear dimensionality reduction method that builds a neighbor graph in the original space and then finds a low-dimensional layout whose graph is as similar as possible. It is used both for visualization and as a general-purpose reduction step.\n\nUMAP first connects each point to its k nearest neighbors with edge weights that decay with distance relative to the local scale, then symmetrizes this fuzzy graph. It optimizes a cross-entropy objective with stochastic gradient descent and negative sampling, pulling connected points together and pushing random pairs apart. Compared with t-SNE it is usually faster, scales to millions of points, tends to keep more global structure, and supports transform() for new data. n_neighbors trades local detail against global structure, and min_dist controls how tightly points pack. As with t-SNE, cluster sizes and inter-cluster gaps in the plot should not be read as true densities or distances, and results vary with hyperparameters and seeds.","u":["Visualizing large embedding collections in 2D","Reducing dimensions before density-based clustering","Projecting new samples into an existing map"],"x":"A research team maps millions of single-cell profiles with UMAP and then runs HDBSCAN on a 10-dimensional UMAP output to propose cell populations. Because UMAP supports transform, new experimental batches are projected into the reference map, and biologists confirm populations with marker genes rather than trusting the layout alone."},"autoencoder":{"n":"Autoencoder","d":"An autoencoder is a neural network trained to reconstruct its input after passing it through a narrower intermediate representation, called the bottleneck or latent code. The encoder compresses the input and the decoder tries to rebuild it, so the latent code must keep the most informative structure.\n\nTraining minimizes a reconstruction loss such as mean squared error or cross-entropy, and needs no labels. With linear layers and squared error, an autoencoder learns the same subspace as PCA; nonlinear layers let it capture curved manifolds. Variants change the constraint: denoising autoencoders reconstruct clean inputs from corrupted ones, sparse autoencoders penalize active units, and variational autoencoders impose a probabilistic latent space suitable for generation. Common uses are compression, denoising and anomaly detection, where a high reconstruction error flags unusual inputs. A frequent pitfall is that a powerful autoencoder can also reconstruct anomalies well, so the bottleneck size and capacity must be tuned on validation data that contains known anomalies.","u":["Reconstruction-based anomaly detection on sensor data","Learning compact embeddings without labels","Denoising images or signals"],"x":"A payments company trains an autoencoder on normal merchant transaction patterns and routes transactions with reconstruction error above a validated threshold to review. The model scores in a few milliseconds on CPU, and the threshold is recalibrated weekly because legitimate spending patterns shift seasonally."},"isoforest":{"n":"Isolation Forest","d":"Isolation Forest detects anomalies by measuring how easily a point can be separated from the rest of the data with random splits. Anomalies are few and different, so they tend to be isolated after only a few splits, while normal points sit deep in the trees.\n\nEach tree is grown on a small random subsample, commonly 256 points, by repeatedly choosing a random feature and a random threshold between its minimum and maximum until points are isolated. The average path length across trees is normalized into an anomaly score between 0 and 1, where values near 1 indicate likely anomalies. The method needs no distance computations, runs in linear time, handles high-dimensional tabular data well, and requires no labels. Its main limitation is that axis-parallel random splits struggle with anomalies that are only unusual in a combination of features, and the contamination parameter only sets the decision threshold, so it must reflect a realistic anomaly rate rather than a guess.","u":["Unsupervised fraud screening on transaction features","Detecting faulty readings in IoT sensor streams","Flagging unusual server metrics for investigation"],"x":"A cloud provider runs Isolation Forest on per-host resource metrics such as CPU, memory, disk latency and network errors to surface unusual machines for on-call engineers. Training on small subsamples keeps retraining cheap every hour, and the team sets the alert threshold from score percentiles reviewed against past incidents instead of relying on the contamination default."},"lof":{"n":"Local Outlier Factor (LOF)","d":"Local Outlier Factor scores each point by comparing its local density with the local densities of its nearest neighbors. A point in a sparser region than its neighbors gets a score above 1 and is considered an outlier.\n\nFor each point, LOF computes the reachability distance to its k neighbors, takes the inverse of the average to obtain local reachability density, and divides the neighbors' average density by the point's own density. Because the comparison is relative, LOF can find outliers near a dense cluster even when a sparse cluster elsewhere contains points that are farther away, which a single global distance threshold would miss. It works well on low- to moderate-dimensional data with clusters of varying density. Drawbacks include quadratic cost without an index, sensitivity to the choice of k, and weaker performance in high dimensions where distances become less informative. In scikit-learn, scoring unseen data requires novelty=True, and then fit_predict on the training set is not available.","u":["Finding local anomalies in clusters of varying density","Detecting unusual network flows in security logs","Cleaning outliers from training datasets"],"x":"A network security team applies LOF to flow-level features from each office network, where normal traffic density differs greatly between sites. A relative density score catches hosts that behave unusually for their own site, and the team uses novelty mode to score new flows against a reference window refreshed daily."},"spectral":{"n":"Spectral Clustering","d":"Spectral clustering groups points by building a similarity graph, computing eigenvectors of its graph Laplacian, and clustering the points in that eigenvector space. It finds clusters defined by connectivity rather than by distance to a center.\n\nThe pipeline starts with an affinity matrix, such as an RBF kernel or a k-nearest-neighbor graph. The leading eigenvectors of the normalized Laplacian embed each point so that well-connected groups map to compact regions, and k-means then separates them. The procedure approximately solves the normalized cut problem, which minimizes connections between clusters relative to their size. It handles non-convex shapes such as interlocking moons and suits graph data like social networks. Cost is the main limitation: the affinity matrix and eigendecomposition scale poorly beyond tens of thousands of points unless sparse graphs and approximate solvers are used. Results are sensitive to the affinity bandwidth or neighbor count, and the number of clusters must still be chosen, often with the eigengap heuristic.","u":["Clustering non-convex shapes in low dimensions","Community detection in social or citation graphs","Image segmentation from pixel similarity graphs"],"x":"A telecom analytics team applies spectral clustering to a call graph between business accounts to find groups of companies that communicate mostly with each other. The precomputed sparse affinity matrix keeps memory manageable, and the eigengap of the Laplacian guided the choice of cluster count."},"stationarity":{"n":"Stationarity","d":"A time series is stationary when its statistical properties, such as mean, variance and autocorrelation, do not change over time. Most classical forecasting models, including ARMA, assume stationarity, because patterns learned from the past are then valid for the future.\n\nWeak (covariance) stationarity requires a constant mean, constant variance, and an autocovariance that depends only on the lag. Trends, seasonality and changing volatility all break it. The usual fixes are differencing to remove trends or unit roots, seasonal differencing, and log or Box-Cox transforms to stabilize variance. The Augmented Dickey-Fuller test has a unit root as its null hypothesis, while the KPSS test has stationarity as its null, so running both gives a more reliable verdict. A common pitfall is over-differencing, which adds artificial negative autocorrelation and inflates forecast variance. Also, failing to reject the ADF null does not prove a unit root, especially in short series where the test has low power.","u":["Preparing series for ARIMA-family models","Testing for unit roots in economic data","Choosing transformations before feature engineering"],"x":"A retail demand-planning team runs ADF and KPSS tests on each store-product series before automatic ARIMA fitting. Series with a trend get one regular difference and strongly seasonal ones get a seasonal difference, and they log-transform sales first because variance grows with volume."},"acf":{"n":"Autocorrelation (ACF / PACF)","d":"The autocorrelation function (ACF) measures the correlation between a time series and lagged copies of itself at each lag. The partial autocorrelation function (PACF) measures the correlation at lag k after removing the effects of all shorter lags.\n\nTogether they are the classic tools for identifying ARIMA orders. For a pure AR(p) process, the PACF cuts off after lag p while the ACF decays gradually; for a pure MA(q) process, the ACF cuts off after lag q while the PACF decays. Spikes at multiples of the seasonal period reveal seasonality. The ACF of model residuals is also a key diagnostic, since a good model leaves residuals with no significant autocorrelation, which the Ljung-Box test checks formally. Plots usually show approximate 95% confidence bands. With many lags, a few bars will exceed the bands by chance, and on a non-stationary series the ACF decays very slowly, which hides the structure, so make the series stationary before reading orders from the plots.","u":["Choosing AR and MA orders for ARIMA","Detecting seasonality periods in a series","Checking model residuals for leftover structure"],"x":"A call-center workforce team examines the ACF of half-hourly call volumes and finds strong spikes at daily and weekly lags, which tells them to include both seasonal periods in their model. After fitting, they run a Ljung-Box test on residuals as an automated quality gate in the forecasting pipeline."},"decomp":{"n":"Seasonal Decomposition","d":"Seasonal decomposition splits a time series into a trend component, a repeating seasonal component and a remainder. It makes underlying patterns visible and is often a preprocessing step before modeling or anomaly detection.\n\nAn additive decomposition, y = trend + seasonal + remainder, fits series whose seasonal swings have a constant size; a multiplicative one, y = trend × seasonal × remainder, fits swings that grow with the level, and is equivalent to additive decomposition after a log transform. Classical decomposition estimates the trend with a centered moving average and averages the detrended values for each season. STL (Seasonal-Trend decomposition using Loess) is more flexible: it allows the seasonal pattern to evolve, handles any period, and has a robust option that downweights outliers. Decomposition is descriptive. It is not a forecast by itself, the classical method loses values at the series ends, and choosing the wrong period or the wrong additive or multiplicative form leaves seasonality in the remainder.","u":["Deseasonalizing data before trend analysis","Anomaly detection on the remainder component","Communicating seasonal patterns to business teams"],"x":"A web analytics team runs robust STL on hourly traffic with a weekly period and alerts when the remainder exceeds several robust standard deviations. Removing the expected daily and weekly cycles cut false alarms on Monday mornings, and the robust fit keeps a past outage from distorting the seasonal profile."},"arima":{"n":"ARIMA","d":"ARIMA (AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average) forecasts a time series by modeling each value as a linear combination of its own past values and past forecast errors, after differencing the series to make it stationary. The model is written ARIMA(p, d, q).\n\nThe AR part uses p lagged values, the I part applies d rounds of differencing, and the MA part uses q lagged errors. Orders are chosen from ACF and PACF plots or by minimizing an information criterion such as AIC, often through automatic search. Parameters are estimated by maximum likelihood, and the model gives forecast intervals as well as point forecasts. ARIMA works well for single, fairly short univariate series with stable autocorrelation, and it is a strong baseline. Limitations include linearity, a single seasonal pattern only through the SARIMA extension, and per-series fitting that becomes expensive across thousands of series. Forecast intervals assume normal, homoscedastic errors and widen quickly for integrated series as the horizon grows.","u":["Short-term forecasting of a single business metric","Baseline model for forecasting benchmarks","Forecasting macroeconomic indicators with intervals"],"x":"A hospital operations team forecasts daily emergency department admissions with ARIMA models selected by AIC, refit each night on the most recent two years. The forecast intervals inform minimum staffing levels, and residual diagnostics are logged to catch the model drifting after policy changes."},"sarima":{"n":"SARIMA","d":"SARIMA extends ARIMA with seasonal autoregressive, differencing and moving-average terms that operate at a fixed seasonal lag, such as 12 for monthly data with a yearly cycle. It is written ARIMA(p, d, q)(P, D, Q)ₛ.\n\nThe seasonal part multiplies the non-seasonal polynomials, so the model can capture both short-range dependence and the relation between a value and the same season in previous cycles. Seasonal differencing (D = 1) subtracts the value from one period earlier, removing a stable seasonal pattern. With exogenous regressors, the model is called SARIMAX and can include holidays or prices. SARIMA is a well-understood choice for monthly or quarterly business data with one clear seasonality. It struggles with long seasonal periods, for example 365 for daily data or 168 for hourly data, because estimation becomes slow and unstable; Fourier terms as exogenous regressors or TBATS-style models handle those better. It also supports only a single seasonal period directly.","u":["Monthly sales forecasting with yearly seasonality","Quarterly economic series with seasonal patterns","Seasonal demand forecasting with holiday regressors"],"x":"A beverage distributor forecasts monthly shipments per region with SARIMAX(1,1,1)(1,1,1)₁₂ and a regressor for the number of public holidays in each month. The seasonal structure captures the summer peak, and planners use the 80% interval to set safety stock rather than relying on the point forecast alone."},"prophet":{"n":"Prophet","d":"Prophet is an open-source forecasting library from Meta that fits an additive model with a piecewise trend, Fourier-based seasonalities and holiday effects. It is designed for business time series with strong seasonal patterns and analysts who want reasonable forecasts with little tuning.\n\nThe trend is piecewise linear or logistic with automatically placed changepoints whose flexibility is controlled by a prior scale. Yearly, weekly and daily seasonalities are modeled with Fourier series, and holidays or custom events enter as extra regressors. Fitting is done with Stan, and the output includes uncertainty intervals. Prophet tolerates missing data and irregular timestamps and produces interpretable component plots. It is a curve-fitting approach, not an autoregressive one, so it ignores recent residual dynamics and often loses to simple baselines or gradient boosting on series where short-term autocorrelation matters. Trend extrapolation can also drift unrealistically over long horizons, so changepoint settings need validation with time-based cross-validation.","u":["Daily business metric forecasting with holidays","Capacity planning for seasonal web traffic","Quick forecasts for many series by analysts"],"x":"A food delivery company forecasts daily orders per city with Prophet, adding regional holidays and a regressor for major sporting events. Analysts inspect component plots to explain changes to operations managers, and they compare Prophet against a seasonal naive baseline in rolling cross-validation before each quarterly planning cycle."},"holtwinters":{"n":"Exponential Smoothing (Holt-Winters)","d":"Exponential smoothing forecasts a series from weighted averages of past observations, with weights that decrease exponentially for older data. Holt-Winters extends simple smoothing with a trend component and a seasonal component, each updated by its own smoothing parameter.\n\nThree equations update the level (α), trend (β) and seasonal index (γ) at every step, and forecasts combine the latest level, trend and the matching seasonal index. The seasonal part can be additive, for constant-size swings, or multiplicative, for swings proportional to the level. A damped trend flattens long-horizon forecasts and is often more accurate than an undamped linear trend. Formulated as state-space ETS models, these methods give likelihoods, AIC-based selection and prediction intervals. Holt-Winters is fast, robust and a strong baseline for many demand and operations series. It handles only one seasonal period, cannot use external regressors in the standard form, and multiplicative versions fail when the series contains zeros or negative values.","u":["Inventory demand forecasting for many products","Short-term forecasting of seasonal utility loads","Fast baseline for forecasting competitions"],"x":"A grocery chain forecasts weekly demand for thousands of products with automatically selected ETS models, choosing additive or multiplicative seasonality per item by AIC. The models fit in seconds per series, which allows a full nightly refresh, and a damped trend prevents runaway forecasts for promoted items."},"tssplit":{"n":"Time-Based Validation","d":"Time-based validation evaluates a forecasting or temporal model by always training on earlier data and testing on later data, mirroring how the model will be used in production. Random K-fold splits leak future information into training and give overly optimistic scores.\n\nThe usual scheme is rolling-origin or expanding-window evaluation: several folds, each with a training period that ends at a cutoff and a test period that follows it. A sliding window keeps the training length fixed, which is useful when old data is no longer representative. A gap between training and test periods should match the real delay between data availability and prediction, and features such as rolling means must be computed only from data available at each cutoff. Averaging errors across folds gives a more stable estimate than a single split. A common mistake is fitting scalers, encoders or feature selection on the full dataset before splitting, which leaks future statistics into every fold.","u":["Honest evaluation of demand forecasting models","Tuning hyperparameters for churn or fraud models","Backtesting trading or pricing strategies"],"x":"A fraud detection team evaluates each candidate model on the four most recent months as separate folds, training only on earlier transactions with a two-week gap to account for delayed chargeback labels. Random splits had overstated performance because the same fraud rings appeared in both training and test data."},"multistep":{"n":"Multi-Step Forecasting","d":"Multi-step forecasting predicts several future time steps at once, such as the next 24 hours or the next 12 weeks, rather than only the next value. The choice of strategy affects accuracy, cost and how errors accumulate over the horizon.\n\nThe recursive strategy trains a one-step model and feeds its own predictions back as inputs, which is simple but lets errors compound. The direct strategy trains a separate model for each horizon step, avoiding feedback errors but multiplying training cost and ignoring dependence between steps. The multi-output (MIMO) strategy predicts the whole horizon vector with one model, as many neural forecasters do. Hybrid strategies combine these, for instance direct models that also use earlier-step predictions. Evaluation should report error per horizon, since accuracy usually degrades with distance. A frequent pitfall in the recursive strategy is using features, such as future lag values or rolling means, that will not actually be known at prediction time for later steps.","u":["Day-ahead hourly electricity load forecasting","Weekly demand forecasts over a planning horizon","Multi-hour traffic or ride demand prediction"],"x":"A power grid operator produces day-ahead forecasts for each of the next 24 hours using a direct strategy with one gradient-boosted model per hour of the horizon. The direct approach avoided the error buildup of a recursive model in the evening hours, and errors are tracked per horizon step in monitoring."},"gbmts":{"n":"Gradient Boosting for Time Series","d":"Gradient boosting for time series turns forecasting into a tabular regression problem: each row describes a series at a point in time with lag features, rolling statistics, calendar fields and external variables, and a model such as LightGBM predicts the future value. It is one of the most successful approaches in large retail forecasting competitions.\n\nThe key advantage is that one global model is trained across thousands of related series, sharing patterns and handling categorical identifiers, prices, promotions and holidays. Losses such as Tweedie or Poisson suit intermittent sales, and quantile loss gives prediction intervals. Feature engineering does most of the work, and it must respect time: every lag and rolling window has to be computable at the moment of prediction, which for multi-step horizons means lags of at least the horizon length. Tree models cannot extrapolate beyond the target range seen in training, so strong trends are handled by differencing or by predicting ratios to a recent level.","u":["Store-item sales forecasting across thousands of series","Ride demand forecasting with weather and events","Energy load forecasting with calendar features"],"x":"A large grocery retailer forecasts daily unit sales for every store and product with a single global LightGBM model using Tweedie loss for the many zero-sales days. Lags start at 28 days so the model can produce a full four-week horizon without recursive feedback, and price and promotion features come from the planning system."},"nbeats":{"n":"N-BEATS","d":"N-BEATS (Neural Basis Expansion Analysis for Time Series) is a deep forecasting architecture built only from fully connected layers, organized into stacks of blocks connected by residual links. It showed that a pure deep learning model could outperform statistical ensembles on the M4 benchmark.\n\nEach block takes a lookback window, passes it through an MLP, and outputs two vectors of basis expansion coefficients: a backcast that reconstructs part of the input and a forecast for the horizon. The backcast is subtracted from the block's input before the next block, so later blocks model what earlier ones missed, and the final forecast is the sum of all block forecasts. In the interpretable variant, blocks are constrained to polynomial trend and Fourier seasonal bases, giving a decomposition into trend and seasonality. The generic variant uses learned bases and is usually more accurate. The original model is univariate; the N-BEATSx and N-HiTS extensions add exogenous inputs and better long-horizon efficiency.","u":["Univariate forecasting across many related series","Interpretable trend and seasonality decomposition","Strong neural baseline for forecasting benchmarks"],"x":"A logistics company trains one global N-BEATS model on weekly shipment volumes from thousands of routes, using the interpretable variant so planners can see separate trend and seasonal forecasts. Training on many series at once gives the model enough data, and inference on a GPU is fast enough for weekly batch refreshes."},"tft":{"n":"Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT)","d":"The Temporal Fusion Transformer is a deep learning architecture for multi-horizon forecasting that combines recurrent layers, attention and gating to handle static attributes, past observed inputs and future known inputs together. It produces quantile forecasts and exposes interpretable weights.\n\nVariable selection networks learn how much each input feature matters at each time step. Static covariates, such as store type, condition the network through context vectors. An LSTM encoder-decoder processes local sequence patterns, and interpretable multi-head attention over time captures long-range dependencies; gated residual networks let the model skip components it does not need. Training uses quantile loss, so the model predicts, for example, the 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles directly. TFT suits problems with rich covariates such as promotions, prices, holidays and weather. It is heavier to train and tune than gradient boosting, needs a substantial amount of data, and its attention and selection weights indicate model reliance, not causal influence.","u":["Retail demand forecasting with promotion calendars","Electricity price forecasting with weather covariates","Probabilistic forecasts for inventory decisions"],"x":"An energy trading desk uses TFT to forecast day-ahead prices with quantile outputs, feeding it weather forecasts and scheduled plant outages as known future inputs. The predicted quantiles drive bidding risk limits, and the variable selection weights help analysts check that the model responds to the expected drivers."},"patchtst":{"n":"PatchTST","d":"PatchTST is a Transformer for long-horizon time-series forecasting that splits each series into short, possibly overlapping patches and treats each patch as one token. It also processes every channel of a multivariate series independently with shared weights.\n\nPatching reduces the number of tokens by roughly the stride, so attention cost drops sharply and the model can look much further back. Each token also carries local shape information that a single time step lacks, which helps attention find meaningful patterns. Channel independence means a multivariate dataset with many variables is treated as many univariate series sharing one model, which acts as regularization and helped PatchTST beat earlier Transformer forecasters that mixed channels at every step. Instance normalization (RevIN) handles level shifts between training and test windows. The model also supports self-supervised pretraining by masking patches. The design choice has a cost: pure channel independence cannot directly exploit cross-variable relationships, which matter in some applications such as related sensors.","u":["Long-horizon forecasting of electricity load","Forecasting many sensor channels with shared weights","Self-supervised pretraining on unlabeled time series"],"x":"A data center operations team forecasts the next 96 intervals of temperature and power draw for hundreds of racks with PatchTST, using a long lookback window that would be too expensive for a point-wise Transformer. Channel independence lets one model serve all racks, and RevIN keeps forecasts stable when a rack's baseline load shifts."},"tsfm":{"n":"Time-Series Foundation Models","d":"Time-series foundation models are large neural networks pretrained on very large and diverse collections of time series so they can forecast new series zero-shot, without training on the target data. Examples include Amazon's Chronos, Google's TimesFM, Salesforce's Moirai and Lag-Llama.\n\nDesigns differ. Chronos scales and quantizes values into tokens and trains a T5-style language model on them, producing probabilistic forecasts by sampling. TimesFM is a decoder-only Transformer over patches of the series. Moirai uses patching with multiple patch sizes and supports multivariate inputs and covariates. Pretraining mixes real and synthetic series. They are useful as strong baselines, for cold-start problems with many short series, and when a team cannot maintain per-dataset models. Limitations include limited or no use of covariates in some models, high inference cost compared with classical methods, and the risk that benchmark scores overlap with pretraining data. Fine-tuning or a well-tuned gradient boosting model can still win on domain-specific data.","u":["Zero-shot forecasts for new products without history","Quick baselines across many heterogeneous series","Probabilistic forecasts without per-series training"],"x":"A cloud infrastructure team uses a pretrained Chronos model to produce zero-shot capacity forecasts for thousands of newly launched services that have only weeks of history. They benchmark it monthly against seasonal naive and LightGBM baselines and keep the foundation model only for series where it wins, since inference on GPU costs more than classical models."},"gd":{"n":"Gradient Descent","d":"Gradient descent minimizes a function by repeatedly moving the parameters a small step in the direction of the negative gradient, which is the direction of steepest local decrease. It is the foundation of almost all neural network training.\n\nEach iteration computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every parameter and subtracts it, scaled by the learning rate. In full-batch gradient descent the gradient is computed on the entire training set, so each step is exact but expensive. For convex, smooth functions with a suitable learning rate, it converges to the global minimum; for non-convex losses such as deep networks, it reaches a stationary point that depends on initialization. The learning rate is critical: too small wastes computation, and too large causes oscillation or divergence, with the safe limit set by the curvature of the loss. Poorly scaled features create elongated valleys that make plain gradient descent zigzag slowly, which is why feature scaling, momentum and adaptive methods help.","u":["Fitting linear and logistic regression models","Training neural networks via backpropagation","Solving smooth optimization problems in scientific computing"],"x":"A risk modeling team fits a logistic regression on a few million rows with full-batch gradient-based optimization because the convex loss guarantees a unique solution that auditors can reproduce exactly. They standardize features first, which noticeably reduces the number of iterations to converge."},"sgd":{"n":"Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)","d":"Stochastic gradient descent updates model parameters using the gradient computed on a single example or a small random subset instead of the whole dataset. Each step is cheap and noisy, but on average it points in the right direction because the sampled gradient is an unbiased estimate of the full gradient.\n\nThis makes training on datasets far larger than memory practical, since the cost per update does not grow with dataset size. The noise also has benefits: it helps the optimizer escape sharp minima and saddle points, and it is believed to favor solutions that generalize well. In deep learning, \"SGD\" almost always means mini-batch SGD, often with momentum. Plain SGD with a constant learning rate does not converge exactly; it keeps bouncing in a region around the minimum whose size scales with the learning rate, so a decaying schedule is needed for convergence. SGD is also more sensitive than adaptive methods to the learning rate and to feature scaling.","u":["Training large linear models on streaming data","Training convolutional networks for image classification","Online learning where data arrives continuously"],"x":"An ad-tech company trains click-prediction logistic models with SGD on a continuous event stream, updating weights as new impressions arrive. Per-example updates keep memory constant regardless of traffic volume, and a decaying learning rate keeps the model stable while still adapting to new campaigns."},"minibatch":{"n":"Mini-Batch","d":"A mini-batch is a small random subset of training examples, typically between 16 and a few thousand, used to compute one gradient estimate and one parameter update. Mini-batch training sits between full-batch gradient descent and single-example SGD and is the default in deep learning.\n\nAveraging the gradient over B examples reduces its variance roughly by a factor of B, and batching lets GPUs process many examples in parallel, so throughput rises sharply until the hardware is saturated. Larger batches give smoother but fewer updates per epoch; the linear scaling rule suggests increasing the learning rate in proportion to batch size, usually with warmup. Beyond a certain critical batch size, adding examples no longer reduces the number of steps needed and simply wastes computation. When memory limits the batch, gradient accumulation sums gradients over several smaller batches before one update. Batch size also interacts with batch normalization, whose statistics become unreliable for very small batches.","u":["Efficient GPU training of deep neural networks","Gradient accumulation for memory-limited LLM fine-tuning","Scaling training across multiple devices"],"x":"A team fine-tuning a language model on a single GPU uses a per-device batch of 4 with 16 gradient accumulation steps to reach an effective batch of 64. This matched the stable settings from their multi-GPU runs while fitting within memory, and they scaled the learning rate based on the effective batch size."},"momentum":{"n":"Momentum","d":"Momentum speeds up gradient descent by accumulating an exponentially decaying average of past gradients and moving the parameters along this velocity instead of the raw gradient. Directions where gradients agree build up speed, while directions where they oscillate cancel out.\n\nWith a coefficient β, commonly 0.9, the velocity integrates about 1/(1 − β) recent gradients, so the effective step in a consistent direction becomes up to ten times larger. In narrow, curved valleys where plain gradient descent zigzags across the walls, momentum damps the oscillation and moves faster along the valley floor. It also smooths the noise of stochastic gradients. SGD with momentum remains a strong optimizer for convolutional networks and is the basis of Adam's first-moment estimate. Two conventions exist: some libraries scale the gradient by (1 − β) and others do not, which changes the effective learning rate, so hyperparameters do not transfer directly between implementations. High momentum with a high learning rate can overshoot and cause instability.","u":["Training ResNet-style image classifiers with SGD","Accelerating convergence in ill-conditioned problems","Smoothing noisy gradients in small-batch training"],"x":"A medical imaging team trains a convolutional segmentation model with SGD with momentum 0.9 and a cosine schedule, after finding that it generalized slightly better than Adam on their held-out hospital data. They keep momentum fixed and tune only the learning rate, which simplified their hyperparameter search."},"nesterov":{"n":"Nesterov Accelerated Gradient","d":"Nesterov Accelerated Gradient is a variant of momentum that evaluates the gradient at the position the parameters are about to reach, rather than at the current position. This look-ahead lets the optimizer correct its velocity before overshooting.\n\nClassical momentum adds the current gradient to the accumulated velocity; Nesterov first moves along the velocity and then measures the gradient there. If the velocity is carrying the parameters past a minimum, the look-ahead gradient already points back, so the update slows down sooner and oscillates less. For smooth convex problems, Nesterov's method achieves the optimal convergence rate of O(1/t²) for first-order methods, versus O(1/t) for plain gradient descent. In deep learning the practical gain over classical momentum is usually modest but consistent, and it is enabled by a single flag in most frameworks. Implementations use an equivalent reformulation that computes the gradient at the current parameters, so no extra forward pass is needed. Nadam combines the same idea with Adam.","u":["Training convolutional networks with SGD","Accelerated convex optimization in classical ML","Reducing oscillation compared with classical momentum"],"x":"A computer-vision team benchmarking optimizers for an image classifier switched from classical momentum to Nesterov momentum with the same learning rate schedule. The change cost nothing in compute and gave slightly smoother loss curves, so they adopted it as the default in their training template."},"rmsprop":{"n":"RMSProp","d":"RMSProp is an adaptive optimizer that divides each parameter's gradient by a running root mean square of its recent gradients. Parameters with consistently large gradients get smaller steps, and parameters with small gradients get larger ones.\n\nThe method keeps an exponential moving average of squared gradients, usually with decay ρ = 0.9 or 0.99, and scales the update by the inverse square root of that average plus a small ε. It fixes the main weakness of AdaGrad, whose accumulated sum of squared gradients only grows and eventually shrinks the learning rate to almost zero; the moving average lets RMSProp keep adapting during long training. It works well on non-stationary objectives and was a common choice for recurrent networks and reinforcement learning. Adam extends it with momentum and bias correction. RMSProp has no bias correction, so early updates can be large while the average warms up, and the ε value, sometimes placed inside the square root, can noticeably change behavior.","u":["Training recurrent networks on sequential data","Deep reinforcement learning such as DQN agents","Optimizing objectives that change during training"],"x":"A game studio training reinforcement learning agents for automated playtesting uses RMSProp, following the settings of established DQN implementations. The per-parameter scaling handles the shifting reward signal as the agent's policy changes, and the team clips gradients to prevent rare large updates from destabilizing the value network."},"adam":{"n":"Adam","d":"Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation) is an optimizer that combines momentum with per-parameter adaptive step sizes. It keeps exponential moving averages of the gradient and of the squared gradient, corrects both for their initialization at zero, and uses their ratio as the update.\n\nThe first moment m acts like momentum and smooths the update direction; the second moment v scales each parameter's step by the inverse of its typical gradient magnitude. Bias correction divides each average by 1 − βᵗ, preventing tiny or oversized steps in early iterations. Its defaults work across many problems, which made Adam the standard optimizer for Transformers and much of deep learning. Its update magnitude is roughly bounded by the learning rate, making it robust to gradient scale. Limitations include higher memory use, since two extra values are stored per parameter, and occasional worse generalization than tuned SGD on vision tasks. With weight decay, the L2 penalty gets rescaled by v, so AdamW is preferred.","u":["Training Transformers and language models","Default optimizer for prototyping neural networks","Training models with sparse or noisy gradients"],"x":"A recommendation team training a two-tower retrieval model with large sparse embedding tables uses Adam because rarely seen item embeddings receive infrequent gradients, and adaptive scaling gives them larger effective steps. The optimizer state triples memory for the embeddings, so they use a sparse Adam variant that only updates rows present in each batch."},"adamw":{"n":"AdamW","d":"AdamW is Adam with decoupled weight decay: instead of adding an L2 penalty to the loss, it shrinks the weights directly by a fixed fraction at every step, separately from the adaptive gradient update. It is the standard optimizer for training and fine-tuning Transformers.\n\nIn plain Adam, an L2 term is added to the gradient and then divided by the adaptive denominator √v̂, so parameters with large gradient history are barely regularized while rarely updated ones are penalized heavily. This makes L2 regularization and weight decay non-equivalent for adaptive methods. AdamW applies θ ← θ − ηλθ outside the adaptive scaling, so every weight decays at the same relative rate, which improves generalization and makes the learning rate and decay strength easier to tune independently. Common practice excludes biases and normalization weights from decay. Note that in PyTorch the decay is multiplied by the learning rate, so changing the schedule also changes the effective regularization.","u":["Pretraining and fine-tuning large language models","Training Vision Transformers and BERT-style encoders","Regularized training of any Adam-based model"],"x":"An NLP team fine-tunes a BERT-style encoder for document classification with AdamW, weight decay 0.01 and linear warmup followed by linear decay, matching the Hugging Face Trainer defaults. They exclude bias and LayerNorm parameters from decay, which follows the original BERT recipe and avoids shrinking normalization scales toward zero."},"lr":{"n":"Learning Rate","d":"The learning rate is the scalar that sets how large a step the optimizer takes along its update direction at each iteration. It is usually the single most important hyperparameter in training a neural network.\n\nIf it is too small, training is slow and may stall on plateaus; if it is too large, the loss oscillates or diverges, often showing up as NaN values. For a quadratic loss, gradient descent is stable only when the learning rate is below 2 divided by the largest curvature. The best value depends on the optimizer, batch size, model size and architecture: Adam-style optimizers commonly use 1e-4 to 1e-3, while SGD on vision models often starts near 0.1. A learning rate range test, which increases the rate exponentially during a short run and watches the loss, helps find a good starting value. Fine-tuning a pretrained model typically needs a much smaller rate than training from scratch to avoid destroying learned features.","u":["Tuning the first hyperparameter of any training run","Choosing small rates for fine-tuning pretrained models","Diagnosing divergence and loss spikes"],"x":"An ML platform team runs a short learning rate range test before every new model family, sweeping exponentially across several orders of magnitude and picking a value somewhat below where the loss starts to rise. This replaced long grid searches and caught several configurations where the default rate diverged after a batch size change."},"lrsched":{"n":"Learning Rate Schedules","d":"A learning rate schedule changes the learning rate during training according to a predefined rule, typically starting high to make fast progress and ending low to settle into a good minimum. Almost all modern training recipes use one.\n\nCommon schedules include step decay, which divides the rate by a factor at fixed epochs; exponential decay; cosine annealing, which follows a half cosine from the peak to a small minimum; linear decay to zero, standard for Transformer fine-tuning; and one-cycle, which rises and then falls within a single run. Reduce-on-plateau schedules react to the validation metric instead of following a fixed plan. Large early steps explore broadly and add helpful noise, while small late steps reduce the noise floor of stochastic gradients so the loss can drop further. A practical pitfall is that cosine and linear schedules depend on the total number of steps, so extending training afterward requires restarting or using a schedule such as warmup-stable-decay.","u":["Cosine decay for pretraining language models","Linear decay for Transformer fine-tuning","Step decay for training image classifiers"],"x":"A team pretraining a mid-sized language model uses a warmup-stable-decay schedule, holding the learning rate constant for most of training and decaying it only in the final phase. This let them branch extra decay runs from intermediate checkpoints when they decided to train on more tokens, which a fixed cosine schedule would not allow."},"warmup":{"n":"Warmup","d":"Learning rate warmup starts training with a small learning rate and increases it, usually linearly, to the target value over the first few hundred or thousand steps. It prevents unstable updates at the start of training, when the model and optimizer statistics are still poorly calibrated.\n\nEarly in training, gradients can be large and erratic, and adaptive optimizers such as Adam have second-moment estimates based on only a few samples, which can produce oversized steps even with bias correction. In Transformers, large early updates can push the network into a region it never recovers from. Warmup lets these statistics settle and lets the model reach a smoother region before full-size steps begin. It is also important when using large batches with a scaled-up learning rate. Typical warmup lengths are 1 to 10 percent of training steps for fine-tuning, or a few thousand steps for pretraining. Warmup does not replace a decay schedule, and an excessively long warmup wastes training budget.","u":["Stabilizing early Transformer and LLM training","Large-batch training with scaled learning rates","Fine-tuning pretrained models with AdamW"],"x":"A research team scaling batch size across many GPUs found that the scaled learning rate diverged in the first few hundred steps. Adding a linear warmup over the first few epochs, as in published large-batch ImageNet recipes, removed the divergence without changing the rest of the schedule."},"landscape":{"n":"Loss Landscape","d":"The loss landscape is the surface traced by a model's loss as a function of its parameters, and its shape determines how easily gradient-based training can find good solutions. With millions of weights the surface has millions of dimensions, so researchers study it through low-dimensional slices: the loss along a line between two checkpoints, or on a 2D plane spanned by random directions that are filter-normalized so layer scale differences do not distort the picture.\n\nThese visualizations explain several practical effects. Skip connections and batch normalization produce visibly smoother surfaces, wider networks tend to have more connected low-loss regions, and large learning rates bounce out of narrow valleys. Landscape analysis is useful for comparing architectures, diagnosing unstable training and studying mode connectivity between solutions. The main limitation is that a 2D slice of a huge space can look convex or chaotic depending on the chosen directions, so conclusions drawn from a single plot should be treated with caution.","u":["Comparing smoothness of architectures with and without skip connections","Diagnosing divergence and loss spikes during training","Studying mode connectivity between independently trained checkpoints"],"x":"A research team evaluating a new normalization layer plots 2D loss slices around the final checkpoints of both the baseline and the new model. The smoother, wider basin of the new variant supports adopting it, and the team combines the plots with learning-rate sweeps because a slice alone is not conclusive."},"saddle":{"n":"Saddle Points","d":"A saddle point is a location where the gradient is zero but the surface curves upward in some directions and downward in others, so it is neither a minimum nor a maximum. Formally, the Hessian at that point has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nIn high-dimensional neural network losses, saddle points are far more common than poor local minima: for a critical point to be a minimum, every one of millions of curvature directions must point upward, which is unlikely at high loss values. Near a saddle the gradient becomes tiny, so plain gradient descent can slow down on long plateaus even though a descent direction exists. Gradient noise and momentum help escape by pushing parameters along negative-curvature directions. Training plateaus are more often caused by saddles and flat regions than by bad local minima. Pure Newton's method is attracted to saddles, which is one reason it is rarely used unmodified for deep learning.","u":["Explaining long training plateaus in deep networks","Motivating noisy or momentum-based optimizers","Analyzing critical points through Hessian eigenvalues"],"x":"A team training a deep recommendation model sees the loss stall for thousands of steps before dropping again. Computing the top and bottom Hessian eigenvalues with Hessian-vector products shows a negative-curvature direction, so they add momentum and a short learning-rate warmup instead of restarting with a new initialization."},"sharpflat":{"n":"Sharp vs Flat Minima","d":"Sharp and flat minima differ in how quickly the loss rises when the parameters are perturbed: in a flat minimum the loss stays low over a wide neighborhood, while in a sharp minimum a small change causes a large increase. Sharpness is usually measured by the largest Hessian eigenvalue or by the worst-case loss increase inside a small ball around the weights.\n\nThe common intuition is that flat minima generalize better, because the test loss surface is a slightly shifted version of the training surface, and a wide basin tolerates that shift. Small-batch SGD, large learning rates and Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) all bias training toward flatter regions. The idea is useful for choosing batch sizes, learning-rate schedules and optimizers. An important caveat is that sharpness is not invariant to reparameterization: rescaling weights in a ReLU network can make a minimum arbitrarily sharp without changing the function, so flatness is a helpful heuristic rather than a guaranteed predictor of generalization.","u":["Choosing batch size and learning rate together","Training with Sharpness-Aware Minimization for robustness","Comparing generalization of competing checkpoints"],"x":"An image classification team notices that increasing the batch size to speed up training lowers test accuracy. They switch to SAM with a small neighborhood radius, accepting roughly double the compute per step because each update needs two forward-backward passes, and recover the lost accuracy."},"convex":{"n":"Convex Optimization","d":"Convex optimization is the study of minimizing a convex function over a convex set, a setting in which every local minimum is also a global minimum. A function is convex when the straight line between any two points on its graph lies on or above the graph, which for twice-differentiable functions means the Hessian is positive semidefinite everywhere.\n\nThis property makes convex problems reliable: gradient descent, Newton's method and interior-point solvers converge to the global optimum with provable rates, and duality gives certificates of optimality. Linear regression, logistic regression, linear SVMs, LASSO and many portfolio and scheduling problems are convex, so they can be solved exactly and reproducibly. Deep neural network training is not convex, yet convex analysis still guides learning-rate choices and convergence proofs. A frequent mistake is assuming that a convex loss guarantees a unique solution; the minimizer is unique only when the function is strictly convex, which is one reason L2 regularization is added to under-determined problems.","u":["Training logistic regression and linear SVMs","Portfolio optimization under risk constraints","Resource allocation and scheduling with linear constraints"],"x":"An asset management team solves a mean-variance portfolio problem every night with a convex quadratic program. Because the problem is convex, the solver returns the global optimum with a duality-gap certificate, which makes the allocation auditable and reproducible for compliance review."},"newton":{"n":"Second-Order Methods (Newton)","d":"Second-order methods use curvature information from the Hessian, not just the gradient, to choose both the direction and the size of each optimization step. Newton's method fits a local quadratic model of the loss and jumps to its minimum, giving the update θ ← θ − H⁻¹∇L.\n\nNear a well-behaved minimum this converges quadratically, often in a handful of iterations, and it automatically rescales poorly conditioned directions that make gradient descent zigzag. That is why Newton and quasi-Newton variants such as L-BFGS are standard for logistic regression, GLMs and other small to medium convex problems. For deep networks the Hessian has n² entries and costs O(n³) to invert, which is infeasible with millions of parameters, so practical methods use approximations like K-FAC, Shampoo or Hessian-vector products. Another pitfall is that on non-convex losses the raw Newton step moves toward any critical point, including saddles and maxima, so damping or trust regions are required.","u":["Fitting logistic regression and GLMs with IRLS","Full-batch optimization with L-BFGS","Preconditioned training with K-FAC or Shampoo"],"x":"A credit risk team fits regularized logistic regression on a few hundred features with scikit-learn's lbfgs or newton-cg solver. The quasi-Newton solver converges in tens of iterations, much faster than SGD, and gives deterministic coefficients that the model validation group can reproduce exactly."},"gridsearch":{"n":"Grid Search","d":"Grid search tunes hyperparameters by evaluating every combination from a fixed list of candidate values for each one. Each combination is usually scored with cross-validation, and the best-scoring setting is then refit on the full training data.\n\nThe method is simple, exhaustive within its grid, trivially parallel and fully reproducible, which makes it a good choice when there are only one to three hyperparameters with a few sensible values each, such as the regularization strength C and kernel width gamma of an SVM. Its cost grows exponentially with the number of hyperparameters: five parameters with five values each already require 3,125 fits per fold. It also wastes budget: when only one or two hyperparameters matter, most grid points repeat their values. Scales matter too, so values like learning rates should be spaced logarithmically. Finally, the cross-validation score of the winner is optimistically biased; use a held-out test set or nested cross-validation for an honest estimate.","u":["Tuning C and gamma for an SVM","Selecting regularization strength for linear models","Small, reproducible sweeps for baseline reports"],"x":"A fraud analytics team tunes an RBF-kernel SVM with a 6 by 6 logarithmic grid over C and gamma, using five-fold stratified cross-validation scored by PR-AUC. The 180 fits run in parallel on one machine, and the final metric is reported on a separate time-based holdout to avoid selection bias."},"randsearch":{"n":"Random Search","d":"Random search tunes hyperparameters by sampling a fixed number of configurations from specified distributions instead of enumerating a grid. Each sampled configuration is evaluated, typically with cross-validation, and the best one is kept.\n\nIts advantage, shown by Bergstra and Bengio, comes from the fact that usually only a few hyperparameters strongly affect performance. A grid of 25 points tests only 5 distinct values of each parameter, while 25 random samples test 25 distinct values of every parameter, so the important ones are explored much more densely for the same budget. Random search also lets you specify continuous and log-uniform distributions, stops at any time, and parallelizes perfectly, making it a strong default for models with many hyperparameters, such as gradient boosting or neural networks. Its limitation is that it ignores earlier results, so it does not concentrate samples in promising regions; Bayesian optimization or Hyperband can be more efficient when each evaluation is expensive.","u":["Tuning gradient boosting with many hyperparameters","Fast baseline sweeps for neural network training","Exploring continuous ranges such as learning rates"],"x":"A marketing analytics team tunes a LightGBM churn model by drawing 60 random configurations from log-uniform learning rates and integer ranges for leaves and depth. The sweep runs overnight across a small cluster, and because each trial is independent, failed jobs are simply skipped without affecting the others."},"bayesopt":{"n":"Bayesian Optimization","d":"Bayesian optimization finds good hyperparameters with few evaluations by fitting a probabilistic surrogate model of the objective and using it to decide which configuration to try next. The surrogate, often a Gaussian process or a tree-structured Parzen estimator (TPE), predicts both the expected score and its uncertainty for every candidate.\n\nAn acquisition function, such as Expected Improvement or Upper Confidence Bound, balances exploitation of regions predicted to be good against exploration of uncertain regions. After each trial the surrogate is updated, so sampling concentrates in promising areas. This makes the method well suited to expensive black-box objectives: training a large model, running a simulation or tuning a physical process, where only tens to a few hundred evaluations are affordable. Libraries like Optuna add pruning to stop weak trials early. The main limitations are that the sequential nature reduces parallelism, Gaussian-process surrogates scale poorly beyond roughly twenty dimensions, and noisy objectives can mislead the model.","u":["Tuning deep learning hyperparameters on a limited budget","Optimizing simulation or experiment settings","Automated machine learning pipeline configuration"],"x":"A speech team tunes learning rate, warmup length, dropout and weight decay for a model where each training run takes several GPU hours. Using Optuna with a TPE sampler and a median pruner, they stop weak runs after a few epochs and reach a strong configuration with far fewer full runs than a random sweep would need."},"neuron":{"n":"Artificial Neuron","d":"An artificial neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs, adds a bias, and passes the result through a nonlinear activation function. It is the basic building block of every neural network layer.\n\nThe weights decide how strongly each input influences the output, and the bias shifts the point at which the neuron responds. Geometrically, the weighted sum defines a hyperplane, and the activation decides how the output changes as an input moves across it. A single neuron with a sigmoid activation is exactly logistic regression, and with a step function it is the classic perceptron. Stacking many neurons into layers, each with a nonlinearity, lets networks represent complex functions. A key limitation is that one neuron can only separate classes with a linear boundary, so it cannot solve problems like XOR. The biological analogy is loose: real neurons communicate through timed spikes and have far richer dynamics than this simple formula.","u":["Building block of dense and convolutional layers","Binary classification as logistic regression","Teaching linear separability and decision boundaries"],"x":"A payments team deploys a single sigmoid neuron, which is logistic regression, as a lightweight first-stage fraud filter that runs in microseconds on every transaction. Its weights are directly interpretable, so risk analysts can review which features push a score up before a heavier model examines flagged cases."},"mlp":{"n":"Multilayer Perceptron (MLP)","d":"A multilayer perceptron is a feedforward neural network made of fully connected layers, where each layer applies a linear transformation followed by a nonlinear activation. Data flows from the input layer through one or more hidden layers to an output layer, with no loops.\n\nEach hidden layer learns new features as combinations of the previous layer's outputs, so deeper layers can capture increasingly abstract patterns. Training uses backpropagation and a gradient-based optimizer to minimize a loss such as cross-entropy or mean squared error. MLPs work well on fixed-size feature vectors, as classification or regression heads on top of pretrained encoders, and as the feed-forward blocks inside every Transformer layer. Without nonlinear activations, any stack of linear layers collapses into a single linear map, so the activation is essential. On raw tabular data, MLPs often underperform gradient-boosted trees unless features are carefully scaled and the network is well regularized, and they ignore spatial or sequential structure that CNNs and Transformers exploit.","u":["Classification heads on top of pretrained embeddings","Regression on standardized numeric feature vectors","Feed-forward sublayers inside Transformer blocks"],"x":"A recommendation team feeds user and item embeddings into a three-layer MLP that predicts click probability. The MLP is small enough to score thousands of candidates per request within a tight latency budget, and the team monitors calibration weekly because feature drift shifts the predicted probabilities."},"backprop":{"n":"Backpropagation","d":"Backpropagation is the algorithm that computes the gradient of a loss with respect to every parameter of a neural network by applying the chain rule backward from the output. It makes gradient-based training of deep networks practical.\n\nDuring the forward pass, each layer computes its outputs and stores the intermediate values it needs. During the backward pass, the gradient of the loss with respect to the output is propagated layer by layer: each layer multiplies the incoming gradient by its local Jacobian to obtain gradients for its own weights and for its inputs, which are passed to the previous layer. Because intermediate results are reused, the full gradient costs roughly two to three times one forward pass rather than one pass per parameter. Backpropagation only computes gradients; the optimizer, such as SGD or Adam, decides how to update the weights. The stored activations dominate training memory, which is why techniques like gradient checkpointing recompute some of them to save memory.","u":["Training any differentiable neural network","Computing input gradients for saliency maps","Gradient checkpointing to reduce training memory"],"x":"A language model team runs out of GPU memory because stored activations for long sequences are too large. They enable activation checkpointing, which discards most activations in the forward pass and recomputes them during backpropagation, trading about a third more compute for the ability to train with longer contexts."},"autograd":{"n":"Chain Rule & Autograd","d":"Automatic differentiation, or autograd, computes exact derivatives of programs by recording elementary operations and applying the chain rule to them. The chain rule states that the derivative of a composed function is the product of the derivatives of its parts.\n\nFrameworks such as PyTorch and JAX build a computational graph as code runs. Each operation knows its local derivative, so reverse-mode differentiation walks the graph backward, multiplying vector-Jacobian products, and obtains the gradient of a scalar loss with respect to all inputs at the cost of a small constant multiple of the forward pass. That efficiency is why reverse mode is used for neural networks with many parameters and one loss. Forward mode is cheaper when there are few inputs and many outputs. Autograd is neither symbolic differentiation, which manipulates expressions, nor numerical finite differences, which are approximate. Common pitfalls include in-place operations that overwrite saved tensors and forgetting to reset accumulated gradients between steps.","u":["Training neural networks without hand-derived gradients","Computing Hessian-vector products for analysis","Differentiating physics simulations and custom losses"],"x":"A robotics team writes its rigid-body simulator in PyTorch so that autograd can differentiate the trajectory error with respect to controller parameters. This removes the need for hand-derived gradients, and they validate the gradients against finite differences with torch.autograd.gradcheck before each release."},"relu":{"n":"ReLU","d":"The Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU) is an activation function that outputs its input when it is positive and zero otherwise. It is the default nonlinearity in many convolutional and fully connected networks.\n\nFor positive inputs its derivative is exactly 1, so gradients pass through active units without shrinking, which greatly eased the training of deep networks compared with sigmoid and tanh. It is also very cheap to compute and produces sparse activations, since roughly half of the units output zero for a typical input. ReLU pairs naturally with He initialization, which compensates for the half of the signal it discards. Its main weakness is the dying ReLU problem: if a unit's pre-activation becomes negative for all inputs, for example after a large update, its gradient is zero and it never recovers. Leaky ReLU, ELU and GELU address this by allowing a small response for negative inputs. At zero, frameworks use 0 as the gradient.","u":["Hidden layers of CNNs for image recognition","Default activation in fully connected networks","Fast inference on mobile and edge hardware"],"x":"A mobile vision team uses ReLU-based convolutional blocks in an on-device object detector because the operation fuses cleanly with convolution in quantized integer inference. They monitor the fraction of dead channels after training and lower the learning rate when too many units stop activating."},"sigmoid":{"n":"Sigmoid","d":"The sigmoid, or logistic function, maps any real number to a value between 0 and 1 along a smooth S-shaped curve. It is the standard way to turn a single logit into a probability.\n\nIn binary classification and multi-label problems, each output logit passes through a sigmoid to give an independent probability, and the model is trained with binary cross-entropy. Sigmoids also act as gates inside LSTMs and GRUs, where a value near 0 blocks information and a value near 1 lets it through. As a hidden-layer activation, however, the sigmoid has largely been replaced: its derivative is at most 0.25 and approaches zero for large positive or negative inputs, so gradients shrink quickly through many layers, and its outputs are not centered at zero, which slows optimization. A numerical pitfall is applying a sigmoid and then a log separately; use a combined, stable function such as BCEWithLogitsLoss.","u":["Output layer for binary classification","Independent probabilities for multi-label tagging","Gating mechanisms in LSTM and GRU cells"],"x":"A content moderation team trains a multi-label classifier where one post can be both spam and offensive, so each of its labels gets its own sigmoid output. Per-label thresholds are tuned on a validation set to meet different precision targets for each policy category."},"tanh":{"n":"Tanh","d":"The hyperbolic tangent (tanh) is an S-shaped activation function that maps inputs to the range from −1 to 1 and is centered at zero. It is a rescaled sigmoid: tanh(x) = 2σ(2x) − 1.\n\nBecause its outputs are symmetric around zero, the inputs to the next layer have roughly zero mean, which tends to make optimization better behaved than with the sigmoid. Its maximum derivative is 1 at the origin, four times that of the sigmoid. Tanh is still common inside recurrent networks, where it squashes the candidate cell state and hidden state of LSTMs and GRUs, keeping values bounded across many time steps. It also appears as the output of models that predict values in a fixed range, such as normalized actions in reinforcement learning or pixel values in some GAN generators. The main drawback is saturation: for large inputs the gradient approaches zero, so deep stacks of tanh layers suffer from vanishing gradients.","u":["Hidden and cell state updates in LSTMs","Bounded continuous actions in reinforcement learning","Generator outputs scaled to the range −1 to 1"],"x":"A robotics team trains a policy network for a robotic arm whose joint commands must lie within fixed limits. A tanh output layer maps each action to the range −1 to 1, which is then scaled to physical torque limits, so the controller can never request an unsafe value."},"gelu":{"n":"GELU","d":"The Gaussian Error Linear Unit (GELU) is a smooth activation function that multiplies each input by the probability that a standard normal variable is smaller than it. Large positive inputs pass almost unchanged, large negative inputs are suppressed toward zero, and values near zero are scaled smoothly.\n\nUnlike ReLU, GELU has a nonzero gradient for moderately negative inputs and a slight dip below zero, which tends to give smoother optimization. It became the standard activation in BERT, GPT-2 and Vision Transformers, usually inside the feed-forward block of each layer. Implementations offer an exact version using the error function and a faster tanh approximation; mixing them between training and inference causes small numerical differences, so checkpoints should be run with the variant they were trained with. GELU costs more than ReLU to compute, though the difference is small relative to matrix multiplications. Many recent large language models have moved to gated variants such as SwiGLU instead.","u":["Feed-forward blocks in BERT and GPT-style models","MLP layers inside Vision Transformers","Smooth activation for fine-tuned encoders"],"x":"A search team fine-tunes a BERT-based reranker and exports it to an optimized inference runtime. They confirm that the runtime uses the same exact GELU as the original checkpoint, because switching to the tanh approximation produced small but measurable shifts in ranking scores during validation."},"swiglu":{"n":"SwiGLU","d":"SwiGLU is a gated feed-forward layer used in modern Transformers, in which one linear projection of the input, passed through the Swish (SiLU) activation, is multiplied element by element with a second linear projection. A third projection maps the result back to the model dimension.\n\nThe gate lets the network control, per hidden unit and per token, how much of the signal passes through, which empirically improves language-modeling quality over ReLU or GELU feed-forward blocks at the same compute. LLaMA, Mistral, PaLM and many other large language models use it. Because SwiGLU has three weight matrices instead of two, implementations typically shrink the hidden size to about two thirds of the usual 4× expansion, often rounded to a hardware-friendly multiple, to keep the parameter count comparable. SwiGLU is not a simple activation function but a full layer with learned weights, and its benefit comes from multiplicative gating rather than the shape of Swish alone.","u":["Feed-forward blocks in LLaMA-style language models","Replacing GELU MLPs in Transformer pretraining","Gated MLP layers in vision and speech Transformers"],"x":"A team pretraining an in-house code model adopts the LLaMA block design with SwiGLU feed-forward layers and sets the hidden size to about 8/3 of the model width, rounded to a multiple of 256. The rounding keeps matrix shapes efficient on their GPUs while matching the parameter budget of the previous GELU baseline."},"softmax":{"n":"Softmax","d":"Softmax converts a vector of real-valued scores, called logits, into a probability distribution: every output is positive and all outputs sum to 1. It exponentiates each score and divides by the sum of the exponentials.\n\nExponentiation amplifies differences, so the largest logit receives most of the probability mass, while the ranking of classes is preserved. Softmax is the output layer for multi-class classification, normalizes attention scores in Transformers, and produces next-token distributions in language models, where dividing logits by a temperature makes the distribution sharper or flatter. Adding the same constant to all logits does not change the result, which is used for numerical stability: implementations subtract the maximum logit before exponentiating to avoid overflow. In training, softmax should be fused with the log inside the cross-entropy loss rather than applied separately. A common misconception is that softmax outputs are calibrated probabilities; modern networks are often overconfident and need calibration such as temperature scaling.","u":["Multi-class classification output layers","Normalizing attention weights in Transformers","Temperature-controlled sampling in language models"],"x":"A document routing team classifies incoming support tickets into twenty queues with a softmax output. Because the raw probabilities were overconfident, they fit a single temperature parameter on a validation set, which improves calibration so that tickets below a confidence threshold are reliably sent to a human."},"xent":{"n":"Cross-Entropy Loss","d":"Cross-entropy loss measures how well predicted class probabilities match the true labels by taking the negative log of the probability assigned to the correct class. Minimizing it is equivalent to maximum likelihood estimation for a categorical model.\n\nThe loss is near zero when the model assigns high probability to the right answer and grows without bound as that probability approaches zero, so confident mistakes are punished heavily. Combined with softmax, its gradient with respect to the logits is simply the predicted probabilities minus the one-hot target, which gives clean, well-scaled updates. It is the standard loss for classification and for next-token prediction in language models, where its exponential is perplexity. Practical variants include class weights for imbalance, label smoothing to reduce overconfidence and focal loss for hard examples. A frequent bug is applying softmax before PyTorch's CrossEntropyLoss, which already expects raw logits and applies log-softmax internally; doing so silently weakens the gradients.","u":["Training image and text classifiers","Next-token prediction in language model pretraining","Knowledge distillation with soft teacher targets"],"x":"A team training a product categorization model on thousands of categories uses cross-entropy with label smoothing of 0.1, because many products plausibly fit neighboring categories. They track validation loss and accuracy together, since loss reveals growing overconfidence before accuracy changes."},"vanishing":{"n":"Vanishing & Exploding Gradients","d":"Vanishing and exploding gradients occur when gradients shrink toward zero or grow without bound as they are propagated backward through many layers or time steps. In both cases, effective training of the early layers breaks down.\n\nBackpropagation multiplies one Jacobian per layer. If those factors are typically smaller than 1 in magnitude, the product decays exponentially and the first layers barely learn; if they are larger than 1, it grows exponentially and updates become unstable, producing NaN losses. Saturating activations such as sigmoid, poor weight scales and long unrolled recurrent networks make the problem worse. Standard remedies are ReLU-family activations, Xavier or He initialization, normalization layers, residual connections, gated architectures like LSTM, and gradient clipping, which caps the gradient norm and mainly addresses explosion. Monitoring per-layer gradient norms during training is the most direct diagnosis. Note that clipping does not fix vanishing gradients; that requires architectural changes.","u":["Diagnosing stalled or unstable deep network training","Choosing gradient clipping thresholds for RNNs","Motivating residual and gated architectures"],"x":"A team training a Transformer with mixed precision sees occasional loss spikes followed by NaNs. They log gradient norms per layer, add global gradient-norm clipping at 1.0, and use a learning-rate warmup, which removes the spikes without slowing convergence."},"residual":{"n":"Residual Connections","d":"A residual connection, also called a skip connection, adds a block's input directly to its output, so the block only has to learn a correction to the identity: y = x + F(x). It is the key ingredient that makes very deep networks trainable.\n\nThe identity path gives gradients a direct route backward: the derivative of y with respect to x is I plus the derivative of F, so the signal never has to pass only through a long product of small Jacobians. If a layer is not useful, the block can learn F ≈ 0 and leave the representation unchanged, which makes adding depth less harmful. Residual connections appear in ResNets, every Transformer layer, U-Nets and diffusion models. A practical detail is where normalization goes: pre-norm Transformers apply LayerNorm inside the branch before attention or the MLP, which trains more stably at depth than post-norm, although the residual stream's magnitude can grow with depth.","u":["Training very deep CNNs such as ResNet","Pre-norm residual streams in Transformer layers","Skip paths in U-Net and diffusion backbones"],"x":"A team scaling a Transformer from 12 to 48 layers finds that the post-norm version diverges early in training. Switching to pre-norm residual blocks, where each sublayer adds its output to an untouched residual stream, lets the deeper model train stably with the same learning rate schedule."},"init":{"n":"Weight Initialization (Xavier / He)","d":"Weight initialization sets the starting values of a network's weights so that activations and gradients keep a stable scale from layer to layer. Xavier (Glorot) and He (Kaiming) initialization choose the weight variance from the number of inputs and outputs of each layer.\n\nIf weights are too large, signals grow layer after layer and explode; if too small, they shrink toward zero. Xavier initialization uses a variance of 2/(fan_in + fan_out), which keeps variance roughly constant for symmetric activations such as tanh. He initialization uses 2/fan_in, doubling the variance to compensate for ReLU zeroing about half of its inputs. Weights must also be random: initializing all weights in a layer to the same value makes every unit compute the same thing and receive the same gradient, so symmetry is never broken. Biases are usually set to zero. Modern deep models add normalization and sometimes scale the residual branch outputs by depth, because good initialization alone does not guarantee stability.","u":["Initializing deep ReLU convolutional networks","Stable starting scales for tanh networks","Custom layers in from-scratch model implementations"],"x":"A team implementing a custom convolutional architecture for medical imaging finds that training stalls at the start. Replacing a default small normal initialization with He initialization for all ReLU layers keeps activation variance stable across depth, and the loss decreases from the first epoch."},"uat":{"n":"Universal Approximation Theorem","d":"The Universal Approximation Theorem states that a feedforward network with a single hidden layer and a suitable nonlinear activation can approximate any continuous function on a bounded, closed domain to any desired accuracy, given enough hidden units.\n\nIntuitively, each hidden unit contributes a simple bump or step, and a large enough weighted sum of such pieces can trace any continuous shape. The result holds for sigmoid, ReLU and most other non-polynomial activations. It explains why neural networks are expressive enough in principle for regression and classification. However, the theorem is purely about existence. It does not say how many units are needed, which can grow exponentially with input dimension, nor that gradient descent will find the right weights, nor that the solution will generalize from finite data. Depth helps in practice because deep networks can represent many functions with far fewer parameters than shallow ones. A frequent misconception is that the theorem justifies using a single wide hidden layer.","u":["Justifying neural networks as flexible function approximators","Teaching expressiveness versus learnability in interviews","Motivating surrogate models for physics simulations"],"x":"An engineering simulation team replaces a slow fluid solver with a neural surrogate that maps design parameters to drag. The theorem assures them that a network can represent the mapping, but they still validate on held-out designs, because the theorem says nothing about how much data or training is needed for accuracy."},"embedding":{"n":"Embedding Layer","d":"An embedding layer maps each discrete identifier, such as a token, user or product ID, to a dense, learned vector. It is a lookup table with one trainable row per item.\n\nSelecting a row is mathematically identical to multiplying a one-hot vector by the weight matrix, but the lookup is far cheaper. During training, only the rows used in a batch receive gradients, and items that appear in similar contexts end up with similar vectors, so distances in the embedding space capture useful relationships. Embedding layers are the input stage of every language model and the core of recommendation systems with millions of users and items, where tables can dominate model size. Practical issues include unseen IDs, usually handled with a reserved unknown index or hashing, and rare items with poorly trained vectors. Language models often tie the input embedding with the output projection to save parameters. Embeddings suit semantic search only when trained with a suitable objective.","u":["Token embeddings in language models","User and item vectors in recommender systems","Encoding high-cardinality categorical features in tabular models"],"x":"A streaming service trains a two-tower recommender where user IDs and show IDs each pass through embedding tables. Show vectors are exported to an approximate nearest-neighbor index for fast candidate retrieval, and new shows without history fall back to content-based embeddings until they collect interactions."},"cnn":{"n":"Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)","d":"A convolutional neural network is a network built mainly from convolutional layers, which slide small learned filters over a grid-structured input such as an image, spectrogram or time series. Stacked convolutions, nonlinearities and downsampling turn raw pixels into increasingly abstract feature maps, followed by a head for classification, detection or segmentation.\n\nTwo design choices make CNNs efficient. Local connectivity means each output depends only on a small neighborhood, and weight sharing means the same filter is applied at every position, so the parameter count does not grow with image size and a pattern is detected wherever it appears. Early layers learn edges and textures; deeper layers combine them into parts and objects as the receptive field grows. CNNs remain strong for vision on limited data and on edge devices. A common misconception is that they are fully translation invariant; convolution is translation equivariant, and strided downsampling breaks even that, so small shifts can change predictions.","u":["Image classification and visual defect inspection","Object detection backbones for cameras","Audio classification from spectrograms"],"x":"A manufacturing company trains a compact CNN to detect scratches and dents on parts photographed on an assembly line. The model runs on an edge GPU next to the camera within the line's cycle time, and new defect images flagged by inspectors are added to the training set each month."},"convolution":{"n":"Convolution","d":"Convolution, as used in deep learning, slides a small kernel of learned weights across an input and computes a dot product at each position, producing a feature map that shows where the kernel's pattern appears. Strictly, most libraries compute cross-correlation, without flipping the kernel, but because the weights are learned the difference does not matter.\n\nThe output size depends on kernel size k, padding p and stride s: out = ⌊(n + 2p − k)/s⌋ + 1. Each output channel has its own kernel spanning all input channels, so a layer has k·k·C_in·C_out weights plus biases. Variants include dilated convolutions, which widen the receptive field without extra parameters, depthwise separable convolutions, which cut computation in mobile models, and 1×1 convolutions, which mix channels. Convolutions also apply to 1D signals and 3D volumes. A common error is miscalculating output shapes; padding = k // 2 with stride 1 preserves spatial size for odd kernels.","u":["Feature extraction from images and video frames","Filtering 1D sensor and audio signals","Channel mixing with 1×1 convolutions"],"x":"A predictive maintenance team applies 1D dilated convolutions to vibration signals from industrial pumps. Dilation lets each output see several seconds of history with few layers, keeping inference cheap enough to run on a gateway device that monitors many pumps at once."},"pooling":{"n":"Pooling","d":"Pooling reduces the spatial size of feature maps by summarizing small neighborhoods with a single value, typically the maximum or the average. It has no learned parameters.\n\nMax pooling keeps the strongest response in each window, which preserves whether a feature was detected while discarding its exact position, adding a small degree of robustness to shifts. Average pooling smooths the response. Downsampling also enlarges the receptive field of later layers and reduces computation. Global average pooling collapses each channel to one number, replacing large fully connected layers in modern CNNs and allowing variable input sizes. In sequence models, mean or max pooling over token embeddings produces fixed-size sentence vectors, where padding tokens must be masked out. Many recent architectures replace pooling with strided convolutions, which learn how to downsample. Pooling does discard spatial detail, which hurts dense prediction tasks like segmentation, so U-Net-style models restore it with skip connections.","u":["Downsampling feature maps in CNN backbones","Global average pooling before a classifier head","Building sentence embeddings from token vectors"],"x":"A semantic search team builds sentence embeddings by mean pooling the token outputs of a Transformer encoder, excluding padding tokens with the attention mask. Mean pooling gave more stable retrieval quality than using only the first token's vector in their offline evaluation."},"resnet":{"n":"ResNet","d":"ResNet (Residual Network) is a convolutional architecture that stacks residual blocks, each adding its input to the output of a few convolutional layers. Introduced in 2015, it made networks with more than a hundred layers trainable and won that year's ImageNet competition.\n\nBefore ResNet, simply adding layers often increased training error, a degradation problem caused by optimization difficulty rather than overfitting. Residual blocks fix this because each block only learns a correction F(x) to the identity, and gradients flow directly through the skip paths. ResNet-18 and ResNet-34 use basic blocks of two 3×3 convolutions; ResNet-50 and deeper use bottleneck blocks with 1×1, 3×3 and 1×1 convolutions to reduce cost. When the shape changes, the shortcut uses a strided 1×1 convolution. ResNets remain widely used as backbones for detection, segmentation and transfer learning. Their BatchNorm layers, however, behave differently in training and evaluation mode, so forgetting model.eval() is a frequent source of inconsistent predictions.","u":["Transfer learning backbone for image classification","Feature extractor for detection and segmentation","Baseline architecture for vision benchmarks"],"x":"A retail company fine-tunes an ImageNet-pretrained ResNet-50 to classify product photos into catalog categories. Starting from pretrained weights lets them reach good accuracy with modest labeled data, and they export the model to ONNX for batch inference on new listings."},"rnn":{"n":"Recurrent Neural Network (RNN)","d":"A recurrent neural network processes a sequence one step at a time while carrying a hidden state that summarizes everything seen so far. At each step the same weights combine the current input with the previous hidden state to produce a new hidden state.\n\nWeight sharing across time lets an RNN handle sequences of any length with a fixed number of parameters, and its constant memory per step suits streaming data. Training uses backpropagation through time, which unrolls the network across the sequence. Simple (Elman) RNNs struggle with long-range dependencies because gradients are multiplied by the recurrent Jacobian at every step and tend to vanish or explode; LSTMs and GRUs add gates to address this. RNNs also cannot be parallelized across time steps during training, which is the main reason Transformers replaced them for large-scale language modeling. Modern state space models such as Mamba revisit the recurrent idea with designs that train in parallel.","u":["Streaming sensor and time-series modeling","Character-level text generation for small models","Low-latency on-device sequence processing"],"x":"A wearable device company runs a small RNN on the device to detect activities such as walking or running from accelerometer data. The recurrent state is updated with each new sample, so memory use stays constant and no window of past data needs to be stored."},"lstm":{"n":"LSTM","d":"Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) is a recurrent network cell that adds a separate cell state and three gates to control what is written to memory, what is kept and what is read out. It was designed to learn dependencies across many more time steps than a simple RNN.\n\nThe forget gate decides how much of the previous cell state to keep, the input gate decides how much of the new candidate to add, and the output gate decides how much of the cell state to expose as the hidden state. Because the cell state is updated additively, with the forget gate as a multiplier close to 1 when needed, gradients can flow across long spans without vanishing. LSTMs were the standard for speech recognition, translation and time-series forecasting before Transformers, and remain practical for streaming and small-data sequence tasks. They still process steps sequentially, which slows training on long sequences, and their effective memory is shorter than theory suggests.","u":["Demand and traffic time-series forecasting","Streaming speech and keyword recognition","Anomaly detection in sensor sequences"],"x":"A city transportation team forecasts traffic speed on road segments for the next hour with an LSTM fed the past few hours of speeds, time-of-day features and event flags. The model is retrained weekly, and forecast error is monitored per segment so that drift after road changes triggers a review."},"gru":{"n":"GRU","d":"The Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) is a recurrent cell that uses two gates, update and reset, to control how the hidden state changes over time. It is a simpler alternative to the LSTM, with no separate cell state.\n\nThe update gate interpolates between the previous hidden state and a new candidate state, playing the combined role of the LSTM's forget and input gates. The reset gate decides how much of the previous state is used when computing that candidate, letting the unit ignore irrelevant history. With fewer parameters per unit, roughly three quarters of an LSTM of the same size, GRUs train somewhat faster and often perform comparably, especially on small and medium datasets. Neither cell is consistently better, so the choice is usually made empirically. Like all classic RNNs, GRUs process time steps sequentially and still struggle with very long dependencies. PyTorch's GRU applies the reset gate slightly differently from the original paper.","u":["Session-based next-item recommendation in e-commerce","Lightweight time-series forecasting models","Sequence encoding on resource-limited devices"],"x":"An e-commerce team uses a GRU-based session model to predict the next product a visitor will click from the sequence of items viewed in the current session. The GRU was chosen over an LSTM because it trained faster with similar accuracy, and the hidden state is updated incrementally after each click to keep latency low."},"bidir":{"n":"Bidirectional RNN","d":"A bidirectional RNN runs two recurrent layers over the same sequence, one from start to end and one from end to start, and combines their hidden states at each position. Every output therefore has context from both the past and the future.\n\nThe forward and backward hidden states are usually concatenated, doubling the feature size. This is valuable in tagging tasks where the meaning of a token depends on words that come after it, such as named entity recognition, part-of-speech tagging and speech recognition on complete utterances. Bidirectional LSTMs combined with a CRF layer were the standard sequence-labeling model before Transformer encoders such as BERT, which achieve bidirectional context through self-attention. The key limitation is that the whole sequence must be available before any output is produced, so bidirectional models cannot be used for real-time streaming prediction or for autoregressive generation. Using one for forecasting would leak future information into predictions.","u":["Named entity recognition and part-of-speech tagging","Offline speech recognition on full recordings","Encoding source sentences for translation"],"x":"A legal tech company extracts party names, dates and amounts from contracts with a bidirectional LSTM tagger running on a CPU fleet. Because whole documents are processed offline, the model can use context on both sides of each token, and it is cheaper to serve than a large Transformer."},"seq2seq":{"n":"Seq2Seq","d":"Sequence-to-sequence (Seq2Seq) models map an input sequence to an output sequence of possibly different length using an encoder and a decoder. The encoder reads the input into internal representations, and the decoder generates the output one token at a time, conditioned on those representations and on the tokens it has already produced.\n\nThe original 2014 models used LSTMs and compressed the entire input into one fixed-size vector, which became a bottleneck for long sentences. Attention removed it by letting the decoder look back at all encoder states at every step, and Transformer encoder-decoders such as T5 and BART generalized the design. During training, teacher forcing feeds the ground-truth previous token to the decoder; at inference the model feeds back its own predictions, typically with beam search or sampling. This mismatch, called exposure bias, means early mistakes can compound. Seq2Seq models suit translation, summarization, speech recognition and text-to-SQL, where input and output are distinct sequences.","u":["Machine translation between languages","Abstractive summarization of long documents","Converting natural language questions to SQL"],"x":"A customer support platform fine-tunes a T5-style encoder-decoder to summarize long chat transcripts into short case notes for agents. Generation uses beam search with a length limit, and summaries are spot-checked weekly because the model can occasionally omit key details."},"word2vec":{"n":"Word2Vec","d":"Word2Vec learns dense vector representations of words from raw text by training a shallow network to predict words from their neighbors. Words that occur in similar contexts end up with similar vectors.\n\nIt has two variants. Skip-gram predicts surrounding words from a center word and works well for rare words, while CBOW predicts the center word from the average of its context and trains faster. Instead of a full softmax over the vocabulary, training usually uses negative sampling: the model learns to score real word-context pairs higher than randomly sampled ones. The resulting vectors capture semantic and syntactic regularities, the classic example being that king − man + woman lands near queen. It remains useful for domain-specific vocabularies and item embeddings from sessions. Its main limitation is that each word gets a single static vector regardless of context, so 'bank' has the same embedding in a river or a finance sentence; contextual models like BERT address that.","u":["Domain-specific word embeddings from internal text","Item embeddings from user browsing sessions","Finding similar terms for search query expansion"],"x":"A travel booking site treats each user's sequence of viewed listings as a sentence and trains skip-gram Word2Vec on millions of sessions. The resulting listing embeddings power a similar-listings feature, and the model is retrained daily because new listings need vectors quickly."},"unet":{"n":"U-Net","d":"U-Net is an encoder-decoder convolutional network for dense prediction that connects each encoder level to the matching decoder level with skip connections, giving its characteristic U shape. It was introduced in 2015 for biomedical image segmentation.\n\nThe encoder repeatedly downsamples the image to capture context, and the decoder upsamples back to full resolution. Because downsampling discards fine spatial detail, the skip connections concatenate high-resolution encoder features into the decoder, so the output can draw precise boundaries while still using global context. U-Net trains well on small datasets, especially with heavy augmentation, and outputs a prediction for every pixel. Beyond segmentation in medical imaging, satellite imagery and manufacturing, a U-Net with attention layers and timestep conditioning is the standard noise-prediction network in many diffusion models. A practical pitfall is input size: dimensions should be divisible by 2 to the power of the number of downsampling steps, or features will not align at the skip connections.","u":["Tumor and organ segmentation in medical scans","Building and road extraction from satellite images","Noise prediction backbone in diffusion models"],"x":"A radiology software team trains a U-Net to segment liver lesions in CT slices, combining Dice and cross-entropy loss to handle the small lesion area. Predictions are reviewed by radiologists, and inputs are padded to a multiple of 16 so that skip connections align at every level."},"gnn":{"n":"Graph Neural Network (GNN)","d":"A graph neural network learns representations of nodes, edges or whole graphs by repeatedly passing messages between connected nodes. In each layer, every node aggregates information from its neighbors and combines it with its own features to produce an updated embedding.\n\nAfter k layers, a node's embedding reflects its k-hop neighborhood. Aggregation functions such as sum, mean or attention must be permutation invariant, because neighbors have no natural order. Popular variants include GCN, which uses a normalized adjacency matrix; GraphSAGE, which samples neighbors so that training scales to large graphs; and GAT, which learns attention weights over neighbors. GNNs suit data whose relationships matter as much as features: molecules, social and transaction networks, knowledge graphs and road networks. A key limitation is over-smoothing: with many layers, node embeddings become nearly identical, so most practical GNNs use only two or three layers. Very high-degree nodes also make full-neighborhood aggregation expensive, which is why sampling is common.","u":["Fraud ring detection in transaction networks","Molecular property prediction for drug discovery","Friend and product recommendation on graphs"],"x":"A payments company builds a graph linking accounts, devices and cards, and trains a GraphSAGE model to flag accounts likely to belong to fraud rings. Neighbor sampling keeps training feasible on a very large graph, and embeddings are recomputed hourly so newly linked accounts are scored quickly."},"vit":{"n":"Vision Transformer (ViT)","d":"The Vision Transformer applies a standard Transformer encoder to images by splitting each image into fixed-size patches and treating each patch as a token. A typical setup cuts a 224×224 image into 16×16 patches, giving 196 tokens.\n\nEach patch is flattened and linearly projected to an embedding, a learned position embedding is added, and a special class token is often prepended whose final state feeds the classifier. Self-attention lets every patch interact with every other patch from the first layer, so the model captures global relationships directly instead of building them up through local convolutions. ViTs have weaker built-in assumptions about locality than CNNs, so they need large pretraining datasets or strong augmentation to match CNNs, but they scale very well and serve as the image encoders of CLIP and many multimodal language models. Attention cost grows quadratically with the number of patches, so high-resolution inputs are expensive, and changing the resolution requires interpolating the position embeddings.","u":["Image classification with large pretrained backbones","Image encoder for multimodal language models","Self-supervised visual features for retrieval"],"x":"A retail company uses a self-supervised ViT backbone to embed product photos for visual similar-item search. The pretrained features work across categories without labels, and the team resizes all images to the pretraining resolution to avoid degraded quality from interpolated position embeddings."},"clip":{"n":"CLIP","d":"CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining), released by OpenAI in 2021, trains an image encoder and a text encoder together so that matching images and captions map to nearby vectors in a shared embedding space. It was trained on hundreds of millions of image-text pairs collected from the web.\n\nFor a batch of N pairs, the model computes all N×N cosine similarities and applies a symmetric cross-entropy loss that pushes each image toward its own caption and away from the other captions, and vice versa, with a learned temperature. After training, zero-shot classification works by embedding prompts such as 'a photo of a dog' for each class and picking the class whose text embedding is closest to the image. CLIP embeddings power text-to-image search, content filtering and the text conditioning of image generators. Limitations include weak counting and spatial reasoning, sensitivity to prompt wording, and social biases inherited from web data.","u":["Zero-shot image classification with text prompts","Text-to-image and image-to-image search","Filtering and deduplicating large image datasets"],"x":"A stock photo platform embeds its image library with a CLIP image encoder and stores the vectors in an approximate nearest-neighbor index. User text queries are embedded with the text encoder at search time, enabling natural-language search without manually written tags."},"contrastive":{"n":"Contrastive Learning","d":"Contrastive learning trains an encoder to place related examples, called positive pairs, close together in embedding space and unrelated examples, called negatives, far apart. The positives can be two augmented views of the same image, a query and its relevant document, or an image and its caption.\n\nThe most common objective is InfoNCE: for each anchor, a softmax over similarities to one positive and many negatives is trained to pick the positive, with a temperature controlling how sharply hard negatives are penalized. Methods like SimCLR and MoCo learn visual features without labels, while CLIP and dense retrieval models learn cross-modal or query-document embeddings. In-batch negatives make training efficient, so larger batches usually help. The approach is powerful because labels are often free, but quality depends heavily on how positives are defined: augmentations determine which invariances are learned, and false negatives, such as near-duplicate documents treated as negatives, can degrade results.","u":["Training dense retrievers for semantic search","Self-supervised pretraining of image encoders","Learning user and item embeddings for recommendation"],"x":"An enterprise search team fine-tunes a sentence embedding model with in-batch negatives on pairs of user questions and the help articles that resolved them. They mine hard negatives from the current retriever's top results, but filter out near-duplicates of the true article to avoid false negatives."},"gan":{"n":"Generative Adversarial Network (GAN)","d":"A generative adversarial network trains two networks against each other: a generator that turns random noise into synthetic samples, and a discriminator that tries to tell real samples from generated ones. As the discriminator improves, the generator receives gradients that push its outputs toward the real data distribution.\n\nThe setup is a two-player minimax game. In practice the generator usually maximizes log D(G(z)), the non-saturating loss, because the original objective gives vanishing gradients early in training. GANs produce sharp images and sample in a single forward pass, so they are fast at inference; StyleGAN set the standard for photorealistic faces. They are used for image synthesis, super-resolution and data augmentation. Training is notoriously unstable: mode collapse, where the generator covers only a few modes of the data, and oscillation between the two players are common. Techniques such as spectral normalization, Wasserstein losses with gradient penalties and careful learning-rate balancing help. Diffusion models have largely replaced GANs for high-diversity image generation.","u":["Image super-resolution and enhancement","Synthetic training images for rare defect classes","Fast single-step image generation at inference"],"x":"A camera software team uses a GAN-based super-resolution model to upscale low-light photos on device. The adversarial loss produces sharper textures than a pure pixel loss, and the single forward pass fits the latency budget; the team watches closely for invented details that do not exist in the scene."},"vae":{"n":"Variational Autoencoder (VAE)","d":"A variational autoencoder is a generative model that encodes each input into a probability distribution over a latent space, rather than a single point, and decodes samples from that distribution back into data. Because the latent space is regularized toward a simple prior, sampling from the prior and decoding yields new data.\n\nThe encoder outputs a mean and variance for each latent dimension. The reparameterization trick, z = μ + σ·ε with ε drawn from a standard normal, keeps sampling differentiable so the model trains with backpropagation. The loss, the negative evidence lower bound (ELBO), combines a reconstruction term with a KL divergence that pulls each encoded distribution toward a standard normal. VAEs give smooth latent spaces useful for interpolation, anomaly detection and compression, and latent diffusion uses a VAE variant. Their samples tend to be blurrier than those of GANs or diffusion models, and with powerful decoders the KL term can collapse, leaving the latent code unused.","u":["Anomaly detection from reconstruction error","Compressing images into latents for diffusion","Generating and interpolating molecular structures"],"x":"An industrial monitoring team trains a VAE on sensor windows from machines operating normally. In production, windows with high reconstruction error or low ELBO are flagged as anomalies, and thresholds are set on a validation period so that the alert rate stays manageable for operators."},"diffusion":{"n":"Diffusion Models","d":"Diffusion models generate data by learning to reverse a gradual noising process. During training, Gaussian noise is added to real samples at a randomly chosen noise level, and a network learns to predict that noise; generation starts from pure noise and removes it step by step.\n\nThe forward process has a closed form, so a noisy sample at any timestep t can be produced directly from the clean data using a schedule ᾱₜ. The training objective is a simple mean squared error between the true and predicted noise, which is far more stable than adversarial training. Sampling runs the denoiser many times; DDPM uses hundreds of steps, while DDIM and modern solvers reduce this to a few dozen, and distillation can reach a few steps. Classifier-free guidance strengthens adherence to a text prompt at some cost to diversity. They lead in image, video and audio generation quality. Their main drawback is slow, compute-heavy sampling compared with single-pass generators.","u":["Text-to-image generation and editing","Audio and speech synthesis","Molecule and protein structure generation"],"x":"A design tool company offers text-to-image generation with a diffusion model served on GPUs. They use a fast sampler with about twenty to thirty steps and classifier-free guidance, tuning the guidance scale to balance prompt fidelity against image variety, and run safety classifiers on every output."},"latentdiff":{"n":"Latent Diffusion","d":"Latent diffusion runs the diffusion process in the compressed latent space of a pretrained autoencoder instead of directly on pixels. Stable Diffusion is the best-known example.\n\nA variational autoencoder first compresses an image, for instance 512×512×3 pixels into a 64×64×4 latent, an 8× reduction per side. The diffusion model, typically a U-Net or a diffusion Transformer, learns to denoise these latents, and the decoder turns the final latent back into an image. Because the latent is far smaller and the autoencoder has already removed imperceptible high-frequency detail, training and sampling cost much less compute than pixel-space diffusion at similar quality. Text conditioning enters through cross-attention to embeddings from a text encoder such as CLIP or T5. The approach also supports image-to-image editing, inpainting and fine-tuning methods like LoRA and DreamBooth. Its quality ceiling is set by the autoencoder: small text, fine faces and precise textures can be distorted by the decoder even when the diffusion model is perfect.","u":["Text-to-image generation on commodity GPUs","Inpainting and image-to-image editing","Custom styles through LoRA fine-tuning"],"x":"A marketing team fine-tunes a Stable Diffusion model with LoRA on approved brand imagery to generate campaign concepts. Because diffusion runs in latent space, both training and generation fit on a single GPU, and all outputs go through human review before use."},"flowmatch":{"n":"Flow Matching","d":"Flow matching trains a generative model to learn a velocity field that continuously transports samples from a simple noise distribution to the data distribution. Generation then integrates this field as an ordinary differential equation from noise to data.\n\nIn the common rectified flow variant, a training example is built by drawing a data point x₁ and a noise point x₀, interpolating linearly xₜ = (1−t)x₀ + t·x₁ at a random time t, and regressing the network output onto the constant velocity x₁ − x₀. The loss is a plain mean squared error, with no adversarial game and no ODE simulation during training. Straight paths allow sampling in fewer steps than classic diffusion, and the framework generalizes diffusion, which uses curved paths. Stable Diffusion 3 and several video and speech models use it. The learned trajectories are not exactly straight: the velocity field averages over crossing pairs, so paths curve unless rectified further.","u":["Text-to-image generation with fewer sampling steps","Speech and audio generation models","Molecular and protein conformation generation"],"x":"A speech synthesis team replaces its diffusion-based acoustic decoder with a flow matching model. The training loss is simpler and more stable, and an ODE solver with a small number of steps produces comparable audio quality at lower latency for real-time voice responses."},"autoregressive":{"n":"Autoregressive Models","d":"An autoregressive model generates a sequence one element at a time, predicting each element from all the elements before it. It factorizes the joint probability of a sequence into a product of conditional probabilities using the chain rule.\n\nGPT-style language models are the most prominent example: a causal Transformer predicts a distribution over the next token, a token is chosen by greedy decoding or sampling, appended to the input, and the process repeats. Training is efficient because, with causal masking and teacher forcing, the losses for all positions in a sequence are computed in one parallel pass. The same idea underlies classical AR models for time series, PixelCNN for images and WaveNet for audio. Autoregressive models give exact likelihoods and are very flexible, but generation is inherently sequential, so latency grows with output length; KV caching and speculative decoding reduce this cost. Errors can also compound, because each prediction is conditioned on the model's own earlier outputs.","u":["Text generation with GPT-style language models","Time-series forecasting with AR and ARIMA models","Code completion in developer tools"],"x":"A developer tools company serves an autoregressive code model for inline completion. Because tokens are generated one at a time, they cap completion length, reuse the KV cache across keystrokes and use speculative decoding with a small draft model to keep suggestions within an interactive latency budget."},"dlm":{"n":"Diffusion Language Models","d":"Diffusion language models generate text by starting from a fully masked or noised sequence and refining many positions in parallel over several steps, instead of producing one token at a time from left to right. Most use discrete masking rather than Gaussian noise.\n\nDuring training, a random fraction of tokens is replaced with a mask token, and a bidirectional Transformer learns to predict the original tokens at the masked positions, with the loss weighted according to the masking ratio. At inference the model begins with an all-mask sequence, predicts every position, keeps the most confident predictions and re-masks the rest, repeating until the text is complete. Examples include LLaDA, Mercury and Gemini Diffusion. Committing several tokens per step with full context visible enables faster decoding and editing text in the middle. Limitations include fixed or estimated output lengths, difficulty reusing standard KV caching, and quality that still trails comparable autoregressive models on many benchmarks.","u":["Low-latency parallel text and code generation","Text infilling and editing in the middle","Research on non-autoregressive decoding"],"x":"A coding assistant team experiments with a diffusion language model for fast code infilling, where the model fills a gap between existing code before and after the cursor. Parallel decoding reduces latency for short spans, but they keep an autoregressive model as a fallback for long completions where its quality is still higher."},"attention":{"n":"Attention Mechanism","d":"Attention lets a model compute each output as a weighted average of a set of value vectors, where the weights depend on how well a query matches each corresponding key. The model can thus focus on the most relevant parts of an input.\n\nIn scaled dot-product attention, query-key dot products are divided by the square root of the key dimension, which keeps the softmax from saturating, and then normalized with softmax to weights that sum to 1. Attention was introduced to let translation decoders look back at all encoder states instead of one compressed vector, and it is the core operation of Transformers. Cross-attention uses queries from one sequence and keys and values from another, as in encoder-decoder models or text-conditioned image generation. Standard attention costs time and memory quadratic in sequence length; FlashAttention reduces memory by computing it in tiles without storing the full weight matrix. Attention weights are not a reliable explanation of model decisions.","u":["Aligning source and target words in translation","Cross-attention from text prompts to image features","Pooling variable-length inputs with learned weights"],"x":"A machine translation team serves an encoder-decoder Transformer in which each decoder layer cross-attends to the encoded source sentence. They use a fused attention kernel to cut GPU memory, which allows larger batches and higher throughput on long documents."},"selfattn":{"n":"Self-Attention","d":"Self-attention is attention in which the queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence, so every token builds its new representation by looking at every other token in that sequence. It is the main building block of Transformer layers.\n\nEach token's vector is projected by three learned matrices into a query, a key and a value. The query of token i is compared with the keys of all tokens to produce attention weights, and token i's output is the weighted sum of their values. Multi-head self-attention runs several such operations in parallel on lower-dimensional projections, so heads can track different relationships. Unlike an RNN, any two positions interact in a single step, and the whole sequence is processed in parallel. Self-attention is permutation equivariant, so it needs positional encodings to know word order. Decoder models add a causal mask so each token sees only earlier positions. Cost grows quadratically with sequence length.","u":["Contextual token representations in language models","Modeling long-range dependencies in documents","Relating image patches in Vision Transformers"],"x":"A legal document team fine-tunes a Transformer encoder whose self-attention layers let each clause attend to definitions elsewhere in the contract. Because cost grows quadratically with length, they split contracts into overlapping windows of a fixed token count and merge predictions afterward."},"qkv":{"n":"Query, Key, Value (Q, K, V)","d":"Queries, keys and values are three learned linear projections of the same token representations that let attention decide which tokens to read from and what to read. Each token's query expresses what it is looking for, each key expresses what that token offers for matching, and each value carries the content that is actually passed along. The dot product of a query with every key gives a relevance score; a softmax turns the scores into weights, and the output is the weighted sum of the values.\n\nSeparating matching (Q·K) from content (V) is what makes attention flexible: two tokens can be highly relevant to each other while contributing information unrelated to the features used for matching. W_Q, W_K and W_V are ordinary learned weight matrices. Keys and values need not come from a different source than queries: in self-attention all three come from the same sequence, and only cross-attention draws keys and values from a different one.","u":["Self-attention inside every Transformer layer","Retrieval-style lookup over memory slots","Inspecting which tokens a model attends to"],"x":"A search team debugging a fine-tuned BERT ranking model inspects query-key scores in the last layers to see which document tokens drive each relevance decision. The analysis helps them spot when the model matches on boilerplate text such as page headers instead of the content that answers the query."},"mha":{"n":"Multi-Head Attention","d":"Multi-head attention runs several attention operations in parallel on lower-dimensional projections of the input and concatenates their results. The model dimension d_model is split into h heads of size d_model/h; each head has its own query, key and value projections, computes scaled dot-product attention independently, and the concatenated outputs pass through a final linear projection W_O.\n\nBecause each head works in its own subspace, different heads can specialize: some track syntactic dependencies, some attend to the previous token, others copy rare names from far away. The total compute is about the same as a single full-width head, since each head is narrower. Multiple heads are therefore a way to get several attention patterns per layer at no extra cost. A frequent misunderstanding is that every head learns a distinct, interpretable role; in practice many heads are redundant, and studies have shown that a sizable fraction can be pruned after training with little loss in accuracy.","u":["Core sublayer of Transformer encoders and decoders","Capturing several dependency types per layer","Vision Transformers attending across image patches"],"x":"A machine translation team trains an encoder-decoder Transformer with 16 heads per layer for a high-traffic language pair. After training, they measure each head's contribution and remove redundant heads to cut decoding latency, then confirm on a held-out set that translation quality stays within tolerance."},"crossattn":{"n":"Cross-Attention","d":"Cross-attention is attention in which the queries come from one sequence and the keys and values come from another, letting one stream read information from a second source. In an encoder-decoder Transformer, each decoder layer projects its own hidden states into queries and attends over keys and values projected from the encoder output, so every generated token can look at the whole input.\n\nThe same mechanism conditions generation on other modalities. Text-to-image diffusion models use cross-attention so that image latents (queries) attend to text-encoder embeddings (keys and values), and some vision-language models insert cross-attention layers so text tokens can attend to image features. The output length always matches the query sequence, while the key and value sequence can have any length. One practical limitation is cost: the score matrix is query length times source length, so very long source contexts, such as many retrieved documents, make each cross-attention layer expensive in memory and compute.","u":["Decoder attending to encoder output in translation","Conditioning diffusion image generation on text prompts","Speech recognition decoders reading audio features"],"x":"A design tool company serves a latent diffusion model in which U-Net layers use cross-attention to read CLIP text embeddings of the user's prompt. Because the text embedding is fixed for a request, its keys and values can be computed once and reused across all denoising steps to reduce latency."},"causalmask":{"n":"Causal Mask","d":"A causal mask prevents each position in a sequence from attending to positions that come after it, so a model's prediction for token t depends only on tokens 1 to t. It is implemented by adding negative infinity to the attention scores above the diagonal of the T×T score matrix before the softmax, which sets those attention weights to exactly zero.\n\nThe mask is what makes parallel training of autoregressive models possible. With it, a decoder-only model processes an entire sequence in one forward pass and computes the next-token loss at every position simultaneously, without any position seeing the token it must predict. At inference the same rule holds naturally, because future tokens do not exist yet. A common mistake when writing custom attention is to combine the causal mask incorrectly with a padding mask, or to use a large finite negative number in low precision, where it can overflow or fail to zero out the weights.","u":["Training GPT-style decoder-only language models","Autoregressive decoders in encoder-decoder models","Preventing future leakage in sequence forecasting"],"x":"A team pretraining a code completion model packs many files into fixed-length sequences and applies a causal mask so every position is trained to predict the next token in parallel. They also add document boundaries to the mask so tokens from one file cannot attend to an unrelated file packed before it."},"posenc":{"n":"Positional Encoding","d":"Positional encoding injects information about token order into a Transformer, which would otherwise treat its input as an unordered set because attention is permutation-equivariant. The original Transformer adds a fixed sinusoidal vector to each token embedding: each pair of dimensions is a sine and cosine at a different frequency, ranging from fast oscillations to very slow ones, so every position gets a unique pattern.\n\nA useful property of sinusoids is that the encoding of position p + k is a linear function (a rotation) of the encoding of position p, which makes relative offsets easy for the model to use. Many models such as BERT and GPT-2 instead learn an absolute position embedding table. Learned tables cannot represent positions beyond the maximum length seen in training, and sinusoidal encodings also extrapolate poorly in practice. This limitation is one reason modern LLMs moved to relative schemes such as RoPE and ALiBi.","u":["Adding order information to Transformer inputs","Encoding timesteps in diffusion models","Representing patch positions in Vision Transformers"],"x":"An image generation team encodes the diffusion timestep with a sinusoidal embedding followed by a small MLP, the same construction used for token positions. Because the function is fixed and smooth, the model can be sampled with step schedules different from the ones used in training."},"rope":{"n":"Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE)","d":"Rotary Position Embedding encodes position by rotating query and key vectors by an angle proportional to their position, so that their dot product depends only on the relative distance between tokens. Each pair of dimensions in a query or key is treated as a 2D point and rotated by p·θᵢ, where p is the position and θᵢ is a frequency that decreases across pairs. Values are not rotated.\n\nSince rotating q by angle a and k by angle b changes their dot product only through the difference a − b, attention scores become a function of content and relative offset, without any extra parameters or learned tables. RoPE is applied inside every attention layer rather than added once at the input; LLaMA, Mistral and Qwen all use it. A known limitation is poor extrapolation beyond the trained context length; methods such as position interpolation, NTK-aware scaling and YaRN rescale the frequencies to extend context, usually with brief fine-tuning.","u":["Relative positions in decoder-only LLMs","Extending context length with frequency scaling","Position encoding without extra learned parameters"],"x":"A team extending an open LLM from 8K to 64K context for long-document question answering rescales the RoPE base frequency and fine-tunes briefly on long sequences. They validate on retrieval-style tests that place a key fact at varying depths to confirm the model still uses distant tokens."},"alibi":{"n":"ALiBi (Attention with Linear Biases)","d":"ALiBi encodes position by subtracting a penalty proportional to the distance between query and key directly from the attention scores, instead of adding position vectors to the embeddings. For head h, the score between positions i and j receives a bias of −m_h·(i − j), where m_h is a fixed, head-specific slope taken from a geometric sequence such as 1/2, 1/4, 1/8 and so on.\n\nThe effect is a built-in recency preference: distant tokens are down-weighted, more strongly in heads with steep slopes and only gently in heads with shallow slopes, so different heads cover different ranges. ALiBi has no learned parameters and was proposed to let models trained on short sequences run on longer ones at inference, which it does better than sinusoidal or learned absolute encodings. The limitation is that the linear penalty biases attention toward nearby context, and in practice extrapolation helps perplexity more than it helps retrieving specific facts from far back in the sequence.","u":["Length extrapolation beyond training context","Position encoding with zero parameters","Language models such as BLOOM and MPT"],"x":"The BLOOM and MPT open model families use ALiBi instead of positional embeddings. A team serving such a model can raise the inference context somewhat beyond the training length without retraining, but they still benchmark long-input tasks because quality degrades gradually as length grows."},"transformer":{"n":"Transformer Block","d":"A Transformer block is the repeating unit of a Transformer: a multi-head attention sublayer followed by a position-wise feed-forward network, each wrapped with a residual connection and layer normalization. Attention mixes information across tokens, while the feed-forward network processes each token independently, and the residual connections keep a direct path for information and gradients through deep stacks.\n\nMost modern LLMs use the pre-norm arrangement, where the normalization is applied to the input of each sublayer (x + Attn(Norm(x))), which trains more stably than the original post-norm design. Typical modern variants replace LayerNorm with RMSNorm, use a SwiGLU feed-forward network, and apply RoPE inside attention. Blocks are stacked dozens of times with identical shapes, which makes the architecture easy to scale and parallelize. A common misconception is that attention holds most of the parameters; in standard configurations the feed-forward network holds about two thirds of each block's weights.","u":["Building blocks of LLMs and BERT-style encoders","Vision Transformers for image classification","Sequence models for audio, proteins, time series"],"x":"A speech company builds its recognition encoder from a stack of identical Transformer blocks, which lets the team scale model size by changing only depth and width. Because each block has the same structure, they can apply tensor parallelism and activation checkpointing uniformly across the stack during training."},"encoderonly":{"n":"Encoder-Only Models (BERT)","d":"Encoder-only models are Transformers that read the whole input with bidirectional self-attention and output a contextual vector for every token, rather than generating text. BERT is the best-known example. Because no causal mask is applied, each token's representation depends on words both before and after it, which gives rich context for understanding tasks.\n\nThey are pretrained with objectives that do not require left-to-right generation, mainly masked language modeling, where randomly hidden tokens must be reconstructed. For downstream use, a small head is placed on top: a classifier on the [CLS] or mean-pooled vector for sentence classification, a per-token classifier for named entity recognition, or a span predictor for extractive question answering. Encoder-only models are compact and fast for classification, retrieval embeddings and reranking. Their main limitation is that they are not natural text generators; producing fluent free-form output requires a decoder, which is why chat assistants are built on decoder-only models.","u":["Text classification and sentiment analysis","Named entity recognition and token tagging","Dense retrieval embeddings and cross-encoder reranking"],"x":"Google has publicly described using BERT in Search to better understand the intent of queries, especially those where small words such as prepositions change the meaning. Many companies run similar distilled BERT encoders for intent classification because they fit tight CPU latency budgets."},"decoderonly":{"n":"Decoder-Only Models (GPT)","d":"Decoder-only models are Transformers that use causal self-attention and are trained to predict the next token, so they generate text one token at a time conditioned on everything before it. The GPT series, LLaMA, Mistral, Qwen and most current chat assistants follow this design. The prompt and the generated output live in a single sequence, with no separate encoder.\n\nTheir appeal is simplicity and generality: one objective, next-token prediction on large text corpora, scales well and turns many tasks into text continuation, including translation, summarization, coding and question answering via prompting or instruction tuning. During generation a KV cache stores keys and values of past tokens so each new step costs time proportional to the context length rather than recomputing everything. The main limitations are sequential decoding, which bounds latency per token, and the fact that each token's representation sees only the left context, which can make them less parameter-efficient than bidirectional encoders for pure classification or embedding tasks.","u":["Chat assistants and instruction-following models","Code completion and generation","Few-shot task solving through prompting"],"x":"A customer support platform fine-tunes an open decoder-only model to draft replies from ticket history and help-center articles. They serve it with continuous batching and a KV cache, and track time to first token separately from per-token speed because long prompts dominate the first."},"encdec":{"n":"Encoder–Decoder Models (T5)","d":"Encoder-decoder models pair a bidirectional encoder that reads the full input with an autoregressive decoder that generates the output while attending to the encoder through cross-attention. This is the original Transformer layout from machine translation, and T5, BART and Whisper use it as well.\n\nThe split suits tasks with a clear input and a distinct output, such as translation, summarization and speech recognition. The encoder processes the source once with full context, and the decoder only has to model the target sequence, which is often shorter. T5 reframed every NLP task as text-to-text, with a task prefix in the input string, and was pretrained with span corruption, where the decoder reconstructs masked spans of the input. A common misconception is that encoder-decoder models are obsolete; they remain competitive and efficient for conditional generation at moderate scale, but decoder-only models are simpler to scale for open-ended chat, which explains their dominance in general-purpose LLMs.","u":["Machine translation between language pairs","Abstractive summarization of long news articles","Speech-to-text transcription with Whisper models"],"x":"OpenAI's Whisper uses an encoder over audio spectrograms and a text decoder that cross-attends to it. A media company running Whisper for subtitle generation benefits from encoding each 30-second audio chunk once while the decoder produces the transcript token by token."},"ffn":{"n":"Feed-Forward Network (FFN)","d":"The feed-forward network in a Transformer is a two-layer MLP applied to each token independently, expanding the hidden size, applying a nonlinearity and projecting back. In the original design the inner dimension is four times d_model with a ReLU; modern LLMs typically use SwiGLU, a gated variant with three weight matrices where one projection, passed through SiLU, multiplies another elementwise.\n\nAttention moves information between tokens, while the FFN transforms each token's representation in place. Interpretability research suggests FFN layers behave partly like key-value memories that store factual and lexical associations. They account for roughly two thirds of the parameters in a standard block, so they dominate model size and much of the compute. This is also why Mixture-of-Experts models replace the FFN with many expert FFNs and route each token to a few of them. When using SwiGLU, the inner dimension is usually reduced to about 8/3·d_model to keep the parameter count comparable to a 4× ReLU FFN.","u":["Per-token transformation inside every Transformer block","Expert layers in Mixture-of-Experts models","Storing factual associations learned in pretraining"],"x":"A team designing an in-house LLM chooses a SwiGLU FFN with an inner size of about 8/3 of the hidden size, following LLaMA, to keep the parameter budget fixed. They round the inner dimension up to a multiple of 256 so the matrix multiplications map efficiently onto GPU tensor cores."},"prenorm":{"n":"Pre-Norm vs Post-Norm","d":"Pre-norm and post-norm differ in where layer normalization sits relative to the residual connection in a Transformer block. Post-norm, used in the original Transformer and BERT, normalizes after the residual addition: x ← Norm(x + F(x)). Pre-norm, used in GPT-2 and nearly all modern LLMs, normalizes only the input to each sublayer: x ← x + F(Norm(x)).\n\nThe placement changes gradient flow. In pre-norm the residual stream is an unnormalized identity path from input to output, so gradients reach early layers directly, and deep models train stably without a long learning-rate warmup. Post-norm puts a normalization on that path at every layer, which can cause vanishing or exploding gradients in deep stacks and makes training sensitive to warmup. The trade-off is that pre-norm often reaches slightly worse final quality at equal depth, because the residual stream grows and later layers contribute relatively less. Variants such as DeepNorm and adding a final norm after the last block address these issues.","u":["Stabilizing training of very deep Transformers","Reducing dependence on learning-rate warmup","Choosing block layout for new LLM architectures"],"x":"A research team scaling a model from 24 to 80 layers sees loss spikes with post-norm and switches to pre-norm with RMSNorm plus a final norm before the output head. Training becomes stable at a higher peak learning rate, which they verify by monitoring gradient norms per layer."},"tokenization":{"n":"Tokenization","d":"Tokenization converts raw text into a sequence of integer ids from a fixed vocabulary, which is the only form of input a language model actually sees. Modern LLMs use subword tokenizers such as BPE, WordPiece or Unigram, which keep frequent words as single tokens and split rare words into smaller pieces, so any string can be represented without unknown tokens.\n\nThe tokenizer is trained before the model and frozen afterward, and it shapes many model properties: sequence length and therefore cost, how numbers and code are split, and how fairly different languages are served. A text in a language underrepresented in the tokenizer's training data may need several times more tokens for the same content, which raises cost and shortens the effective context. A common pitfall is mixing tokenizers and models, or ignoring special tokens and chat templates; a fine-tuned model can behave badly if prompts are not formatted and tokenized exactly as in training.","u":["Preparing text inputs for any language model","Estimating API cost and context usage","Building vocabularies for new languages or domains"],"x":"A company deploying an LLM for Korean and English customer chats measures tokens per character for each language before choosing a base model. A tokenizer with poor Korean coverage would substantially raise serving cost and cut effective context, so token efficiency becomes a selection criterion alongside accuracy."},"bpe":{"n":"Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE)","d":"Byte-Pair Encoding builds a subword vocabulary by starting from single characters or bytes and repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pair into a new token until a target vocabulary size is reached. The ordered list of merges is the tokenizer: to encode new text, the same merges are applied in the order they were learned.\n\nFrequent words end up as single tokens, while rare words and misspellings decompose into common pieces, which balances vocabulary size against sequence length. Byte-level BPE, used by GPT-2 and many later models, starts from the 256 possible byte values, so any Unicode string can be encoded with no unknown token. Vocabulary size is a key design choice: larger vocabularies shorten sequences but enlarge the embedding and output layers. A known weakness is that merges are driven purely by frequency, so number splitting can be inconsistent, for example a number becoming one token while a neighboring value splits in two, which can hurt arithmetic.","u":["Tokenizers for GPT and LLaMA-family models","Open-vocabulary handling of rare words","Training custom tokenizers for code or domains"],"x":"A team building a code model trains a byte-level BPE tokenizer on their source corpus so that common indentation runs and keywords become single tokens. This shortens typical files compared with a general-purpose tokenizer, which lowers training cost and lets more code fit in the context window."},"mlm":{"n":"Masked Language Modeling (MLM)","d":"Masked language modeling is a pretraining objective in which some input tokens are hidden and the model learns to predict them from the surrounding context on both sides. In BERT, 15% of token positions are selected; of those, 80% are replaced with a [MASK] token, 10% with a random token and 10% are left unchanged, and the loss is computed only on the selected positions.\n\nBecause the model sees both left and right context, it learns strong bidirectional representations that transfer well to classification, tagging and retrieval. The 80/10/10 split exists because [MASK] never appears at fine-tuning time; mixing in random and unchanged tokens forces the model to build useful representations for every token, not only masked ones. A key limitation is efficiency: only about 15% of tokens produce a training signal per pass, which is why ELECTRA's replaced-token detection, which learns from every position, can be more compute-efficient. MLM also does not directly yield a left-to-right text generator.","u":["Pretraining BERT and RoBERTa encoders","Domain-adaptive pretraining on in-domain text","Learning protein and genomic sequence representations"],"x":"A legal technology company continues MLM pretraining of a RoBERTa model on contracts before fine-tuning a clause classifier. The extra domain pretraining helps the model handle legal vocabulary and boilerplate that rarely appear in general web text."},"nexttoken":{"n":"Next-Token Prediction","d":"Next-token prediction trains a language model to output a probability distribution over the vocabulary for the token that follows a given prefix, using cross-entropy against the actual next token. Summed over all positions, this maximizes the likelihood of the training text under the chain rule of probability.\n\nWith a causal mask, a single forward pass yields a prediction at every position, so each sequence of length T provides T training signals; the inputs are the tokens and the targets are the same tokens shifted left by one. This simple, self-supervised objective needs no labels and scales to trillions of tokens, and it underlies the pretraining of GPT-style models. Doing it well requires modeling grammar, facts and reasoning patterns present in the data. A common misconception is that the objective only teaches surface statistics; at the same time, it optimizes for plausible continuations rather than truthfulness, so models also need post-training to follow instructions and avoid confident errors.","u":["Pretraining decoder-only language models","Continued pretraining on domain corpora","Scoring sequence likelihood and perplexity"],"x":"A financial data provider continues next-token pretraining of an open model on filings and analyst reports before instruction tuning. They track validation perplexity on held-out filings and on general text to make sure domain gains do not come with large losses in general ability."},"kvcache":{"n":"KV Cache","d":"A KV cache stores the key and value vectors of all previously processed tokens during autoregressive generation, so each new token only needs to compute its own query, key and value instead of reprocessing the whole sequence. The new query attends over the cached keys and values, and the new key and value are appended to the cache.\n\nWithout caching, every step would recompute keys and values for the entire prefix, making total generation cost grow roughly quadratically in the projections and cubically in attention; with it, each decoding step does work proportional to the current length. The cost moves to memory: the cache size is 2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × sequence length × batch size × bytes per value, which for long contexts and large batches can exceed the model weights. This memory, not compute, often limits serving throughput, which motivates grouped-query attention, cache quantization, and paged memory management as in vLLM's PagedAttention.","u":["Fast autoregressive decoding in LLM serving","Prefix caching for shared system prompts","Capacity planning for long-context inference"],"x":"An LLM serving team uses vLLM, whose PagedAttention stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks to reduce fragmentation and allow sharing of common prompt prefixes. They size GPU memory by computing cache bytes per token for the model and set the maximum batch and context so the cache fits."},"flashattn":{"n":"FlashAttention","d":"FlashAttention is an exact attention algorithm that computes the same result as standard attention but never writes the full T×T score matrix to GPU high-bandwidth memory. It splits queries, keys and values into tiles that fit in fast on-chip SRAM, computes partial softmax results tile by tile, and combines them using an online softmax that tracks a running maximum and normalizer for each row.\n\nStandard attention is limited by memory traffic rather than arithmetic: reading and writing the large score and probability matrices dominates runtime. By fusing the matmul, masking, softmax and the multiplication by V into one kernel, FlashAttention reduces memory use from quadratic to linear in sequence length and gives large wall-clock speedups, especially for long contexts. In the backward pass it recomputes attention tiles instead of storing them. A common misconception is that it is an approximation; it is mathematically exact and lowers memory, but it does not change the quadratic number of operations.","u":["Speeding up LLM training on long sequences","Reducing attention memory during inference prefill","Enabling longer context on fixed GPU memory"],"x":"A team pretraining a 7B model switches its attention implementation to FlashAttention through PyTorch's scaled_dot_product_attention. The lower activation memory lets them double sequence length without changing the batch size, and they confirm numerically that outputs match the reference implementation within bf16 tolerance."},"mqa":{"n":"Multi-Query Attention (MQA)","d":"Multi-query attention keeps many query heads but shares a single key head and a single value head across all of them. Each query head still computes its own attention pattern, but all heads read from the same keys and values.\n\nThe motivation is inference speed. During autoregressive decoding, each step must load the entire KV cache from memory, and that memory traffic, not arithmetic, usually limits throughput. With h query heads, MQA shrinks the KV cache and its bandwidth cost by a factor of h compared with standard multi-head attention, which allows larger batches and longer contexts on the same hardware. It was used in models such as PaLM and Falcon. The trade-off is quality and training stability: sharing one key-value head reduces model capacity, and converting an existing multi-head model to MQA typically requires additional training. Grouped-query attention was introduced as a middle ground that recovers most of the quality while keeping most of the savings.","u":["Reducing KV-cache memory in LLM serving","Higher decoding throughput at large batch sizes","Long-context inference on limited GPU memory"],"x":"A team serving a chat model on memory-constrained GPUs chooses an MQA architecture so the KV cache for each conversation is a fraction of the multi-head size. The smaller cache lets each GPU hold many more concurrent sessions, at the cost of a small quality gap they track on internal benchmarks."},"gqa":{"n":"Grouped-Query Attention (GQA)","d":"Grouped-query attention divides the query heads into groups and gives each group its own shared key and value head, sitting between multi-head attention (one KV head per query head) and multi-query attention (one KV head for all). With 32 query heads and 8 KV heads, for example, every 4 query heads share one key-value pair.\n\nThe KV cache shrinks by the ratio of query heads to KV heads, which cuts the memory bandwidth needed per decoding step and allows larger batches or longer contexts. Experiments in the original paper showed that GQA reaches quality close to full multi-head attention with speed close to MQA, and existing multi-head checkpoints can be converted by mean-pooling key and value heads within each group and then briefly continuing training. GQA is now standard in LLaMA 2 70B and later LLaMA models, Mistral and many other LLMs. It reduces cache size but not the attention compute for the prefill of long prompts.","u":["Standard attention layout in modern open LLMs","Shrinking KV cache with minimal quality loss","Uptraining multi-head checkpoints for faster inference"],"x":"Meta's LLaMA 2 70B and the LLaMA 3 models use grouped-query attention with 8 key-value heads. A company self-hosting such a model benefits directly, because the smaller per-token cache lets each GPU serve more concurrent requests at long context lengths."},"swa":{"n":"Sliding Window Attention","d":"Sliding window attention restricts each token to attend only to the previous w tokens instead of the entire sequence, turning attention into a local band. Compute and KV-cache memory therefore grow linearly with sequence length for a fixed window, rather than quadratically for compute and linearly with the full context for the cache.\n\nInformation can still travel beyond the window through depth. After one layer a token sees w positions back; after L stacked layers its receptive field reaches about L × w positions, similar to how stacked convolutions grow their receptive field. Mistral 7B uses a 4,096-token window with a rolling buffer cache that overwrites the oldest entries, and models such as Gemma 2 alternate local sliding-window layers with global attention layers. The limitation is that indirect, multi-hop propagation is lossy: precise retrieval of a fact far outside the window is much weaker than with full attention, which is why many designs keep some global layers.","u":["Bounding KV-cache size for long inputs","Efficient long-document encoding with local context","Hybrid local/global attention in modern LLMs"],"x":"A team deploying a model for streaming meeting transcription uses sliding window layers so the KV cache stays fixed in size however long the meeting runs. They interleave a few global attention layers so the model can still refer back to agenda items mentioned at the start."},"sparseattn":{"n":"Sparse Attention","d":"Sparse attention computes attention scores for only a selected subset of query-key pairs instead of all T² pairs, reducing cost for long sequences. The subset is defined by a pattern: local windows, strided positions, a few global tokens that attend to and are attended by everything, random links, or content-based selection such as hashing or learned top-k routing.\n\nFixed patterns from models such as the Sparse Transformer, Longformer and BigBird bring the cost close to linear in sequence length while keeping paths between any two tokens through a small number of hops or global tokens. They work well for long documents, genomic sequences and high-resolution inputs. The difficulty is practical efficiency: irregular sparsity maps poorly to GPU hardware, so theoretical savings only materialize with block-sparse kernels. A second limitation is that a fixed pattern can miss an important long-range dependency that full attention would capture, so many recent long-context LLMs prefer dense attention with FlashAttention plus selective sparse layers.","u":["Long document classification and question answering","Genomic and other very long sequences","High-resolution image or video Transformers"],"x":"A biotech team models DNA sequences tens of thousands of bases long with a BigBird-style encoder that combines local windows, global tokens and random links. Full attention at that length would not fit in GPU memory, and the block-sparse pattern keeps training throughput acceptable."},"linattn":{"n":"Linear Attention","d":"Linear attention replaces the softmax similarity with a kernel feature map φ so that attention can be computed in time linear in sequence length. Writing sim(q, k) = φ(q)·φ(k), the output for query i becomes φ(qᵢ)ᵀ(Σⱼ φ(kⱼ)vⱼᵀ) divided by φ(qᵢ)ᵀΣⱼ φ(kⱼ); by associativity, the sums over keys can be computed once and reused for every query.\n\nIn the causal case these sums become a running state that is updated with each new token, so the model behaves like a recurrent network with a fixed-size d×d memory, giving constant memory per generated token and no growing KV cache. This idea underlies Performer, RetNet, and the connection between attention and state-space models such as Mamba. The main limitation is quality: a fixed-size state cannot store arbitrary amounts of context, so pure linear attention typically lags softmax attention on precise recall tasks. Modern variants add decay or gating, and many hybrids keep some full attention layers.","u":["Autoregressive generation with constant memory per token","Very long sequence modeling at linear cost","Hybrid recurrent-attention language model layers"],"x":"An on-device assistant team experiments with a hybrid model whose layers are mostly gated linear attention plus a few full attention layers. The recurrent state keeps memory flat during long conversations on a phone, while the full attention layers preserve accurate recall of recent details."},"scaling":{"n":"Scaling Laws","d":"Scaling laws are empirical relationships showing that a language model's test loss decreases as a smooth power law in the number of parameters, the amount of training data and the training compute, when the other factors are not the bottleneck. Kaplan and colleagues at OpenAI observed these trends across several orders of magnitude, and later work refined them.\n\nTheir practical value is prediction and planning. By training a series of small models and fitting the curve, a team can estimate the loss of a much larger run before spending the compute, choose model size and token count for a fixed budget, and compare architectures or data mixtures by how their curves shift. The laws describe pretraining loss, not every downstream ability: some benchmark scores change abruptly or plateau even when loss improves smoothly. Another pitfall is extrapolating too far or across changes in data quality, tokenizer or architecture; fitted exponents are specific to the setup they were measured in.","u":["Forecasting loss of large training runs","Choosing model size for a compute budget","Comparing architectures and data mixtures fairly"],"x":"Before committing to a large pretraining run, an LLM team trains a ladder of models from tens of millions to a few billion parameters on the same data. They fit a power law to the final losses and use it to choose the size and token count of the flagship run, then check the prediction against early checkpoints."},"chinchilla":{"n":"Chinchilla-Optimal Training","d":"Chinchilla-optimal training is the rule that, for a fixed training compute budget, model parameters and training tokens should be scaled in roughly equal proportion, which works out to about 20 tokens per parameter. DeepMind's Chinchilla paper showed that many earlier large models were undertrained: a 70B-parameter model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens outperformed the 280B Gopher model trained with the same compute.\n\nThe analysis uses training compute C ≈ 6ND, where N is parameters and D is tokens, and finds that the loss-minimizing N and D each grow roughly as the square root of C. A frequent misconception is that 20 tokens per parameter is the best target for every project. It minimizes training compute for a given loss, but it ignores inference cost. Models that will serve heavy traffic, such as the LLaMA series, are deliberately trained far beyond this ratio, because a smaller model trained on more data is cheaper to run for its whole lifetime.","u":["Allocating compute between model size and data","Planning token budgets for pretraining runs","Judging whether a model is undertrained"],"x":"A startup with a fixed GPU budget uses the Chinchilla ratio as a starting point, then deliberately trains a smaller model on several times more tokens. Because the model will serve millions of requests, they accept higher training cost per unit of quality in exchange for lower inference cost."},"icl":{"n":"In-Context Learning (ICL)","d":"In-context learning is the ability of a large language model to perform a new task from instructions or a few examples placed in the prompt, without any update to its weights. Given several input-output demonstrations followed by a new input, the model continues the pattern, and the same frozen model can switch tasks just by changing the prompt.\n\nThis ability emerged at scale with GPT-3. Research suggests the model infers the task format and label space from the demonstrations, and mechanisms such as induction heads, which copy patterns seen earlier in the context, contribute to it. Performance depends heavily on the choice, order and format of examples, and selecting demonstrations similar to the query through retrieval often helps. A notable finding is that models sometimes rely more on format and label distribution than on whether the example labels are correct. In-context learning is limited by context length and costs tokens on every call, unlike fine-tuning.","u":["Few-shot classification without fine-tuning","Rapid prototyping of new LLM-powered features","Format-following via example demonstrations"],"x":"A marketplace team builds a product categorization prototype by placing ten labeled examples, retrieved by embedding similarity to each new listing, into the prompt of a hosted LLM. This avoids training a model during exploration, and they move to fine-tuning only once volume makes the long prompts too costly."},"pretrain":{"n":"Pretraining","d":"Pretraining is the first and most expensive stage of building a foundation model, in which the model learns general representations from a very large unlabeled corpus using a self-supervised objective. For LLMs this is next-token prediction over trillions of tokens of web text, books, code and other sources; for encoders such as BERT it is masked language modeling.\n\nThe quality of the result depends as much on data as on architecture. Pipelines deduplicate documents, filter low-quality and toxic text with heuristics and classifiers, remove benchmark contamination, and tune the mixture of domains and languages. Training runs for weeks on thousands of accelerators with data, tensor and pipeline parallelism, careful learning-rate schedules and monitoring for loss spikes. The resulting base model can complete text and has broad knowledge, but it does not reliably follow instructions; post-training such as supervised fine-tuning and preference optimization is what turns it into a usable assistant. Knowledge learned in pretraining also has a fixed cutoff date.","u":["Building base LLMs from web-scale text","Continued pretraining on domain or language data","Self-supervised encoders for vision and speech"],"x":"A national research lab pretrains a bilingual LLM with heavy weight on its own language, after deduplication and quality filtering of crawled text. They track validation loss per data source throughout training and adjust the mixture when one domain stops improving."},"sft":{"n":"Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)","d":"Supervised fine-tuning trains a pretrained language model on curated prompt-response pairs so that it learns to follow instructions and produce responses in a desired style. The objective is still next-token cross-entropy, but the loss is usually computed only on the response tokens, with the prompt tokens masked out, so the model learns to answer rather than to imitate user messages.\n\nSFT is typically the first post-training step after pretraining. A few thousand to a few hundred thousand high-quality examples, formatted with the model's chat template, are often enough to change behavior substantially, because the model already has the underlying knowledge. Data quality and diversity matter more than volume. SFT is also used to specialize models for domains or output formats such as JSON. Its limitations are that it only imitates demonstrations, so it cannot easily teach the model to prefer better answers over merely plausible ones, and that aggressive fine-tuning on narrow data can cause forgetting of general abilities.","u":["Turning base models into instruction followers","Teaching structured output formats such as JSON","Domain specialization for support or legal tasks"],"x":"An insurance company fine-tunes an open LLM on several thousand reviewed question-answer pairs about its policies, formatted with the model's chat template. They mask the prompt tokens from the loss and evaluate on a held-out set plus general benchmarks to catch any loss of broader ability."},"rlhf":{"n":"RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback)","d":"Reinforcement learning from human feedback aligns a language model with human preferences by training it to maximize a learned reward that predicts which responses people prefer. The classic pipeline, used for InstructGPT and early ChatGPT, has three stages: supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations, training a reward model on human comparisons between pairs of responses, and optimizing the policy against that reward with PPO.\n\nComparisons are easier for people to provide reliably than ideal answers, and the reward model generalizes those judgments to new prompts. During RL a KL penalty keeps the policy close to the SFT reference model, which preserves fluency and limits exploitation of reward-model errors. RLHF improves helpfulness, harmlessness and instruction following beyond what SFT achieves. Its weaknesses are cost and fragility: it needs several models in memory, many hyperparameters, and is prone to reward hacking, where the policy learns quirks such as excessive length or flattery that the reward model scores highly but people do not actually prefer.","u":["Aligning chat assistants with human preferences","Reducing harmful or unhelpful responses","Improving summarization quality beyond imitation"],"x":"OpenAI described using RLHF to train InstructGPT, where labelers ranked model outputs and a reward model trained on those rankings guided PPO. Teams applying the same recipe monitor response length and reward-model scores together, because a rising reward with steadily longer answers is a common sign of reward hacking."},"rewardmodel":{"n":"Reward Model","d":"A reward model is a network that takes a prompt and a response and outputs a scalar score estimating how much humans, or another judge, would prefer that response. It is usually a pretrained language model with a single linear output head, trained on pairwise comparisons where one response was chosen over another.\n\nThe standard training loss is the Bradley-Terry objective: maximize the log-sigmoid of the score difference between the chosen and the rejected response. Only differences matter, so the absolute scale of rewards is arbitrary. Reward models drive RLHF, rank candidate responses in best-of-n sampling, and filter synthetic data. Their main weakness is that they are imperfect proxies trained on limited data. When a policy is optimized hard against a reward model, it finds inputs where the model is wrong, such as verbose or sycophantic answers, which is called reward hacking or overoptimization. Ensembles, KL constraints and fresh comparison data reduce this risk.","u":["Scoring responses for RLHF policy optimization","Best-of-n reranking of sampled answers","Filtering synthetic training data by quality"],"x":"A team building a coding assistant trains a reward model on developer preferences between pairs of suggested code edits. Besides using it for RL, they use it at inference to rerank several sampled candidates, and they audit cases where it prefers longer code to check for a length bias."},"ppo":{"n":"PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization)","d":"Proximal Policy Optimization is a policy-gradient reinforcement learning algorithm that improves a policy while preventing each update from moving it too far from the policy that collected the data. It does this with a clipped objective: the probability ratio between the new and old policy for each action is multiplied by the advantage, but the ratio is clipped to a range such as 0.8 to 1.2, so there is no incentive to push it further.\n\nPPO is popular because it is stable, reuses each batch of experience for several epochs, and is simpler than trust-region methods. A learned value function with generalized advantage estimation supplies advantages. In LLM training, each generated token is an action, the reward arrives at the end of the response, and a per-token KL penalty to a reference model is added. The main drawback for LLMs is cost: policy, value, reward and reference models must all fit in memory, and results depend heavily on hyperparameters.","u":["RLHF policy optimization for language models","Robotics and continuous control tasks","Game-playing agents with simulated environments"],"x":"OpenAI used PPO to train game-playing agents in OpenAI Five and later for the RL stage of InstructGPT. Teams running PPO on LLMs typically watch the clip fraction and the KL to the reference model each step, and stop or lower the learning rate when either grows too quickly."},"dpo":{"n":"DPO (Direct Preference Optimization)","d":"Direct Preference Optimization trains a language model on preference pairs with a simple classification-style loss, without training a separate reward model or running reinforcement learning. It relies on the fact that the optimal policy for the KL-regularized RLHF objective has a closed form, so the reward can be written through the log-probability ratio between the policy and a frozen reference model.\n\nSubstituting that implicit reward into the Bradley-Terry preference model yields a loss that increases the policy's relative likelihood of the chosen response over the rejected one, compared with the reference model. The temperature β controls how far the policy may move from the reference. DPO is stable, needs two models in memory and is a standard alignment step for open models. Its limitations are that it learns from a fixed offline dataset, so it cannot explore or correct mistakes outside that data, and it can reduce the absolute likelihood of both chosen and rejected responses, sometimes degrading outputs.","u":["Preference alignment of open chat models","Tuning style or tone from pairwise feedback","Reducing unwanted behaviors without RL infrastructure"],"x":"A team aligning an open model for internal use collects pairwise preferences from domain experts and runs DPO with TRL on top of their SFT checkpoint. They choose DPO over PPO because it fits on fewer GPUs and has fewer hyperparameters, and they compare win rates against the SFT model on held-out prompts."},"grpo":{"n":"GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization)","d":"Group Relative Policy Optimization is a variant of PPO for language models that removes the learned value function and instead computes each response's advantage relative to a group of responses sampled for the same prompt. For every prompt, the policy generates G completions, each is scored, and the advantage of a completion is its reward minus the group mean, divided by the group standard deviation.\n\nUsing the group as a baseline saves the memory and compute of a separate critic, which is often as large as the policy, and suits rewards given at the end of a response, such as math answer checks. The policy update keeps PPO's clipped ratio and adds a KL penalty to a reference model. GRPO was introduced in DeepSeekMath and used to train DeepSeek-R1's reasoning. A practical limitation is that prompts where all sampled answers receive the same reward give zero advantage and no learning signal, so prompt difficulty must suit the model.","u":["Reasoning training with verifiable math rewards","Code generation optimized against unit tests","Critic-free RL fine-tuning on limited GPUs"],"x":"DeepSeek used GRPO to train its R1 reasoning models with rule-based rewards for answer correctness and format. Teams reproducing this filter their prompt sets so that the model solves some but not all samples per prompt, since groups with uniform rewards contribute nothing to the gradient."},"rlvr":{"n":"RL with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR)","d":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards trains a language model using rewards computed by a program that checks the output, instead of a learned reward model. Typical verifiers compare a final math answer with the ground truth, run generated code against unit tests, or check that an output satisfies a format or constraint.\n\nBecause the reward is objective, it cannot be flattered by style or length in the way a learned reward model can, which allows long RL runs with less reward hacking. Combined with algorithms such as PPO or GRPO, RLVR is the main recipe behind recent reasoning models: the model learns to produce long chains of thought that end in correct answers, and behaviors such as self-checking and backtracking can emerge. The limitation is coverage: it only works for tasks with reliable automatic checks. Lenient verifiers can still be gamed, and sparse binary rewards give no signal on problems the model never solves.","u":["Training math reasoning with checkable answers","Code generation rewarded by passing tests","Instruction following with programmatic constraint checks"],"x":"DeepSeek-R1 was trained largely with rule-based rewards that check final answers and output format. A team applying RLVR to SQL generation executes each generated query against a test database and rewards exact result matches, sandboxing execution so generated queries cannot modify data."},"cai":{"n":"Constitutional AI & RLAIF","d":"Constitutional AI is a method from Anthropic for training a harmless and helpful assistant using a written set of principles, the constitution, and AI-generated feedback instead of human labels for harmfulness. RLAIF, reinforcement learning from AI feedback, is the general idea of replacing human preference labels with judgments from a language model.\n\nThe method has two phases. In the supervised phase, the model responds to difficult prompts, then critiques and revises its own responses according to randomly selected principles, and is fine-tuned on the revised answers. In the RL phase, an AI model compares response pairs against the principles, and these AI preferences train a reward model that the policy is optimized against. This scales feedback cheaply and makes the target behavior explicit and auditable through the written principles. The limitation is that quality is bounded by the judging model's ability and biases, so errors or blind spots in the AI feedback can be reinforced rather than corrected.","u":["Harmlessness training without large human labeling","Scaling preference data with LLM judges","Making target behavior explicit through principles"],"x":"Anthropic introduced Constitutional AI to train Claude models to avoid harmful outputs while remaining helpful and willing to explain refusals. Other teams adopt RLAIF by using a strong model as judge to label preference pairs, then spot-check a sample with human reviewers to measure agreement."},"lora":{"n":"LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)","d":"LoRA fine-tunes a large model by freezing its original weights and learning a low-rank update for selected weight matrices. For a frozen matrix W of shape d×k, it adds a product BA, where B is d×r and A is r×k with a small rank r such as 8 or 16; only A and B are trained, and the update is scaled by α/r.\n\nFine-tuning updates tend to have low intrinsic rank. LoRA cuts trainable parameters by orders of magnitude, reduces optimizer memory accordingly, and yields small adapter files that can be swapped on one shared base model. B is initialized to zero so training starts exactly from the base model, and after training BA can be merged into W with no added latency. Targeting all linear layers usually beats targeting only the attention projections. A limitation is that low rank can underfit tasks that require large changes, such as learning new languages or substantial new knowledge.","u":["Parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large LLMs","Serving many task adapters on one base model","Customizing diffusion models for styles or subjects"],"x":"A SaaS company serves one base LLM and loads a separate LoRA adapter for each enterprise customer's tone and terminology. Serving engines such as vLLM can batch requests that use different adapters together, so adding a customer costs only a small adapter file rather than a full model copy."},"qlora":{"n":"QLoRA","d":"QLoRA fine-tunes a large language model by keeping the frozen base weights in 4-bit precision and training LoRA adapters in 16-bit on top of them. Gradients flow through the quantized weights, dequantized on the fly for each matrix multiplication, into the small adapters, which are the only trainable parameters.\n\nThree techniques make this work. NF4 is a 4-bit data type whose levels are placed at quantiles of a normal distribution, matching how pretrained weights are distributed. Double quantization also quantizes the per-block scaling constants to save further memory. Paged optimizers move optimizer state to CPU memory during memory spikes. Together they made it possible to fine-tune a 65B model on a single 48 GB GPU with quality close to 16-bit LoRA. The trade-offs are slower training steps because of repeated dequantization, and a subtle deployment issue: merging adapters into a dequantized base gives slightly different weights than those used in training, so outputs should be re-evaluated after merging.","u":["Fine-tuning large LLMs on a single GPU","Low-budget domain adaptation experiments","Academic research on limited hardware"],"x":"A university lab with a few 24 GB GPUs uses QLoRA to adapt a 30B-class open model to clinical note summarization. Running the base in 4-bit lets each experiment fit on one card, and they evaluate the merged model separately before deployment to confirm quality matches the adapter-on-quantized setup."},"adapters":{"n":"Adapters & PEFT","d":"Parameter-efficient fine-tuning, or PEFT, is a family of methods that adapt a pretrained model to a new task by training only a small number of added or selected parameters while the original weights stay frozen. Bottleneck adapters, the original approach, insert small modules inside each Transformer layer: a down-projection to a low dimension, a nonlinearity and an up-projection, added back through a residual connection.\n\nOther PEFT methods include LoRA, which adds low-rank updates to weight matrices; prefix and prompt tuning, which learn virtual tokens; and IA³ and BitFit, which train scaling vectors or only biases. The shared benefits are lower training memory, tiny per-task checkpoints, and one base model that switches behavior by loading different adapters. Classic adapters add sequential computation at inference, which increases latency slightly, whereas LoRA can be merged into the weights. PEFT methods generally match full fine-tuning on moderate tasks but may fall short when the task differs greatly from pretraining.","u":["Multi-task models with per-task adapter modules","Cheap fine-tuning under limited GPU memory","Storing many customer-specific model variants"],"x":"A translation provider keeps one multilingual base model and trains a small adapter per language pair and domain. Adapters are loaded on demand for each request, so adding a new domain means training and storing a few megabytes instead of copying the full model."},"prompttuning":{"n":"Prefix Tuning & Prompt Tuning","d":"Prompt tuning and prefix tuning adapt a frozen language model by learning continuous vectors that act like extra tokens, instead of changing the model's weights. Prompt tuning prepends a small number of trainable embeddings, often 20 to 100 virtual tokens, to the input embedding sequence only. Prefix tuning instead learns key and value vectors prepended at every attention layer, giving it more influence over the computation.\n\nOnly these soft prompt parameters are trained, so each task needs a few thousand to a few hundred thousand parameters, and a single batch can mix requests for different tasks by attaching different prompts. Prompt tuning was shown to approach full fine-tuning quality as model size grows into the billions of parameters, but it is noticeably weaker on smaller models and harder tasks. Training can also be unstable and sensitive to initialization; initializing virtual tokens from embeddings of real words related to the task usually helps. Soft prompts are not human-readable text.","u":["Lightweight per-task adaptation of frozen LLMs","Mixed-task batching on one shared model","Multi-tenant serving with tiny task parameters"],"x":"A platform team offering classification tasks to many internal groups trains a soft prompt for each task on a shared frozen model. Because requests differ only in their prepended vectors, the server can batch tasks together, though for their smaller models they found LoRA gave better accuracy on harder tasks."},"distill":{"n":"Knowledge Distillation","d":"Knowledge distillation trains a smaller student model to imitate a larger teacher model, transferring much of the teacher's ability into a model that is cheaper to run. Instead of learning only from hard labels, the student is trained to match the teacher's full output distribution, softened with a temperature T so that the relative probabilities of wrong classes, which carry information about similarity, become visible.\n\nThe usual loss mixes cross-entropy on true labels with the KL divergence between temperature-softened teacher and student distributions, multiplied by T² to keep gradient scales comparable. DistilBERT used this approach to reach most of BERT's accuracy with fewer layers. For LLMs, distillation also takes the form of training a student on text generated by a teacher, sometimes called sequence-level distillation, or matching token-level logits when both share a tokenizer. A limitation is that a student with much less capacity cannot absorb everything, and a teacher's errors and biases transfer along with its strengths.","u":["Compressing large models for mobile deployment","Creating fast models for low-latency serving","Transferring LLM abilities into smaller open models"],"x":"Hugging Face released DistilBERT, trained by distilling BERT into a model with half the layers. A search team uses a similar approach, distilling a large cross-encoder reranker into a small student so that reranking fits within their per-query latency budget."},"synthdata":{"n":"Synthetic Data","d":"Synthetic data is training data generated by a model or a program rather than collected from real users or written by people. For LLMs, a strong model is prompted to write instructions, answers, dialogues, reasoning traces or code, and the filtered results train another model; for other domains, simulators or generative models produce images, tabular records or sensor data.\n\nIt is valuable when real data is scarce, expensive to label, private or lacks rare cases. Pipelines such as Self-Instruct bootstrap varied tasks from a small seed set, and verification steps such as code execution, math answer checks or reward-model scoring raise quality. Diversity and correctness are the central challenges: generated data tends to repeat the generator's style and errors, and training repeatedly on a model's own outputs without fresh real data can narrow the distribution, which is known as model collapse. License terms of the generator model may also restrict using its outputs to train competing models.","u":["Instruction tuning data for smaller LLMs","Augmenting rare classes and edge cases","Privacy-preserving substitutes for sensitive records"],"x":"A bank's NLP team needs training data for routing customer complaints but cannot use raw transcripts. They generate synthetic complaints with an LLM from a taxonomy of issues and personas, then have reviewers check a sample and deduplicate by embedding similarity to keep the set diverse."},"forgetting":{"n":"Catastrophic Forgetting","d":"Catastrophic forgetting is the tendency of a neural network to lose previously learned abilities when it is trained further on new data or a new task. Because the same weights encode both old and new knowledge, gradient updates that reduce loss on the new data can overwrite what supported earlier performance, and nothing in the objective protects it.\n\nIn LLM practice it appears when fine-tuning on narrow domain data degrades general instruction following, safety behavior or performance in other languages. Common mitigations include mixing a fraction of general or original-task data into the fine-tuning set (replay), using lower learning rates and fewer steps, parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA that change fewer weights, and regularization methods such as elastic weight consolidation, which penalizes changes to parameters important for earlier tasks. A common misconception is that LoRA prevents forgetting entirely; it typically forgets less than full fine-tuning, but it also learns less, and significant forgetting can still occur.","u":["Diagnosing regressions after domain fine-tuning","Continual learning on evolving data streams","Designing replay mixtures for model updates"],"x":"A company fine-tuning an assistant on internal documentation notices it starts ignoring formatting instructions and answers general questions worse. They add about one fifth general instruction data to the training mix and track a fixed general benchmark alongside the domain metrics for every new checkpoint."},"quantization":{"n":"Quantization","d":"Quantization stores and computes a model's weights, and sometimes its activations, with fewer bits, such as 8-bit or 4-bit integers instead of 16-bit floats, to reduce memory and speed up inference. Each real value is mapped to an integer through a scale and often a zero point, and values are reconstructed approximately when needed.\n\nFor LLMs, decoding is usually limited by memory bandwidth, so halving or quartering the bytes per weight translates fairly directly into faster token generation and lets larger models fit on a given GPU. Quantization can be applied after training (post-training quantization) with a small calibration set or simulated during training (quantization-aware training) for better accuracy at very low bit widths. Per-channel or per-group scales greatly reduce error. The main pitfall is outliers: a few very large activation or weight values stretch the range and destroy precision for the rest, which is why naive 8-bit activation quantization can badly hurt large models.","u":["Running LLMs on smaller or fewer GPUs","On-device inference on phones and laptops","Lowering serving cost and token latency"],"x":"A team running a local coding assistant on developer laptops ships a 4-bit quantized model in GGUF format via llama.cpp. They compare a few quantization levels on their internal code tasks and pick the smallest one whose accuracy stays close to the 16-bit baseline."},"gptq":{"n":"INT8 / INT4 Weight Quantization (GPTQ, AWQ)","d":"GPTQ and AWQ are post-training methods that compress LLM weights to 4-bit or 3-bit integers with little accuracy loss, using only a small calibration dataset and no retraining. Plain rounding to 4 bits often damages large models, so both methods decide how to quantize using information about the activations the weights will see.\n\nGPTQ quantizes the columns of each weight matrix one at a time and, after each step, updates the remaining unquantized weights to compensate for the error, using second-order information from the inverse Hessian of the layer's reconstruction loss. AWQ observes that a small fraction of weight channels, those multiplied by large activations, matter most; it scales these channels up before quantization (and the activations down correspondingly) so they lose less precision. Weight-only methods mainly save memory and bandwidth during decoding; compute-bound prefill speeds up less. Calibration data should resemble the deployment domain.","u":["Serving 70B-class models on fewer GPUs","4-bit checkpoints for local LLM inference","Reducing memory bandwidth during decoding"],"x":"A team self-hosting a 70B model serves an AWQ 4-bit checkpoint with vLLM, which fits on half as many GPUs as the 16-bit version. Before switching, they run their evaluation suite and spot-check long reasoning outputs, where small quantization errors are most likely to show up."},"mixedprec":{"n":"Mixed Precision (FP16 / BF16)","d":"Mixed precision training runs most computations in 16-bit floating point while keeping numerically sensitive parts in 32-bit, which roughly halves activation memory and speeds up matrix multiplications on tensor-core GPUs. Matmuls run in FP16 or BF16, while a master copy of the weights, the optimizer state, and reductions such as softmax and loss stay in FP32.\n\nThe two 16-bit formats trade off differently. FP16 has 5 exponent bits and 10 mantissa bits: good precision but a narrow range, so small gradients underflow to zero. It therefore needs loss scaling, where the loss is multiplied by a large factor before backpropagation and gradients are divided back before the update, with the factor adjusted dynamically when overflows occur. BF16 keeps FP32's 8 exponent bits with only 7 mantissa bits, so it has the same range, usually needs no loss scaling, and is the default for LLM training on recent hardware. Its low precision makes summing many small values error-prone.","u":["Faster training on tensor-core GPUs","Halving activation memory for larger batches","Standard BF16 training of large language models"],"x":"A vision team moves from FP32 to automatic mixed precision and roughly halves per-step training time on the same GPUs. On older GPUs without BF16 support they use FP16 with a gradient scaler, and they log the scale value so that repeated drops can flag overflow problems early."},"prune":{"n":"Pruning","d":"Pruning removes weights, neurons, attention heads or whole layers from a trained network to make it smaller or faster while preserving as much accuracy as possible. Unstructured pruning zeroes individual weights, typically those with the smallest magnitude or the lowest estimated importance; structured pruning removes entire rows, channels, heads or layers so the remaining model is a smaller dense network.\n\nThe distinction matters for speed. Unstructured sparsity reduces parameter count but rarely speeds up inference on GPUs without specialized sparse kernels, except for hardware-supported patterns such as NVIDIA's 2:4 sparsity, where two of every four consecutive weights are zero. Structured pruning gives real speedups on standard hardware but usually costs more accuracy and needs fine-tuning or distillation to recover. For LLMs, methods such as SparseGPT and Wanda prune in one shot using calibration data and activation statistics. Moderate sparsity is often nearly free, but high sparsity degrades quality sharply, and results reported only as parameter counts can overstate practical gains.","u":["Shrinking models for edge and mobile devices","Removing redundant attention heads or layers","2:4 structured sparsity for GPU speedups"],"x":"A team deploying a vision model to factory cameras applies structured channel pruning followed by fine-tuning, which shrinks the model enough to meet the frame-rate requirement on the edge device. They measure real latency on the target hardware, since parameter reductions alone did not predict the speedup."},"gradaccum":{"n":"Gradient Accumulation","d":"Gradient accumulation simulates a large batch by running several smaller micro-batches through forward and backward passes, summing their gradients, and applying a single optimizer step at the end. With a micro-batch of 4 and 8 accumulation steps, the update uses gradients from 32 examples while only 4 examples' activations are held in memory at once.\n\nIt is used when the desired batch size, often chosen for training stability or to match a published recipe, does not fit in GPU memory. To get the same gradient as a true large batch, each micro-batch loss is divided by the number of accumulation steps, or the losses are normalized by the total token count when sequence lengths vary. The trade-off is time: accumulation saves memory but not compute, and it reduces the number of optimizer steps per epoch. A common pitfall is that batch normalization still sees only micro-batch statistics, so accumulation is not exactly equivalent for models that use it.","u":["Training with large effective batches on small GPUs","Reproducing published batch sizes on less hardware","Fine-tuning LLMs with long sequences"],"x":"A team fine-tuning a 7B model on a single GPU can fit only two long sequences per forward pass, so they accumulate 16 micro-batches to reach an effective batch of 32. They scale the learning-rate schedule by optimizer steps, not micro-batches, so warmup and decay match the intended recipe."},"gradckpt":{"n":"Gradient Checkpointing","d":"Gradient checkpointing trades extra computation for lower memory by storing only some intermediate activations during the forward pass and recomputing the rest during the backward pass. Backpropagation normally keeps every layer's activations so it can compute gradients, and for deep networks with long sequences this activation memory often exceeds the memory used by the weights themselves.\n\nWith checkpointing, the network is split into segments and only the inputs to each segment are saved. When the backward pass reaches a segment, that segment's forward computation is run again to rebuild its activations, and then gradients flow through it. Checkpointing every transformer block typically costs about one extra forward pass, roughly 20–35% more training time, while cutting activation memory sharply. It is the standard way to fit larger batches or longer contexts on a fixed GPU. A common misconception is that it saves weight or optimizer memory; it only reduces activation memory, so it is usually combined with sharding or mixed precision.","u":["Fine-tuning large LLMs on a single GPU","Training with long sequence lengths","Increasing batch size under fixed memory"],"x":"A research team fine-tunes a 13B-parameter model on 8K-token documents using GPUs with limited memory. Enabling gradient checkpointing on every decoder block lets the run fit without reducing sequence length, at the cost of slower steps. They accept the longer wall-clock time because the alternative was renting larger accelerators."},"parallelism":{"n":"Data, Tensor & Pipeline Parallelism","d":"Large models are trained across many accelerators by splitting either the data, the individual weight matrices, or the sequence of layers. In data parallelism, every device holds a full model replica, processes a different slice of the batch, and gradients are averaged with an all-reduce before each update. Sharded variants such as ZeRO and FSDP also split optimizer states, gradients and weights so that no device stores a full copy.\n\nTensor parallelism splits a single matrix multiplication across devices, for example by columns in the first MLP layer and by rows in the second, so each device computes part of every layer; it needs fast interconnect because it communicates inside each layer. Pipeline parallelism assigns consecutive layers to different devices and streams micro-batches through them. Real systems combine all three. A key pitfall of pipeline parallelism is the pipeline bubble: devices sit idle while the pipeline fills and drains, so enough micro-batches are needed to keep utilization high.","u":["Pretraining models too large for one GPU","Scaling training throughput across a cluster","Serving huge models across multiple GPUs"],"x":"A foundation-model team trains a large LLM on thousands of GPUs, using tensor parallelism within each 8-GPU node, pipeline parallelism across nodes, and data parallelism across replicas. This layout keeps the communication-heavy tensor-parallel traffic on fast intra-node links. They tune the number of micro-batches to keep the pipeline bubble small."},"greedy":{"n":"Greedy Decoding","d":"Greedy decoding generates text by choosing the single most probable next token at every step. The model produces a probability distribution over the vocabulary, the argmax token is appended to the sequence, and the process repeats until an end token or a length limit is reached.\n\nBecause it involves no randomness, greedy decoding is deterministic for a fixed model and prompt (up to small numerical differences on GPUs), which makes it useful for evaluation, code completion, extraction tasks and any setting where reproducibility matters. It is also the cheapest decoding strategy. Its main limitation is that locally best choices do not guarantee the most probable full sequence: a slightly less likely token early on can lead to a much better continuation. In open-ended generation, greedy decoding also tends to produce repetitive loops and bland text, which is why sampling methods with temperature, top-k or top-p are usually preferred for creative or conversational output.","u":["Deterministic outputs for benchmark evaluation","Structured extraction into fixed JSON formats","Short code completions in an IDE"],"x":"An insurance company uses an LLM to extract fields such as policy number and claim date from scanned forms. The team uses greedy decoding so the same document always yields the same output, which simplifies testing and auditing. A repetition check is still added because long outputs occasionally loop."},"beam":{"n":"Beam Search","d":"Beam search is a decoding algorithm that keeps the B most probable partial sequences at each step instead of only the single best one. At every step, each of the B beams is extended with candidate next tokens, all extensions are scored by their cumulative log-probability, and only the top B are kept. When beams finish with an end token, the highest-scoring complete sequence is returned.\n\nKeeping several hypotheses lets the search recover from an early choice that looked good locally but led nowhere, so beam search usually finds sequences with higher total likelihood than greedy decoding. It works well for tasks with one mostly correct answer, such as machine translation, summarization and speech recognition. Raw log-probability favors short outputs, so a length penalty is normally applied. A well-known limitation is that higher likelihood does not mean better text: for open-ended generation, beam search produces generic, repetitive output, and larger beams can even reduce quality.","u":["Neural machine translation output decoding","Abstractive summarization of documents","Speech recognition transcript decoding"],"x":"A translation service decodes its sequence-to-sequence model with a beam size of 4 and a length penalty tuned on a held-out set. Larger beams gave no quality gain while raising latency, so the team kept the beam small. Outputs are also filtered for repeated n-grams."},"temperature":{"n":"Temperature","d":"Temperature is a scalar that controls how random sampling from a language model is, by dividing the logits before the softmax. A temperature below 1 sharpens the distribution so that likely tokens become even more likely; a temperature above 1 flattens it so that unlikely tokens get more probability mass. As temperature approaches 0, sampling becomes equivalent to greedy decoding, and at exactly 1 the model's original distribution is used.\n\nPractitioners lower the temperature for factual question answering, code and extraction, where consistency matters, and raise it for brainstorming or creative writing, where diversity is useful. Temperature is often combined with top-k or top-p truncation, which removes the long tail of very unlikely tokens that high temperature would otherwise expose. A common misconception is that temperature changes what the model knows or makes it more creative in a deep sense; it only reshapes the existing distribution, and very high values mostly produce incoherent text.","u":["Lower randomness for factual question answering","Diverse candidates for creative writing tools","Calibrating confidence of classifier probabilities"],"x":"A marketing-copy tool generates five ad headline candidates per request at temperature 0.9 so that suggestions differ meaningfully. The same company's customer-support assistant runs at temperature 0.2 to keep answers consistent with policy. Both settings were chosen by comparing human ratings on sampled outputs."},"topk":{"n":"Top-k Sampling","d":"Top-k sampling restricts each generation step to the k most probable tokens, renormalizes their probabilities, and samples from that reduced set. All other tokens receive zero probability, so the model can never pick a token from the long tail of the vocabulary, where many individually rare but collectively frequent nonsense tokens live.\n\nThis simple truncation produces more coherent text than sampling from the full distribution while still allowing variety, which made it popular for early open-ended generation systems. Typical values range from about 20 to 100, often combined with a temperature. The main weakness is that k is fixed regardless of the shape of the distribution. When the model is very confident, for example after \"The capital of France is\", a large k still admits poor candidates; when many continuations are reasonable, a small k cuts off valid options. Top-p sampling was introduced largely to address this by adapting the candidate set size to the model's confidence.","u":["Open-ended story and dialogue generation","Generating diverse synthetic training data","Limiting tail tokens in chat assistants"],"x":"A game studio uses an LLM to generate varied lines of dialogue for background characters. Top-k sampling with k = 40 and a moderate temperature keeps lines varied while avoiding rare garbled tokens. The team reviews a sample of generations each release to adjust k."},"topp":{"n":"Top-p (Nucleus) Sampling","d":"Top-p sampling, also called nucleus sampling, samples the next token from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches a threshold p. Tokens are sorted by probability, added one by one until their total mass is at least p, the rest are discarded, and the kept probabilities are renormalized before sampling.\n\nThe key advantage over top-k is adaptivity. When the model is confident, the nucleus may contain only one or two tokens, so output stays precise; when many continuations are plausible, the nucleus grows and allows variety. Values between 0.8 and 0.95 are common, usually together with a temperature, and it is a default in many chat APIs. A frequent misunderstanding is the order of operations: temperature is applied to the logits first, which changes the distribution and therefore the size of the nucleus. Also, p = 1 disables truncation entirely, and very low p behaves almost like greedy decoding.","u":["Default sampling for conversational assistants","Creative writing with controlled diversity","Generating paraphrases for data augmentation"],"x":"A writing-assistant product samples suggestions with top-p = 0.9 and temperature 0.7. Because the nucleus shrinks when the model is confident, grammar fixes stay stable while open-ended rewrites still vary. The team logs the average nucleus size to detect prompts where outputs become erratic."},"specdec":{"n":"Speculative Decoding","d":"Speculative decoding speeds up LLM generation by letting a small, fast draft model propose several tokens, which the large target model then verifies in a single forward pass. Autoregressive decoding is usually limited by memory bandwidth rather than compute, so checking k proposed tokens in parallel costs about the same as generating one.\n\nEach drafted token is accepted with probability min(1, p(x)/q(x)), where p is the target model's probability and q the draft's. At the first rejection, a replacement token is sampled from the normalized residual distribution max(0, p − q), and drafting restarts. This rejection scheme guarantees that the output has exactly the same distribution as sampling from the target model alone, so quality is unchanged. Speedups of 2–3x are common when the draft model agrees often with the target. The benefit shrinks when acceptance rates are low, when batch sizes are large enough that the system becomes compute-bound, or when the draft model's own latency is not small.","u":["Lowering latency of interactive chat responses","Faster code completion with large models","Cheaper serving of large models at low batch"],"x":"A coding-assistant team pairs a large code model with a small draft model from the same family that shares its tokenizer. Because code is highly predictable, most drafted tokens are accepted and completion latency drops noticeably. They monitor the acceptance rate per language, since it falls for less common languages."},"pagedattn":{"n":"PagedAttention","d":"PagedAttention is an attention implementation that stores the key-value cache in fixed-size blocks that do not need to be contiguous in GPU memory, similar to virtual memory pages in an operating system.\n\nWithout paging, a server must reserve a contiguous KV-cache region for each request sized for the maximum possible output length, which wastes much of the memory through over-reservation and fragmentation. With PagedAttention, each sequence has a block table mapping logical token positions to physical blocks, and new blocks are allocated only as tokens are generated. The attention kernel reads keys and values through this table. Near-zero waste means more requests fit in memory at once, which raises throughput substantially. Blocks can also be shared between sequences, such as a common system prompt, with copy-on-write when they diverge. PagedAttention does not reduce the size of the KV cache per token; it only removes wasted space, so long contexts still consume a lot of memory.","u":["High-throughput LLM serving with many users","Sharing system prompts across concurrent requests","Parallel sampling and beam search memory sharing"],"x":"A company hosting an internal chatbot moves from a naive serving stack to vLLM. Because PagedAttention removes KV-cache fragmentation, the same GPUs handle many more concurrent conversations. The team tunes the GPU memory fraction reserved for the cache and monitors preemptions when memory runs out."},"contbatch":{"n":"Continuous Batching","d":"Continuous batching, also called in-flight or iteration-level batching, schedules LLM inference one decoding step at a time, so new requests can join the running batch and finished requests can leave it at every step. Static batching, in contrast, waits until every sequence in a batch has finished before starting the next batch.\n\nBecause output lengths vary widely, static batching leaves GPU slots idle while short requests wait for the longest one to finish, and new requests queue in the meantime. With continuous batching, the scheduler checks after each forward pass which sequences emitted an end token, removes them, and inserts waiting requests. This keeps the GPU busy and greatly increases throughput while also lowering average queueing delay. It needs careful KV-cache memory management, which is why it is usually paired with PagedAttention. A limitation is that mixing long prefills with decodes can cause latency spikes for running requests, which chunked prefill is designed to reduce.","u":["Serving chat APIs with variable-length outputs","Maximizing GPU throughput for batch inference","Reducing queueing delay under bursty traffic"],"x":"An LLM API provider serves models with an engine that uses continuous batching and chunked prefill. Short answers no longer wait for long essays in the same batch, so time-to-first-token improves under load. The team caps the maximum number of concurrent sequences to protect per-token latency."},"longctx":{"n":"Long Context Extension","d":"Long context extension refers to techniques that let a pretrained LLM handle sequences much longer than its original training length. Models with rotary position embeddings (RoPE) fail beyond their trained length mainly because they encounter rotation angles they never saw, so most methods rescale positions or frequencies into a familiar range.\n\nPosition Interpolation linearly compresses positions by the extension factor. NTK-aware scaling instead increases the RoPE base so that high-frequency dimensions, which encode local order, are barely changed while low-frequency dimensions are stretched. YaRN combines a per-frequency interpolation schedule with an attention temperature adjustment. These methods work best with a short fine-tuning phase on long documents. A key limitation is that a model accepting 128K tokens does not necessarily use them well: retrieval accuracy often drops for information placed in the middle of the context, so long-context ability should be measured with realistic tasks, not only perplexity.","u":["Question answering over long legal contracts","Repository-level code understanding and editing","Summarizing long meeting transcripts and books"],"x":"A legal-tech company extends an open 8K-context model to 64K tokens using YaRN scaling followed by brief fine-tuning on long contracts. This lets lawyers query an entire agreement without chunking. The team validates with needle-in-a-haystack tests and with real clause-lookup questions placed at different positions."},"prompting":{"n":"Prompt Engineering","d":"Prompt engineering is the practice of designing the input text given to a language model so that it reliably produces the desired output without changing its weights. A prompt typically combines an instruction, relevant context, constraints on format, and sometimes a few worked examples (few-shot prompting).\n\nEffective prompts state the task explicitly, define the audience and output format, separate instructions from data with clear delimiters, and give examples that cover edge cases. Asking for structured output such as JSON makes downstream parsing robust. Prompt engineering is the fastest and cheapest way to adapt a model, so it is usually tried before retrieval or fine-tuning. The main pitfall is treating prompts as finished after a few manual tests. Small wording changes can shift behavior, and prompts tuned for one model often transfer poorly to another, so prompts should be versioned and evaluated against a fixed test set like any other code.","u":["Few-shot classification of support tickets","Enforcing JSON output for downstream parsing","Defining persona and tone for assistants"],"x":"A customer-support team builds a ticket triage prompt with a role description, category definitions, three labeled examples and a required JSON schema. Every prompt change is run against a few hundred labeled tickets before release. This regression check caught several edits that improved one category while degrading others."},"cot":{"n":"Chain-of-Thought (CoT)","d":"Chain-of-thought prompting asks a language model to write out intermediate reasoning steps before giving a final answer. It can be triggered with few-shot examples that include worked solutions or simply by an instruction such as \"think step by step\".\n\nBecause a transformer spends a fixed amount of computation per generated token, writing intermediate steps gives the model more computation and lets later tokens condition on earlier partial results, such as a computed subtotal. This substantially improves accuracy on multi-step arithmetic, logic and planning problems, especially for larger models; for small models the gains are much weaker. Two limitations matter in practice. Longer outputs increase latency and cost. More importantly, the written reasoning is not guaranteed to be a faithful account of how the model reached its answer: models can produce plausible steps that do not actually determine the result, so a chain of thought should not be treated as a reliable explanation.","u":["Solving multi-step math word problems","Complex reasoning over business rules","Debugging and planning code changes"],"x":"A tax-preparation software team asks an LLM to reason step by step through deduction eligibility before stating a final answer in a separate tagged field. Only the final field is shown to users, while the reasoning is logged for reviewers. Accuracy on their internal test cases improved compared with direct answers, at the cost of longer responses."},"selfcons":{"n":"Self-Consistency","d":"Self-consistency improves chain-of-thought reasoning by sampling several independent reasoning paths for the same question and choosing the final answer that appears most often. Instead of trusting one greedy chain, the model is run with a nonzero temperature N times, each final answer is extracted, and a majority vote decides.\n\nThe intuition is that a hard problem may have many valid reasoning routes that converge on the same correct answer, while errors tend to scatter across different wrong answers. Marginalizing over reasoning paths therefore gives a more reliable estimate than any single path. It brings large gains on arithmetic and commonsense benchmarks. The obvious cost is N times more inference. It also requires answers that can be compared exactly, such as numbers or multiple-choice labels; for free-form text, voting needs a similarity measure or an LLM judge, and if the model is systematically wrong, more samples only reinforce the same mistake.","u":["Boosting accuracy on math reasoning tasks","Confidence estimation from answer agreement","Robust multiple-choice question answering"],"x":"An education platform grades generated math solutions by sampling ten reasoning paths and voting on the final number. When agreement falls below a threshold, the problem is routed to a human tutor instead of being auto-answered. The extra inference cost is limited because only flagged problems use the full ten samples."},"tot":{"n":"Tree of Thoughts (ToT)","d":"Tree of Thoughts is a prompting framework in which a language model explores many partial reasoning steps as a search tree instead of following a single linear chain. Each node is a \"thought\", a coherent intermediate step such as a candidate equation or a plan fragment.\n\nAt each node, the model proposes several next thoughts, then evaluates them by scoring or voting. A search algorithm such as breadth-first search with a fixed beam width, or depth-first search with backtracking, expands the most promising states and prunes the rest. This allows lookahead and backtracking, which plain chain-of-thought cannot do, and it gives large improvements on puzzles with clear intermediate checks, such as the Game of 24 or crosswords. The cost is many more model calls per problem, and the approach depends on the model being able to judge partial progress reliably. For most everyday tasks, simpler sampling with self-consistency is cheaper and good enough.","u":["Solving combinatorial puzzles like Game of 24","Multi-step planning with backtracking","Exploring alternative designs before committing"],"x":"A logistics research team uses a tree-of-thoughts loop to draft delivery schedules, where the model proposes partial assignments and a rule-based checker scores constraint violations. Combining the model's proposals with a deterministic evaluator made pruning far more reliable than self-evaluation alone. The approach is used offline because each plan needs many model calls."},"rag":{"n":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)","d":"Retrieval-augmented generation answers a query by first retrieving relevant documents from an external knowledge source and then giving them to a language model as context for generating the answer. It lets a model use private or up-to-date information that was not in its training data, without retraining.\n\nA typical pipeline splits documents into chunks, embeds each chunk, and stores the vectors in an index. At query time, the question is embedded, the nearest chunks are retrieved (often combined with keyword search such as BM25), optionally reranked with a cross-encoder, and inserted into the prompt with an instruction to answer only from the provided sources and cite them. RAG reduces hallucination on knowledge-heavy tasks and makes answers traceable. Its quality is limited by retrieval: if the right chunk is not retrieved, the model cannot use it, and poorly chosen chunk sizes or missing metadata cause many failures. Retrieval recall and answer faithfulness should therefore be evaluated separately.","u":["Question answering over internal company documents","Customer support bots grounded in help articles","Search with cited, summarized answers"],"x":"An enterprise IT department builds a RAG assistant over policy documents and runbooks, using hybrid search and a reranker before generation. Answers must cite the source chunk so employees can verify them. The team tracks retrieval recall on a labeled question set separately from answer quality, which showed most errors came from retrieval."},"vectordb":{"n":"Vector Search & ANN","d":"Vector search finds the stored vectors most similar to a query vector, and approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) algorithms make this fast at large scale by giving up exactness. Exact search compares the query with every vector, which is too slow for millions or billions of embeddings at low latency.\n\nThe main ANN families are graph-based, partition-based and compression-based. HNSW builds a layered proximity graph and greedily walks it from coarse to fine layers. IVF clusters vectors with k-means and searches only the few clusters nearest to the query. Product quantization compresses each vector into short codes so that distances can be estimated cheaply and indexes fit in memory. Each index exposes a parameter, such as efSearch or nprobe, that trades recall for speed. A frequent mistake is assuming ANN results are exact: recall@k should be measured against brute-force search, and metadata filters applied after the ANN step can silently return too few results.","u":["Semantic search over document embeddings","Product and content recommendation retrieval","Deduplication of near-identical images or text"],"x":"An e-commerce company serves visual similar-item recommendations from an HNSW index of product image embeddings. The team tuned efSearch so recall against exact search stays high while latency remains within the page-load budget. The index is rebuilt nightly, with new products added incrementally during the day."},"embedmodel":{"n":"Text Embeddings","d":"A text embedding model maps a piece of text to a fixed-length vector so that texts with similar meaning lie close together, usually measured by cosine similarity. Modern embedding models are transformer encoders whose token outputs are pooled, for example by averaging or by taking a special token, into one vector.\n\nThey are trained with contrastive learning: pairs of related texts, such as a question and its answer, are pulled together while other texts in the batch act as negatives and are pushed apart. Embeddings power semantic search, retrieval for RAG, clustering, deduplication and as features for lightweight classifiers. Important practical details are that query and document embeddings must come from the same model, some models require task prefixes such as \"query:\" and \"passage:\", and inputs longer than the model's maximum length are truncated. Similarity scores are only meaningful relative to each other within one model, not as absolute probabilities.","u":["Semantic search and RAG retrieval","Clustering customer feedback by topic","Detecting duplicate questions in forums"],"x":"A software company embeds all incoming support tickets and clusters them weekly to spot emerging issues. A spike in a new cluster after a release alerted the team to a regression before ticket volume became large. They chose a multilingual embedding model because tickets arrive in several languages."},"rerank":{"n":"Reranking","d":"Reranking is a second retrieval stage that re-scores a small set of candidate documents with a more accurate but more expensive model. A fast first stage, such as BM25 or vector search, returns perhaps 50–200 candidates, and the reranker reorders them so that the best few are passed to the user or to an LLM.\n\nThe most common reranker is a cross-encoder, a transformer that reads the query and one document together and outputs a relevance score. Because attention operates across both texts jointly, it captures fine-grained interactions such as negation or exact entity matches that independent bi-encoder embeddings miss. Reranking usually brings a large precision gain in RAG and search pipelines for modest extra latency. Its limitation is that it can only reorder what the first stage retrieved: it cannot recover a relevant document that was never in the candidate set, and its cost grows linearly with the number of candidates.","u":["Improving top results in RAG pipelines","Ranking e-commerce search results by relevance","Prioritizing legal or patent documents"],"x":"A knowledge-base search team retrieves 100 candidates with hybrid search and reranks them with a cross-encoder before sending the top 5 to an LLM. The reranker substantially improved answer quality in their evaluation. To control latency, they cap the candidate count and run the reranker on GPU with batched inputs."},"agent":{"n":"LLM Agents","d":"An LLM agent is a system in which a language model repeatedly decides what action to take next, executes it through tools, observes the result, and continues until a goal is reached. Instead of producing one answer, the model runs in a loop with memory of previous steps and access to functions such as search, code execution, databases or APIs.\n\nTypical components are a system prompt describing the goal and available tools, a loop that feeds tool results back into the context, a stopping condition. Agents suit open-ended tasks whose steps cannot be fixed in advance, such as debugging code or multi-source research. The main limitation is reliability: errors compound over many steps, so a small per-step failure rate becomes a large task failure rate. Production agents therefore need step limits, cost budgets, sandboxed tools, human approval for risky actions, and logging of complete trajectories for evaluation, since prompt injection through tool outputs is a real security risk.","u":["Autonomous coding and bug fixing","Multi-source research and report writing","Automating multi-step back-office workflows"],"x":"A software company deploys a coding agent that reads an issue, searches the repository, edits files and runs tests in a sandbox before opening a pull request. Humans review every pull request before merge. The team limits the number of steps per task and records full trajectories to analyze failures."},"tooluse":{"n":"Tool Use & Function Calling","d":"Function calling lets a language model request the execution of external functions by emitting a structured call, usually JSON with a function name and arguments, instead of plain text. The application declares available tools with names, descriptions and JSON Schema parameters; the model decides whether to call one, the application runs it, and the result is returned to the model as a new message.\n\nModels are fine-tuned to produce well-formed calls and to choose tools based on their descriptions, so clear tool names, precise descriptions and tight schemas strongly affect accuracy. Function calling connects LLMs to databases, calculators, search engines and business APIs, and it is the basic building block of agents. A key misconception is that the model executes the function itself; it only proposes the call, and the application is responsible for validating arguments, enforcing permissions and handling errors, because models can produce wrong or hallucinated arguments.","u":["Querying databases from natural-language questions","Booking and order actions in assistants","Structured data extraction via argument schemas"],"x":"A travel company's assistant exposes tools for searching flights, holding a seat and fetching booking details. The model chooses the tool and fills arguments, while the backend validates dates and requires explicit user confirmation before any payment step. Logs of tool calls are reviewed to improve tool descriptions where the model chose wrongly."},"react":{"n":"ReAct (Reason + Act)","d":"ReAct is a prompting pattern in which a language model interleaves reasoning traces with actions, alternating between \"Thought\", \"Action\" and \"Observation\" steps. The model first writes a thought about what it needs, then issues an action such as a search query or a tool call, receives an observation from the environment, and uses it in the next thought.\n\nCombining the two addresses weaknesses of each alone. Pure chain-of-thought reasoning can hallucinate facts because it never checks the outside world, while pure action sequences lack a plan and struggle to recover from unexpected results. With ReAct, the reasoning steers which actions to take, and the observations ground the reasoning in retrieved facts. It was shown to improve multi-hop question answering and interactive tasks, and its loop structure underlies most modern agent frameworks. Limitations include long contexts as trajectories grow, loops where the model repeats the same action, and sensitivity to noisy observations, so step limits and output parsing safeguards are needed.","u":["Multi-hop question answering with web search","Interactive troubleshooting with diagnostic tools","Grounded fact-checking of generated claims"],"x":"An IT operations team uses a ReAct-style assistant that reasons about an alert, queries logs and metrics through tools, and summarizes the likely root cause. Observations from real systems keep the reasoning grounded, and every action is read-only. Engineers see the full thought-action trace, which makes it easier to judge whether to trust the conclusion."},"ttc":{"n":"Reasoning Models & Test-Time Compute","d":"Reasoning models are language models trained to spend extra computation at inference time, usually by generating a long internal chain of thought before answering, and test-time compute refers to scaling that inference effort to improve accuracy. Instead of only making models bigger, quality is raised by letting them think longer on harder problems.\n\nThese models are typically trained with reinforcement learning on tasks with verifiable rewards, such as math problems with known answers or code with unit tests. Through this training, behaviors like decomposing problems, checking intermediate results and backtracking emerge in the reasoning trace. Test-time compute can also be scaled externally with parallel sampling and majority voting, best-of-N selection with a verifier. Accuracy on math and coding often rises steadily with more reasoning tokens. The trade-offs are higher latency and cost per query, and overthinking: for simple questions, long reasoning adds expense without benefit and can even introduce errors, so many systems let users set a reasoning-effort budget.","u":["Competition-level math and science problems","Complex multi-file code generation","Difficult analytical and planning queries"],"x":"A data-analytics product routes simple lookups to a fast standard model and sends complex multi-step analysis questions to a reasoning model with a high effort setting. This routing keeps average cost and latency low while improving accuracy on hard queries. The team logs reasoning-token counts per request to monitor spending."},"llmeval":{"n":"LLM Evaluation & Benchmarks","d":"LLM evaluation measures how well a language model performs on tasks of interest, using standardized benchmarks, task-specific test sets, human judgments and model-based judges. Public benchmarks cover knowledge (MMLU), math (GSM8K, MATH), code (HumanEval, SWE-bench) and preference-based ranking (Chatbot Arena), giving common reference points for comparing models.\n\nEvaluation methods differ by task type. Multiple-choice benchmarks compare log-probabilities or extracted letters; code is checked by running unit tests, reported as pass@k; open-ended answers are scored by humans or by an LLM-as-a-judge with a rubric, often through pairwise comparison. For production systems, the most valuable evaluation is a custom test set built from real user queries with clear pass criteria, run automatically on every model or prompt change. Key pitfalls are data contamination, where benchmark items appeared in training data and inflate scores, sensitivity to prompt format, and judge biases toward longer answers or a fixed position, which must be controlled by swapping order and calibrating against human labels.","u":["Comparing candidate models before deployment","Regression testing after prompt or model changes","Measuring code correctness with unit tests"],"x":"A bank evaluating LLMs for an internal assistant builds a test set of real anonymized employee questions with reference answers and grading rubrics. Candidate models are scored by an LLM judge whose verdicts were first checked against human graders. Public benchmark scores were used only for the initial shortlist."},"moe":{"n":"Mixture of Experts (MoE)","d":"A Mixture of Experts layer replaces one large feed-forward network with many smaller expert networks and a router that sends each token to only a few of them. Total parameter count grows with the number of experts, but compute per token stays close to that of a much smaller dense model, because only the selected experts run.\n\nIn transformer LLMs, the MoE layer usually replaces the MLP in some or all blocks. For each token, a learned router scores all experts, the top-k (often 1, 2 or 8 of many) are activated, and their outputs are combined with the router weights. Mixtral and DeepSeek-V3 use this design to gain quality at equal FLOPs. The main costs are memory and communication: all experts must be stored, even if few are used per token, and expert parallelism requires all-to-all traffic between devices. Training also needs load-balancing mechanisms, otherwise the router collapses onto a few experts.","u":["Scaling LLM capacity at fixed compute","Efficient serving of very large language models","Multilingual or multi-domain model specialization"],"x":"Mistral AI's Mixtral 8x7B activates two of eight experts per token, so it runs at roughly the cost of a much smaller dense model while having far more parameters. Teams serving such models must still fit all expert weights in GPU memory. They often use expert parallelism across GPUs and monitor per-expert load during serving."},"router":{"n":"Router & Top-k Gating","d":"The router, or gating network, is the small learned component in a Mixture of Experts layer that decides which experts process each token. It is usually a single linear layer that produces one score per expert; the k highest-scoring experts are selected and their outputs are weighted by normalized router scores.\n\nTop-k gating makes computation sparse, since only k experts run per token. Variants differ in when they normalize: some apply softmax over all experts and keep the top-k probabilities, others apply softmax only over the selected k. Noisy top-k gating adds noise during training to encourage exploration, and expert-choice routing reverses the direction so that each expert picks its top tokens. Each expert has a capacity limit per batch; tokens beyond it may be dropped and pass through only the residual connection. A key subtlety is that the top-k selection itself is not differentiable, so experts receive gradient only through the gate weights of tokens they actually processed.","u":["Token routing in sparse MoE transformers","Conditional computation in large vision models","Specializing experts by domain or language"],"x":"A team training an MoE language model logs, for every layer, the fraction of tokens each expert receives and the number of dropped tokens. When a few experts began receiving most tokens, they increased the auxiliary balancing loss weight and the capacity factor. Router logits are also kept in higher precision to avoid instability."},"loadbal":{"n":"Load-Balancing Loss","d":"A load-balancing loss is an auxiliary training objective in Mixture of Experts models that encourages the router to spread tokens evenly across experts. Without it, routing tends to collapse: a few experts get most tokens early in training, become better, and therefore attract even more tokens, while the others stay undertrained.\n\nThe common Switch Transformer form multiplies, for each expert, the fraction of tokens actually dispatched to it by the average router probability assigned to it, sums over experts, and scales by the number of experts. The dispatch fraction is not differentiable, but the probability term is, so gradients push probability away from overloaded experts. The loss reaches its minimum of 1 under perfectly uniform routing and is added with a small coefficient, often around 0.01. The main trade-off is that a strong balancing term forces uniform usage and can hurt quality, which motivated auxiliary-loss-free approaches such as DeepSeek-V3's per-expert bias adjustment that only affects selection.","u":["Preventing expert collapse in MoE training","Improving hardware utilization across expert devices","Reducing dropped tokens under capacity limits"],"x":"During pretraining of an MoE model, an engineering team monitors per-expert token fractions alongside the balancing loss. When imbalance appears in deeper layers, they compare raising the loss coefficient with a bias-based balancing scheme, checking validation perplexity to ensure balance is not bought with quality."},"finegrained":{"n":"Fine-Grained & Shared Experts","d":"Fine-grained and shared experts are two refinements of Mixture of Experts design, popularized by DeepSeekMoE, that improve how specialized experts become. Fine-grained segmentation splits each standard expert into several smaller ones and activates proportionally more of them, keeping compute per token the same while greatly increasing the number of possible expert combinations.\n\nShared experts are a few experts that process every token regardless of routing. They capture common knowledge, such as basic syntax, that every token needs, so routed experts do not each have to learn it redundantly and can specialize more sharply. In DeepSeek-V3, for example, each MoE layer has one shared expert and 256 small routed experts, of which 8 are selected per token. The trade-offs are engineering ones: many tiny experts mean small matrix multiplications that use GPUs less efficiently, more routing overhead and more all-to-all communication, so efficient grouped-GEMM kernels and careful expert placement become important.","u":["Pretraining large sparse language models","Increasing expert specialization at fixed compute","Reducing redundant knowledge across experts"],"x":"DeepSeek's V2 and V3 models combine shared experts with many fine-grained routed experts. Teams adopting this design for their own MoE pretraining rely on grouped matrix-multiplication kernels to keep GPUs efficient with small experts. They also limit how many devices each token's experts can span to control communication cost."},"ssm":{"n":"State Space Models (S4)","d":"A state space model processes a sequence by maintaining a hidden state that evolves through a linear recurrence, and S4 (Structured State Space for Sequences) made this approach practical for deep learning on very long sequences. The model is defined by continuous-time matrices A, B, C that are discretized with a step size Δ, giving h_t = Ā h_{t−1} + B̄ x_t and y_t = C h_t.\n\nBecause the system is linear and time-invariant, the same computation can be done in two ways: as a recurrence, for fast inference, or as a long convolution over the whole sequence, computed efficiently with FFTs for parallel training. S4 initializes A with the HiPPO matrix so the state stores a compressed input history. It excelled on long-range benchmarks. Its key limitation is that parameters do not depend on the input, so the model cannot selectively ignore or focus on tokens, which hurts performance on language; Mamba was designed to fix this.","u":["Modeling very long time series signals","Raw audio and speech waveform modeling","Long-range sequence classification tasks"],"x":"A medical-device research team classifies long ECG recordings sampled at high frequency with an S4-based model. Its convolutional training mode handles sequences of tens of thousands of steps that would be too expensive for full attention. For streaming use, the same model runs in recurrent mode with constant memory per sample."},"mamba":{"n":"Mamba (Selective SSM)","d":"Mamba is a sequence model built on a selective state space model, in which the SSM parameters B, C and the step size Δ are computed from the current input rather than being fixed. This input dependence lets the model decide, token by token, what to write into its state and what to forget.\n\nSelectivity breaks the time-invariance that allowed earlier SSMs such as S4 to be trained as a global convolution. Mamba instead uses a hardware-aware parallel scan that keeps the expanded state in fast on-chip memory, giving training time linear in sequence length. At inference, it runs as a recurrence with a fixed-size state, so memory does not grow with context length and per-token generation cost is constant. Its main limitation is that a fixed-size state cannot store arbitrary amounts of exact information, so tasks that require copying or recalling specific details from far back in the context remain harder than with attention.","u":["Long-sequence modeling with linear-time training","Fast constant-memory autoregressive generation","Genomics and long DNA sequence modeling"],"x":"A genomics research group models long DNA sequences with a Mamba-based architecture because attention cost would grow quadratically with the sequence length. The constant-size recurrent state also keeps inference memory flat. They still evaluate recall-heavy tasks separately, since those are a known weakness of pure SSMs."},"rwkv":{"n":"RWKV","d":"RWKV is a neural network architecture that combines transformer-style parallel training with recurrent-network inference, using a linear attention mechanism with learned exponential time decay instead of softmax attention. Its name comes from the four main elements in each block: Receptance, Weight (decay), Key and Value.\n\nIn the time-mixing block, each channel keeps a running weighted sum of past values, where older tokens are discounted by a learned per-channel decay, and a receptance gate controls how much of this summary is passed on. Because this sum can be updated incrementally, inference runs as an RNN with a constant-size state per layer, so memory and per-token cost do not grow with context length. During training, the same computation can be parallelized over the sequence. Later versions, such as RWKV-6 and RWKV-7, make the decay data-dependent. As with other recurrent designs, the fixed state limits precise recall of details from far back in long contexts.","u":["Efficient LLM inference on edge devices","Long-context generation with constant memory","Multilingual open-source language modeling"],"x":"A mobile app team evaluating on-device text generation chooses an RWKV model because its memory use stays constant regardless of conversation length. This avoids the growing KV cache that limits transformers on phones. They accept somewhat weaker performance on tasks that need exact recall of long earlier passages."},"hybrid":{"n":"Hybrid Architectures (Jamba)","d":"Hybrid architectures interleave attention layers with state space or other recurrent layers in one model, aiming to combine the precise in-context recall of attention with the efficiency of linear-time sequence layers. Jamba, from AI21 Labs, is a well-known example that mixes Mamba layers and transformer attention layers, roughly one attention layer for every seven Mamba layers, and adds Mixture of Experts feed-forward layers.\n\nThe intuition is that most layers can process sequence information cheaply with a fixed-size recurrent state, while a few attention layers handle tasks that need exact lookup of earlier tokens, such as copying or retrieval. Because only a small share of layers use attention, the KV cache is several times smaller than in a pure transformer of similar size, which allows very long contexts on limited GPU memory with good throughput. The main challenges are choosing the layer ratio and placement, and the need for specialized kernels and serving support for both layer types.","u":["Long-document processing with lower memory","High-throughput serving of long-context models","Combining exact recall with linear-time layers"],"x":"An enterprise document-processing team evaluates a Jamba model for analyzing long contracts because its small KV cache lets very long inputs fit on a single GPU. Compared with a transformer of similar quality, it supports more concurrent long requests. They verify that their serving engine has optimized kernels for the Mamba layers."},"mla":{"n":"Multi-Head Latent Attention (MLA)","d":"Multi-Head Latent Attention is an attention variant introduced in DeepSeek-V2 that greatly reduces KV-cache size by storing a single low-dimensional latent vector per token instead of full keys and values for every head. Keys and values for all heads are reconstructed from this shared latent through learned up-projection matrices.\n\nEach token's hidden state is down-projected to a compressed latent c, which is all that needs to be cached. At inference, the up-projection for keys can be absorbed into the query projection and the one for values into the output projection, so attention can operate directly on the cached latents without materializing full keys and values. Rotary position embeddings do not commute with this absorption, so MLA uses a decoupled design: a small extra key component carries RoPE and is cached separately. MLA reaches a smaller cache than grouped-query attention while matching standard attention quality. The cost is a more complex implementation and extra projection computation.","u":["Shrinking KV cache for long-context serving","Higher batch throughput in LLM inference","Efficient attention in large MoE models"],"x":"DeepSeek-V2 and V3 use MLA so that each token's cache entry is a compact latent rather than full per-head keys and values. Serving teams running these models rely on engine support for MLA-specific kernels. The smaller cache lets them hold more concurrent long sequences per GPU."},"mtp":{"n":"Multi-Token Prediction (MTP)","d":"Multi-token prediction trains a language model to predict several future tokens at each position instead of only the next one. Extra output heads or small extra transformer modules, attached to the shared trunk, predict tokens t+2, t+3 and so on, and their losses are added to the standard next-token loss.\n\nThe additional targets give a denser training signal and push the hidden representations to plan further ahead, which has been reported to improve sample efficiency and downstream performance. DeepSeek-V3 uses sequential MTP modules that keep the causal chain by conditioning each extra prediction on the previous depth's representation and the embedding of the next true token. A second benefit appears at inference: the extra heads can act as a built-in draft model for speculative decoding, proposing several tokens that the main head verifies, which speeds up generation. Gains are not universal; small models may see little benefit or even worse results.","u":["Improving sample efficiency in LLM pretraining","Built-in drafts for speculative decoding","Stronger planning signals for code models"],"x":"DeepSeek-V3 was pretrained with one additional MTP module that predicts the token after next. At inference, that module can serve as a draft predictor for speculative decoding, and DeepSeek reported a high acceptance rate for the second token. Teams without that serving path simply drop the module after training."},"mod":{"n":"Mixture of Depths (MoD)","d":"Mixture of Depths is a technique in which each transformer block processes only a selected subset of tokens, while the remaining tokens skip the block through the residual connection. A learned router assigns each token a scalar score, and only the top-k tokens in the sequence pass through the block's attention and MLP.\n\nBecause k is fixed in advance, the computation graph has static tensor shapes and a known FLOP budget, which suits accelerators, while the model still learns to spend more compute on harder or more informative tokens. The block output for selected tokens is multiplied by the router score, which keeps the router on the gradient path. The main practical issue is that top-k over the sequence is non-causal, since it depends on future tokens, so autoregressive sampling needs an auxiliary predictor or classifier that decides from the current token alone whether it will be selected.","u":["Reducing FLOPs per token in LLMs","Adaptive compute allocation across tokens","Faster training at fixed quality"],"x":"A research team training language models under a fixed compute budget replaces every other block with an MoD block routing a small fraction of tokens. They reach similar validation loss with fewer FLOPs per step. For generation, they train a small causal predictor to approximate the top-k routing decision."},"ringattn":{"n":"Ring Attention","d":"Ring Attention computes exact attention over sequences too long for one device by splitting the sequence into blocks across devices arranged in a ring and passing key-value blocks around that ring. Each device keeps its own query block fixed and, in successive steps, receives the next device's key and value block while sending its current one onward.\n\nAt each step, a device computes blockwise attention between its queries and the key-value block it currently holds, combining partial results exactly with the online softmax used in FlashAttention. After as many steps as there are devices, every query block has attended to the whole sequence. Communication of the next block is overlapped with computation on the current one. Memory per device scales with the block size, not the full sequence, allowing contexts of millions of tokens. For causal models, naive splitting causes load imbalance because later blocks attend to more tokens, which striped or zigzag orderings are used to fix.","u":["Training models on million-token contexts","Long video and multimodal sequence modeling","Sequence parallelism across GPU clusters"],"x":"A research lab trains a long-context model on book-length and video-frame sequences by sharding each sequence across many GPUs with ring attention. The approach keeps attention exact while memory per GPU stays bounded. They use a zigzag token ordering so that causal masking does not leave some GPUs idle."},"multimodal":{"n":"Multimodal LLMs","d":"A multimodal LLM is a language model that can take inputs in more than one modality, such as images, audio or video, alongside text, and reason over them jointly. The most common design connects a pretrained vision encoder to a pretrained LLM through a projector that maps image patch features into the LLM's token embedding space.\n\nThe image becomes a sequence of visual tokens placed in the prompt next to text tokens, so the LLM attends over both with its usual machinery. Training typically has two stages: aligning the projector on image-caption pairs, then instruction tuning on visual question answering, document and chart data. Some models instead train natively multimodal from the start, or use cross-attention layers to inject visual features. Common weaknesses include hallucinating objects that are not present, difficulty with fine detail when images are downscaled, and weak counting or spatial reasoning, so high-resolution tiling and careful evaluation matter.","u":["Extracting fields from scanned documents","Answering questions about charts and screenshots","Visual assistance and image description"],"x":"An accounts-payable team uses a multimodal LLM to read invoices of varying layouts and return structured fields such as vendor, total and due date. It replaced a template-based OCR pipeline that broke whenever a supplier changed its layout. Low-confidence extractions are sent to a human reviewer."},"vla":{"n":"Vision-Language-Action Models (VLA)","d":"A vision-language-action model is a robot policy built on a pretrained vision-language model that takes camera images and a natural-language instruction as input and outputs robot actions. Examples include RT-2, OpenVLA and Physical Intelligence's π0.\n\nThe key idea is to reuse the visual and semantic knowledge of a large web-trained model for control. Actions, such as end-effector position changes and gripper state, are either discretized into bins and emitted as special tokens, like words, or produced by a continuous action head such as a diffusion or flow-matching decoder, which handles smooth, high-frequency motion better. Models are fine-tuned on large datasets of robot demonstrations collected across many robots and tasks. This gives better generalization to new objects and instructions than policies trained from scratch. Major limitations are inference latency relative to control rates, which is why action chunks are often predicted at once, the high cost of collecting demonstration data, and the safety risks of deploying learned policies on physical hardware.","u":["Language-instructed robotic pick-and-place of objects","General-purpose household manipulation tasks for robots","Warehouse handling of unseen objects"],"x":"A robotics company fine-tunes an open VLA model on teleoperated demonstrations for sorting items in a warehouse. The pretrained vision-language backbone helps the robot handle products it never saw in training. Predicting short chunks of actions per inference step keeps control smooth despite model latency."},"worldmodel":{"n":"World Models","d":"A world model is a learned model of an environment's dynamics that predicts how the state of the world will change in response to actions, allowing an agent to simulate outcomes before acting. Typically an encoder compresses observations into a latent state, a dynamics model predicts the next latent state given an action, and decoders predict rewards or reconstruct observations.\n\nAgents can then plan or learn policies inside this imagined environment. The Dreamer family trains actor and critic networks entirely on imagined latent rollouts, which is far more sample-efficient than learning from real interaction alone, and MuZero plans with a learned model through tree search. Large video-generation models are also studied as world models. The central limitation is compounding error: small prediction mistakes accumulate over long imagined horizons, and agents can exploit model inaccuracies, learning behaviors that work only in the imperfect simulation.","u":["Sample-efficient reinforcement learning for robots","Simulating driving scenarios for autonomous vehicles","Planning in games and control tasks"],"x":"An autonomous-driving team uses a learned world model to generate plausible future camera frames for rare scenarios, such as a pedestrian stepping out between parked cars. These simulations add training and test coverage for situations that are rare in recorded data. Engineers still validate final behavior in closed-course tests because imagined rollouts can be unrealistic."},"jepa":{"n":"JEPA (Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture)","d":"A Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture is a self-supervised learning approach, proposed at Meta, that predicts the representation of a missing part of the input from the representation of a visible part, rather than reconstructing raw pixels or tokens. I-JEPA applies it to images and V-JEPA to video.\n\nA context encoder processes the visible regions, a target encoder, which is an exponential moving average of the context encoder, embeds the masked target regions, and a predictor maps context embeddings plus target positions to predicted target embeddings. The loss is the distance between predicted and actual target embeddings. Because the model predicts in abstract representation space, it can ignore unpredictable low-level detail, such as exact texture, and focus on semantic structure, which yields strong features for downstream tasks with efficient training. The main risk is representation collapse, where all inputs map to the same embedding and the loss becomes trivially zero; the EMA target encoder and stop-gradient are used to prevent it.","u":["Self-supervised pretraining of vision encoders","Learning video representations without labels","Feature extraction for low-label downstream tasks"],"x":"Meta released I-JEPA and V-JEPA models trained with this objective, which produce useful image and video features without hand-crafted augmentations. A manufacturing team could pretrain a JEPA encoder on unlabeled camera footage from production lines, then train a small classifier for defect types with few labels. This reduces the labeling effort needed per new product line."},"kan":{"n":"Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks (KAN)","d":"A Kolmogorov–Arnold Network is a neural network in which the learnable nonlinear functions sit on the edges between neurons rather than as fixed activations on the nodes. Each edge applies its own learned univariate function, and each node simply sums its incoming edge outputs.\n\nThe design is inspired by the Kolmogorov–Arnold representation theorem, which states that any continuous multivariate function can be written as sums and compositions of univariate functions. An MLP instead learns linear edge weights with fixed node activations. Because each learned edge function is a one-dimensional curve, KANs can be visualized, pruned and sometimes matched to symbolic formulas, which makes them attractive for scientific discovery on small, smooth problems such as fitting physical laws. Their main limitations are practical: spline evaluation is slower and less GPU-friendly than matrix multiplication, and on large-scale tasks like language or vision, KANs have not shown clear advantages over MLPs at equal parameter or compute budgets.","u":["Symbolic regression of physical formulas","Interpretable fitting of smooth scientific data","Small-scale function approximation and PDE solving"],"x":"A physics research group fits a small KAN to simulation data and inspects the learned edge functions, several of which closely match simple forms such as sines and squares. They then replace those edges with symbolic expressions to recover a compact formula. For their larger prediction models they still use standard MLPs because of speed."},"ttt":{"n":"Test-Time Training (TTT)","d":"Test-time training updates some of a model's parameters on each test input, using a self-supervised objective, before or while making a prediction. Since no labels are available at test time, the model minimizes an auxiliary loss, and the adapted weights are then used for the actual task.\n\nThe idea originated as a way to handle distribution shift: if a test image is corrupted differently from training data, a few gradient steps on a rotation-prediction or masked-reconstruction task adapt the shared features to it. Later, in TTT layers, the hidden state of a sequence layer is itself a small model whose weights are updated by gradient steps on each token. In reasoning tasks such as ARC, models are fine-tuned on the examples given in each problem before answering. The costs are extra computation and latency per input, and if the auxiliary task is poorly aligned with the main task, adaptation can make predictions worse.","u":["Adapting vision models to corrupted inputs","Expressive recurrent layers for long sequences","Per-task fine-tuning for abstract reasoning puzzles"],"x":"A medical imaging team deploys a segmentation model across hospitals whose scanners produce images with different contrast and noise. Before predicting, the model takes a few gradient steps on a reconstruction loss for each new scan to adapt its encoder. Validation on held-out sites guards against cases where adaptation degrades results."},"merging":{"n":"Model Merging","d":"Model merging combines the weights of several models fine-tuned from the same base model into a single model, without additional training, so that it inherits the abilities of each. The simplest form averages weights; more effective methods operate on task vectors, the difference between a fine-tuned model's weights and the base weights.\n\nTask arithmetic adds scaled task vectors to the base model. TIES-Merging reduces interference by trimming small changes, resolving sign conflicts between task vectors by majority sign, and averaging only agreeing values. DARE randomly drops a large share of delta parameters and rescales the rest before merging. Merging is cheap, needs no training data, and is popular for combining skills in open models. The key requirement is a shared base model: models trained from different initializations generally lie in different regions of weight space, and averaging them produces a broken model. Even with a shared base, merged skills often trade off, so evaluation on each task is essential.","u":["Combining coding and math fine-tunes","Merging LoRA adapters for multiple domains","Averaging checkpoints for robustness (model soups)"],"x":"An open-source team merges a math fine-tune and a coding fine-tune of the same base model with TIES-Merging, using mergekit. The merged model is evaluated on both benchmark suites plus general chat quality before release. Several merge weightings are tried, since the best values differ across task combinations."},"bitnet":{"n":"1-bit LLMs (BitNet)","d":"BitNet is a family of language model architectures that train transformers with extremely low-precision weights from scratch, rather than quantizing a full-precision model afterward. BitNet b1.58 constrains every weight in its linear layers to one of three values, −1, 0 or +1, which carries about 1.58 bits of information per weight.\n\nDuring training, full-precision latent weights are kept for the optimizer, and in the forward pass they are scaled by their mean absolute value, rounded and clipped to the ternary set; gradients pass through the rounding with a straight-through estimator. Activations are quantized to 8 bits. With ternary weights, matrix multiplication reduces mainly to additions and subtractions, which greatly lowers memory, bandwidth and energy. Reported results show BitNet b1.58 approaching full-precision quality at billions of parameters. The main limitations are that models must be trained this way from the start, and current GPUs lack native ternary arithmetic, so speedups depend on specialized kernels or future hardware.","u":["Running LLMs efficiently on CPUs","On-device inference with tight memory budgets","Energy-efficient large-scale model serving"],"x":"Microsoft released BitNet b1.58 2B4T, an open model trained natively with ternary weights, together with the bitnet.cpp inference framework. An edge-device team could use such a model to run a local assistant on laptop CPUs without a GPU. They would compare its quality on their tasks against a quantized conventional model of similar memory size."},"mechinterp":{"n":"Mechanistic Interpretability","d":"Mechanistic interpretability is the study of reverse-engineering neural networks into human-understandable algorithms, by identifying the features a model represents and the circuits of components that compute them. Instead of only explaining which inputs mattered for a prediction, it asks how the computation is actually carried out inside the weights and activations.\n\nCore tools include activation patching, where activations from one input are substituted into a run on another input to locate which components cause a behavior, the logit lens and linear probes for reading information from intermediate layers, and sparse autoencoders for decomposing activations into interpretable features. Well-known findings include induction heads that implement in-context copying and circuits for tasks such as indirect object identification. The work supports AI safety, debugging and model editing. A major limitation is that neurons are often polysemantic, responding to several unrelated concepts because of superposition, and that analyses of small circuits do not easily scale to full frontier models.","u":["Locating circuits behind specific model behaviors","Auditing models for unsafe or deceptive features","Debugging and editing factual associations"],"x":"Anthropic's interpretability team has published work on circuits in transformer models and on extracting millions of interpretable features from production-scale Claude models with sparse autoencoders. Safety researchers use such techniques to look for features related to deception or harmful content. Activation patching then tests whether those features causally affect behavior."},"sae":{"n":"Sparse Autoencoders (SAE) for Interpretability","d":"In interpretability research, a sparse autoencoder is trained to rewrite a model's internal activations as a sparse combination of many learned feature directions, with the goal that each feature corresponds to a single human-interpretable concept. The dictionary is typically much larger than the activation dimension.\n\nThe encoder maps an activation vector to nonnegative feature activations, and the decoder reconstructs the original activation as a weighted sum of feature directions. Training minimizes reconstruction error plus a sparsity penalty, usually an L1 term on feature activations or a hard TopK constraint, so that only a few features are active per token. This addresses superposition, where models pack more concepts than they have neurons by using overlapping directions. Features can also be clamped to steer behavior. Limitations include imperfect reconstruction, which leaves part of the model's computation unexplained, dead or redundant features, and the fact that the learned dictionary depends on size and training choices.","u":["Finding interpretable features in LLM activations","Steering model behavior by clamping features","Detecting safety-relevant concepts inside models"],"x":"Anthropic and OpenAI have both published sparse autoencoders trained on activations of large production language models, finding millions of interpretable features. Google DeepMind released Gemma Scope, a set of open SAEs for Gemma 2 models. Researchers use these releases to search for features tied to specific behaviors and test them by steering."}},"legend":{}});
